[[:db/retract 17592186168801 :answer-field/choices 17592186168802] [:db/retract 17592186168801 :answer-field/choices 17592186168803] [:db/retract 17592186168801 :answer-field/choices 17592186168804] [:db/retract 17592186168801 :answer-field/choices 17592186168805] [:db/retract 17592186168801 :answer-field/choices 17592186168806] [:db/retract 17592186168891 :answer-field/choices 17592186168896] [:db/retract 17592186168891 :answer-field/choices 17592186168892] [:db/retract 17592186168891 :answer-field/choices 17592186168893] [:db/retract 17592186168891 :answer-field/choices 17592186168894] [:db/retract 17592186168891 :answer-field/choices 17592186168895] {:db/id 17592186171935 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f0e37f45-88ec-572a-8222-4d40197b3b96" "question-53bd4dd2-6540-514d-83fb-ebaccad00681"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172323 :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=80$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.7,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=80$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.75,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=80\\cdot 0.7=56>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=80\\cdot (1-0.7)=24>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.7\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.084\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168801 :answer-field/correct "answer-32ac34ec-29b6-5020-92bf-b3c0a87a87ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff9b0397-2df1-5c1b-bc5c-68b1434b1f4f" "answer-32ac34ec-29b6-5020-92bf-b3c0a87a87ca" "answer-49b3faa2-261e-5b23-a9d3-453a88a3fc61" "answer-e39d1902-3b9f-5884-a0ff-1cb6fa586fa3" "answer-4d90ca59-9e8e-5ca6-9c41-334319a81f44"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff9b0397-2df1-5c1b-bc5c-68b1434b1f4f" :answer/id #uuid "ff9b0397-2df1-5c1b-bc5c-68b1434b1f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.051" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32ac34ec-29b6-5020-92bf-b3c0a87a87ca" :answer/id #uuid "32ac34ec-29b6-5020-92bf-b3c0a87a87ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.084" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49b3faa2-261e-5b23-a9d3-453a88a3fc61" :answer/id #uuid "49b3faa2-261e-5b23-a9d3-453a88a3fc61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.126" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e39d1902-3b9f-5884-a0ff-1cb6fa586fa3" :answer/id #uuid "e39d1902-3b9f-5884-a0ff-1cb6fa586fa3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9\\pm 0.152" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d90ca59-9e8e-5ca6-9c41-334319a81f44" :answer/id #uuid "4d90ca59-9e8e-5ca6-9c41-334319a81f44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9\\pm 0.205" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171936 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-42df1748-d6ee-5969-968b-973a27a68070"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172338 :question/problem "A survey conducted by a sports magazine on a random sample of $125$ students from various U.S. universities reported that $40$ respondents love soccer. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the national proportion of college students who love soccer. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all college students in the US,\n\n- some of the students surveyed love soccer, and some don't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=125,$ and\n\n- the proportion of students from the sample who love soccer can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{40}{125}=0.32\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=125\\cdot 0.32=40>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=125\\cdot (1-0.32)=85>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.32\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.32(1-0.32)}{125}} \\\\  & 0.32\\pm 0.107\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168891 :answer-field/correct "answer-f5dd3fe5-4f40-50ef-9d86-6ca6a32afafc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e296f059-0c25-50c1-9765-f7943410911b" "answer-25aeefc7-0d24-5ed3-a18a-7d64cdb2b831" "answer-f5dd3fe5-4f40-50ef-9d86-6ca6a32afafc" "answer-1aa3363d-ab33-5aa9-af84-d3b6a64ef0a1" "answer-4c12b846-20ca-5213-95bb-ffa4ca0fbb3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e296f059-0c25-50c1-9765-f7943410911b" :answer/id #uuid "e296f059-0c25-50c1-9765-f7943410911b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.16\\pm 0.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25aeefc7-0d24-5ed3-a18a-7d64cdb2b831" :answer/id #uuid "25aeefc7-0d24-5ed3-a18a-7d64cdb2b831" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.32\\pm 0.054" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5dd3fe5-4f40-50ef-9d86-6ca6a32afafc" :answer/id #uuid "f5dd3fe5-4f40-50ef-9d86-6ca6a32afafc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.32\\pm 0.107" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aa3363d-ab33-5aa9-af84-d3b6a64ef0a1" :answer/id #uuid "1aa3363d-ab33-5aa9-af84-d3b6a64ef0a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.40\\pm 0.576" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c12b846-20ca-5213-95bb-ffa4ca0fbb3b" :answer/id #uuid "4c12b846-20ca-5213-95bb-ffa4ca0fbb3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.64\\pm 0.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-42df1748-d6ee-5969-968b-973a27a68070" :question/id #uuid "42df1748-d6ee-5969-968b-973a27a68070" :question/math-academy-id "q-143308" :question/problem "A poll of $800$ randomly selected U.S. Internet subscribers reveals that only $560$ of those interviewed are satisfied with their current internet provider. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the proportion of all U.S. internet users that are satisfied with their current internet providers.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the data:\n\n- the population consists of all U.S. internet users,\n\n- some internet subscribers are satisfied with their current internet provider, and some aren't,\n\n- the size of the random sample is $n=800,$ and\n\n- the proportion of Internet subscribers from the sample who are satisfied with their current internet provider is found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{p\\,}=\\frac{560}{800}=0.7\n$$\n\nNow, we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=800\\cdot 0.7=560>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=800\\cdot (1-0.7)=240>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation: \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\n![](images/f5/f5e154855d4a4f18fb81d522873fb29c11fd7c9543ffebdeac0f15672c7e72b2.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$  \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0,7\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{800}} \\\\  & 0.7\\pm 0.042\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-16ab0739-3e4c-54dd-b8d9-d593dca45aa7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-16ab0739-3e4c-54dd-b8d9-d593dca45aa7" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ab0739-3e4c-54dd-b8d9-d593dca45aa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-06a23d11-10d2-51ff-b549-c564d4d0adc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-91e6c7ed-6456-52e2-b6b3-ca05ed0e1fd8" "answer-fbec8e4e-7d76-5bea-a85e-7474bdb2c704" "answer-cff2a8a8-d184-58b6-9e13-e0ce109be435" "answer-06a23d11-10d2-51ff-b549-c564d4d0adc7" "answer-0059189e-a1d5-5009-984c-ab56aaf9c32f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91e6c7ed-6456-52e2-b6b3-ca05ed0e1fd8" :answer/id #uuid "91e6c7ed-6456-52e2-b6b3-ca05ed0e1fd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.021" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbec8e4e-7d76-5bea-a85e-7474bdb2c704" :answer/id #uuid "fbec8e4e-7d76-5bea-a85e-7474bdb2c704" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.35\\pm 0.042" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cff2a8a8-d184-58b6-9e13-e0ce109be435" :answer/id #uuid "cff2a8a8-d184-58b6-9e13-e0ce109be435" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.021" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06a23d11-10d2-51ff-b549-c564d4d0adc7" :answer/id #uuid "06a23d11-10d2-51ff-b549-c564d4d0adc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.042" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0059189e-a1d5-5009-984c-ab56aaf9c32f" :answer/id #uuid "0059189e-a1d5-5009-984c-ab56aaf9c32f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7\\pm 0.084" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f0e37f45-88ec-572a-8222-4d40197b3b96" :question/id #uuid "f0e37f45-88ec-572a-8222-4d40197b3b96" :question/math-academy-id "q-143320" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f0308aa9c39034a938a5f5c63ca049ab21a47fddc20b592d660a70254990ff0.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=75$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that the proportion of those in the sample with the characteristic is $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=75$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=0.4,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=75\\cdot 0.4=30>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=75\\cdot (1-0.4)=45>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.4\\pm 2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.4(1-0.4)}{75}} \\\\  & 0.4\\pm 0.146\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd7b3069-b6b7-5440-a62a-32c4be352c17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd7b3069-b6b7-5440-a62a-32c4be352c17" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7b3069-b6b7-5440-a62a-32c4be352c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ecfecdf1-e7ff-52c1-bbfa-bfad67ae31fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b094ffa0-7616-5773-817f-21c3ca2b8253" "answer-f880901b-d44c-5e44-aab2-24fe5ad46d2f" "answer-ecfecdf1-e7ff-52c1-bbfa-bfad67ae31fb" "answer-4dd6bc15-cdc3-53a9-b331-5c5176f9dd4e" "answer-ded03ed4-9849-5189-9368-6cbd92efb18b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b094ffa0-7616-5773-817f-21c3ca2b8253" :answer/id #uuid "b094ffa0-7616-5773-817f-21c3ca2b8253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.111" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f880901b-d44c-5e44-aab2-24fe5ad46d2f" :answer/id #uuid "f880901b-d44c-5e44-aab2-24fe5ad46d2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.132" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecfecdf1-e7ff-52c1-bbfa-bfad67ae31fb" :answer/id #uuid "ecfecdf1-e7ff-52c1-bbfa-bfad67ae31fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4\\pm 0.146" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dd6bc15-cdc3-53a9-b331-5c5176f9dd4e" :answer/id #uuid "4dd6bc15-cdc3-53a9-b331-5c5176f9dd4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.132" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ded03ed4-9849-5189-9368-6cbd92efb18b" :answer/id #uuid "ded03ed4-9849-5189-9368-6cbd92efb18b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6\\pm 0.146" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-53bd4dd2-6540-514d-83fb-ebaccad00681" :question/id #uuid "53bd4dd2-6540-514d-83fb-ebaccad00681" :question/math-academy-id "q-143381" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n=160$ from a large population where some individuals have a particular characteristic. Given that $5%$ of the sample has the characteristic, find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion of individuals having this characteristic.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Since $n=160$ and $\\overset{^}{\\,p}=5%=0.05,$ we have that\n\n- $n\\overset{^}{\\,p}=160\\cdot 0.05=8>5,$ and\n\n- $n(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})=160\\cdot (1-0.05)=152>5.$\n\nAs a result, we may use the following normal approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}-p}{\\sqrt{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})/n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion,\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ of the original distribution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\n![](images/b5/b5b4d260ad31b6c4efc3f7314e98df8b422f8d92f0f21fa436334c4b15d88a67.png)\n\nTherefore, a $90%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 0.05\\pm 1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.05(1-0.05)}{160}} \\\\  & 0.05\\pm 0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-acae107c-a0bc-5d9a-b751-b05730ad5a81"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-acae107c-a0bc-5d9a-b751-b05730ad5a81" :answer-field/id #uuid "acae107c-a0bc-5d9a-b751-b05730ad5a81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-51816d49-6771-5f06-881f-567d136e9342" :answer-field/choices ["answer-51816d49-6771-5f06-881f-567d136e9342" "answer-b891f11a-a92a-540d-8453-b59eaffc3349" "answer-fb5679a7-92b1-5e2b-b3f0-4824eb166850" "answer-245260ce-f9eb-5103-abac-929724075c75" "answer-341d3433-7189-5f66-87a1-9332a008905d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51816d49-6771-5f06-881f-567d136e9342" :answer/id #uuid "51816d49-6771-5f06-881f-567d136e9342" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.028" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b891f11a-a92a-540d-8453-b59eaffc3349" :answer/id #uuid "b891f11a-a92a-540d-8453-b59eaffc3349" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.034" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb5679a7-92b1-5e2b-b3f0-4824eb166850" :answer/id #uuid "fb5679a7-92b1-5e2b-b3f0-4824eb166850" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05\\pm 0.044" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-245260ce-f9eb-5103-abac-929724075c75" :answer/id #uuid "245260ce-f9eb-5103-abac-929724075c75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.028" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-341d3433-7189-5f66-87a1-9332a008905d" :answer/id #uuid "341d3433-7189-5f66-87a1-9332a008905d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.95\\pm 0.034" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-23e61e75-beb0-5233-84f0-0d07934eb397" :activity/id #uuid "23e61e75-beb0-5233-84f0-0d07934eb397" :activity/title "Confidence Intervals for One Proportion" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050936 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-545b80a9-b6e1-5d1f-869a-37ad814aa7f7" "step-f5a2e185-d130-54a1-8f2f-7d0113c88c8b" "step-76cd70fa-6d63-54f7-87b7-85e928a759ea" "step-683d3fd0-7aa6-5a47-b0f5-2215480efbbd" "step-55ab25b8-178a-5f6f-9fb3-b2f790774916"] :activity/first-step "step-545b80a9-b6e1-5d1f-869a-37ad814aa7f7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-545b80a9-b6e1-5d1f-869a-37ad814aa7f7" :step/id #uuid "545b80a9-b6e1-5d1f-869a-37ad814aa7f7" :step/content 17592186172323 :step/next "step-f5a2e185-d130-54a1-8f2f-7d0113c88c8b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f5a2e185-d130-54a1-8f2f-7d0113c88c8b" :step/id #uuid "f5a2e185-d130-54a1-8f2f-7d0113c88c8b" :step/content 17592186172338 :step/next "step-76cd70fa-6d63-54f7-87b7-85e928a759ea" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-76cd70fa-6d63-54f7-87b7-85e928a759ea" :step/id #uuid "76cd70fa-6d63-54f7-87b7-85e928a759ea" :step/content "question-42df1748-d6ee-5969-968b-973a27a68070" :step/next "step-683d3fd0-7aa6-5a47-b0f5-2215480efbbd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-683d3fd0-7aa6-5a47-b0f5-2215480efbbd" :step/id #uuid "683d3fd0-7aa6-5a47-b0f5-2215480efbbd" :step/content "question-f0e37f45-88ec-572a-8222-4d40197b3b96" :step/next "step-55ab25b8-178a-5f6f-9fb3-b2f790774916" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-55ab25b8-178a-5f6f-9fb3-b2f790774916" :step/id #uuid "55ab25b8-178a-5f6f-9fb3-b2f790774916" :step/content "question-53bd4dd2-6540-514d-83fb-ebaccad00681" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147544 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-76598254-2f11-555f-8a0a-b51f61e7748b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-76598254-2f11-555f-8a0a-b51f61e7748b" :question/id #uuid "76598254-2f11-555f-8a0a-b51f61e7748b" :question/math-academy-id "q-350260" :question/problem "Olivia and Harper are musicians. In the previous season, the decimal proportion of Olivia’s performances that were solos was $0.55,$ while $40%$ of Harper’s performances were solos. \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\nII. More than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\nIII. Olivia had a greater percentage of solo performances compared to Harper." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n0.55>0.5,\n$$\n\nmore than half of Olivia's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that \n\n$$\n40%<50%.\n$$\n\nSo, less than half of Harper's performances were solos.\n\n- Statement III is true. We can compare $0.55$ and $40%$ by converting the decimal into a percent. \n\nTo do this, we multiply it by $100:$\n\n$$\n0.55\\times 100=55%\n$$\n\nSince $55%>40%,$ Olivia had a **greater** percentage of solo performances compared to Harper.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6aac269-3443-59ce-abcc-30a3070cf5de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6aac269-3443-59ce-abcc-30a3070cf5de" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6aac269-3443-59ce-abcc-30a3070cf5de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1e7cf7ba-68f6-5297-8ecb-5cbcb4319093" :answer-field/choices ["answer-935a874d-2c69-5a73-a09e-1d948d77d34e" "answer-55b429c7-69d9-5bf9-8c81-7e51c17241c7" "answer-960bdafc-7f50-588d-9837-9eadd76e45a4" "answer-1e7cf7ba-68f6-5297-8ecb-5cbcb4319093" "answer-4ffcb838-2f8f-514c-916d-7ecc0e14a9fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-935a874d-2c69-5a73-a09e-1d948d77d34e" :answer/id #uuid "935a874d-2c69-5a73-a09e-1d948d77d34e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55b429c7-69d9-5bf9-8c81-7e51c17241c7" :answer/id #uuid "55b429c7-69d9-5bf9-8c81-7e51c17241c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-960bdafc-7f50-588d-9837-9eadd76e45a4" :answer/id #uuid "960bdafc-7f50-588d-9837-9eadd76e45a4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e7cf7ba-68f6-5297-8ecb-5cbcb4319093" :answer/id #uuid "1e7cf7ba-68f6-5297-8ecb-5cbcb4319093" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ffcb838-2f8f-514c-916d-7ecc0e14a9fd" :answer/id #uuid "4ffcb838-2f8f-514c-916d-7ecc0e14a9fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152124 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8ba50b21-9ef4-5151-a99b-e87a06229c81"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8ba50b21-9ef4-5151-a99b-e87a06229c81" :question/id #uuid "8ba50b21-9ef4-5151-a99b-e87a06229c81" :question/math-academy-id "q-128553" :question/problem "Which of the following are natural numbers?\n\nI. $-1$\n\nII. $0$\n\nIII. $11$" :question/worked-solution "The natural numbers consist of all positive whole numbers.\n\n$$\n1,\\,2,\\,3,\\,4,\\,5,…\n$$\n\nOf the given numbers, $11$ is a natural number, but $-1$ and $0$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f80727cc-5fda-5d7f-a94d-a0834b4aa2eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f80727cc-5fda-5d7f-a94d-a0834b4aa2eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "f80727cc-5fda-5d7f-a94d-a0834b4aa2eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-61025d0a-c07b-5a36-acca-f1913c26af7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7005b21d-6fc3-5fa8-a675-951cc13b9a8d" "answer-39b063a5-a3ee-5ac4-af1b-7e3f2eb8d73a" "answer-4b6b86f9-95e1-5078-964a-812f65e7c86f" "answer-55a0a46c-e48c-5d8f-b839-aaeda5150bb6" "answer-61025d0a-c07b-5a36-acca-f1913c26af7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7005b21d-6fc3-5fa8-a675-951cc13b9a8d" :answer/id #uuid "7005b21d-6fc3-5fa8-a675-951cc13b9a8d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39b063a5-a3ee-5ac4-af1b-7e3f2eb8d73a" :answer/id #uuid "39b063a5-a3ee-5ac4-af1b-7e3f2eb8d73a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b6b86f9-95e1-5078-964a-812f65e7c86f" :answer/id #uuid "4b6b86f9-95e1-5078-964a-812f65e7c86f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55a0a46c-e48c-5d8f-b839-aaeda5150bb6" :answer/id #uuid "55a0a46c-e48c-5d8f-b839-aaeda5150bb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61025d0a-c07b-5a36-acca-f1913c26af7d" :answer/id #uuid "61025d0a-c07b-5a36-acca-f1913c26af7d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064230 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f162fba1-f09f-5af9-a84b-e3875292cc2e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f162fba1-f09f-5af9-a84b-e3875292cc2e" :question/id #uuid "f162fba1-f09f-5af9-a84b-e3875292cc2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-128441" :question/problem "What is $0÷(-\\frac{9}{7}).$" :question/worked-solution "Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, \n\n$$\n0÷(-\\frac{9}{7})=0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7693271f-4341-5f7a-9e8f-486b996d263b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7693271f-4341-5f7a-9e8f-486b996d263b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7693271f-4341-5f7a-9e8f-486b996d263b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1bbec767-e533-5c10-9e12-149088192f95" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dcdebc50-1a9d-5401-81bd-abe973d6d489" "answer-1bbec767-e533-5c10-9e12-149088192f95" "answer-76154324-9a81-5b0d-a958-23841c6c7f0e" "answer-00eef7cd-53a9-593e-a267-c9da06c25bea" "answer-f24d6aa3-5802-57f1-bbdd-fb4785ed51d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcdebc50-1a9d-5401-81bd-abe973d6d489" :answer/id #uuid "dcdebc50-1a9d-5401-81bd-abe973d6d489" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bbec767-e533-5c10-9e12-149088192f95" :answer/id #uuid "1bbec767-e533-5c10-9e12-149088192f95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76154324-9a81-5b0d-a958-23841c6c7f0e" :answer/id #uuid "76154324-9a81-5b0d-a958-23841c6c7f0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00eef7cd-53a9-593e-a267-c9da06c25bea" :answer/id #uuid "00eef7cd-53a9-593e-a267-c9da06c25bea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f24d6aa3-5802-57f1-bbdd-fb4785ed51d4" :answer/id #uuid "f24d6aa3-5802-57f1-bbdd-fb4785ed51d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Undefined" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064256 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d2a64c1-9bb3-5573-99a2-5c763e9021bd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d2a64c1-9bb3-5573-99a2-5c763e9021bd" :question/id #uuid "9d2a64c1-9bb3-5573-99a2-5c763e9021bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-236833" :question/problem "$23-48=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $48$ is larger than $23,$ the answer will be negative.\n\nWe start by solving an easier problem:\n\n$$\n48-23\n$$\n\nNext, we carry out the subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n48-23=25.\n$$\n\nHowever, since our answer must be negative, we need to change the sign of the answer above. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n23-48=-25.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5d9b0439-1e80-5d6a-bc2b-6811b4343b6f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5d9b0439-1e80-5d6a-bc2b-6811b4343b6f" :answer-field/id #uuid "5d9b0439-1e80-5d6a-bc2b-6811b4343b6f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8de20a7f-62f7-51e8-90de-227ff7c98d0f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8de20a7f-62f7-51e8-90de-227ff7c98d0f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8de20a7f-62f7-51e8-90de-227ff7c98d0f" :answer/id #uuid "8de20a7f-62f7-51e8-90de-227ff7c98d0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073739 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d65e17ea-2def-584a-904b-9397e33a3b17"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d65e17ea-2def-584a-904b-9397e33a3b17" :question/id #uuid "d65e17ea-2def-584a-904b-9397e33a3b17" :question/math-academy-id "q-59888" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd9943f680dedd516084909e74d769e7605c7180f04d55b3c12113834bb47136.png)\n\nWhat ratio describes the number of rectangles to the total number of shapes?" :question/worked-solution "We count the number of rectangles. We see that there are $3$ rectangles.\n\nWe count the total number of shapes. We see that there are $6$ shapes in total.\n\nSo the ratio of rectangles to total shapes is $3:6.$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers in a ratio by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,3 &  & : & \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,6 &  \\\\ \\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,↓÷\\,\\,3 &  \\\\ \\,\\,1 &  & : &  & \\,\\,\\,2 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of rectangles to the total shapes is $1:2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-842c48ea-56dd-5cc6-aceb-60fc8c45526c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-842c48ea-56dd-5cc6-aceb-60fc8c45526c" :answer-field/id #uuid "842c48ea-56dd-5cc6-aceb-60fc8c45526c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e4f770e5-ee30-51e6-a12e-840e5446d7c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4f770e5-ee30-51e6-a12e-840e5446d7c2" "answer-64af21c4-1578-5f21-ba90-bcbd5688e962" "answer-6109d19e-7b42-5696-b0d8-eb8bbfdb9805" "answer-d399264a-ea27-5301-8009-d5f8653eb057" "answer-1ddb27d9-bf9f-58a3-8b13-e2c911a46068"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4f770e5-ee30-51e6-a12e-840e5446d7c2" :answer/id #uuid "e4f770e5-ee30-51e6-a12e-840e5446d7c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1:2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64af21c4-1578-5f21-ba90-bcbd5688e962" :answer/id #uuid "64af21c4-1578-5f21-ba90-bcbd5688e962" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1:4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6109d19e-7b42-5696-b0d8-eb8bbfdb9805" :answer/id #uuid "6109d19e-7b42-5696-b0d8-eb8bbfdb9805" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2:3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d399264a-ea27-5301-8009-d5f8653eb057" :answer/id #uuid "d399264a-ea27-5301-8009-d5f8653eb057" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3:5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ddb27d9-bf9f-58a3-8b13-e2c911a46068" :answer/id #uuid "1ddb27d9-bf9f-58a3-8b13-e2c911a46068" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5:6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054801 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-71607034-c109-5c32-ac61-2615649524b9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-71607034-c109-5c32-ac61-2615649524b9" :question/id #uuid "71607034-c109-5c32-ac61-2615649524b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-184137" :question/problem "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"Nine fewer than a number.\"\n\nUse the letter $p$ to represent the unknown number.\n\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we denote our number by $p,$ then the phrase \"nine fewer than a number\" can be written as \n\n$$\np-9.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd8abb59-1066-5acc-8b33-5e97fe06f21e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd8abb59-1066-5acc-8b33-5e97fe06f21e" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd8abb59-1066-5acc-8b33-5e97fe06f21e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ce077c8b-a643-584e-9991-1ba8f0a61be7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce077c8b-a643-584e-9991-1ba8f0a61be7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce077c8b-a643-584e-9991-1ba8f0a61be7" :answer/id #uuid "ce077c8b-a643-584e-9991-1ba8f0a61be7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "p-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054809 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-84a619bd-064d-5238-b773-07631be56d2c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-84a619bd-064d-5238-b773-07631be56d2c" :question/id #uuid "84a619bd-064d-5238-b773-07631be56d2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-101924" :question/problem "![](images/72/72cfa1c8918f9f50b2426eb05ac16eed5605f557b59870aa914d07148d782676.png)\n\nA point $Q$ is shown on the axis above. To which quadrant does it belong?" :question/worked-solution "We label the quadrants as follows:\n\n![](images/0a/0a6e04eba0886d64c1a36b259c7a3c17afb9a86ad5169e28f476fff2d31c68b1.png)\n\nSince $Q$ is placed below and to the left of the origin, it lies in quadrant $\\text{III},$ which is the third quadrant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-75c412c0-32da-548d-bf84-4a15a64dd939"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-75c412c0-32da-548d-bf84-4a15a64dd939" :answer-field/id #uuid "75c412c0-32da-548d-bf84-4a15a64dd939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65b1977d-52f0-5c1d-90ff-8d07b0a7ec07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d3d529c8-ca6c-5c5f-b5f1-1f054c538fc4" "answer-2c6172b4-b1a0-52e6-b911-311e77e86cfd" "answer-06edf1c7-c8bc-581d-90d0-594bd44549ca" "answer-73c23cad-d442-5454-b829-add3e2e417c0" "answer-65b1977d-52f0-5c1d-90ff-8d07b0a7ec07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3d529c8-ca6c-5c5f-b5f1-1f054c538fc4" :answer/id #uuid "d3d529c8-ca6c-5c5f-b5f1-1f054c538fc4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "First quadrant" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c6172b4-b1a0-52e6-b911-311e77e86cfd" :answer/id #uuid "2c6172b4-b1a0-52e6-b911-311e77e86cfd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Fourth quadrant" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-06edf1c7-c8bc-581d-90d0-594bd44549ca" :answer/id #uuid "06edf1c7-c8bc-581d-90d0-594bd44549ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Left quadrant" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73c23cad-d442-5454-b829-add3e2e417c0" :answer/id #uuid "73c23cad-d442-5454-b829-add3e2e417c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Second quadrant" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65b1977d-52f0-5c1d-90ff-8d07b0a7ec07" :answer/id #uuid "65b1977d-52f0-5c1d-90ff-8d07b0a7ec07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Third quadrant" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074427 :question/problem "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nMark and James shared slices of pizza in the ratio of $2:6$, so there are $\\boxed{}$ slices of Mark's share for each slice of James' share." :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the ratio as a fraction, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{2÷2}{6÷2}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThat means that there are $\\frac{1}{3}$ slices of Mark's share for each slice of James' share." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186072800 :answer-field/correct 17592186072804 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171932 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a8e40c21-fa80-544c-8d3a-4f03c8add411"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8e40c21-fa80-544c-8d3a-4f03c8add411" :question/id #uuid "a8e40c21-fa80-544c-8d3a-4f03c8add411" :question/math-academy-id "q-85400" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $9?$\n\nI. $7\\,050\\,113$\n\nII. $5\\,119\\,232$\n\nIII. $14\\,624\\,100$" :question/worked-solution "A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $7\\,050\\,113$ is\n\n$$\n7+0+5+0+1+1+3=17,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $9.$ So, $7\\,050\\,113$ is *not* divisible by $9.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $5\\,119\\,232$ is\n\n$$\n5+1+1+9+2+3+2=23,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $9.$ So, $5\\,119\\,232$ is *not* divisible by $9.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $14\\,624\\,100$ is\n\n$$\n1+4+6+2+4+1+0+0=18,\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $9.$ So, $14\\,624\\,100$ is divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"llI only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f142419-33c2-52ed-b329-1e3e4574c8cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f142419-33c2-52ed-b329-1e3e4574c8cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f142419-33c2-52ed-b329-1e3e4574c8cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1af6ee9f-ae42-51c5-b7de-ef2b565520ac" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d793313-b695-5fb8-9045-3b51e59571cf" "answer-e266b9f7-eb22-5300-a9a9-5cb7a739a5ee" "answer-931aa220-ad5f-5920-8624-85dee5435de8" "answer-28347767-b666-52fa-a9d8-5e24528f5b0a" "answer-1af6ee9f-ae42-51c5-b7de-ef2b565520ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d793313-b695-5fb8-9045-3b51e59571cf" :answer/id #uuid "8d793313-b695-5fb8-9045-3b51e59571cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e266b9f7-eb22-5300-a9a9-5cb7a739a5ee" :answer/id #uuid "e266b9f7-eb22-5300-a9a9-5cb7a739a5ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-931aa220-ad5f-5920-8624-85dee5435de8" :answer/id #uuid "931aa220-ad5f-5920-8624-85dee5435de8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28347767-b666-52fa-a9d8-5e24528f5b0a" :answer/id #uuid "28347767-b666-52fa-a9d8-5e24528f5b0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1af6ee9f-ae42-51c5-b7de-ef2b565520ac" :answer/id #uuid "1af6ee9f-ae42-51c5-b7de-ef2b565520ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064767 :question/problem "$10\\cdot (-0.7)=$" :question/worked-solution "We have a product of a positive number $(10)$ and a negative number $(-0.7).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n10\\cdot (-0.7)=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $10$ by $0.7$ without any signs: $10\\cdot 0.7=7.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n10\\cdot (-0.7)=-7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186062659 :answer-field/correct 17592186062664 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-37d0b1d2-0d33-5132-981f-7237e41ecb79" :activity/id #uuid "37d0b1d2-0d33-5132-981f-7237e41ecb79" :activity/title "Quiz 4" :activity/type :activity.type/assessment :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-3962c57b-8223-5237-84cb-966ed91f92a5" "step-315ebbfd-2ea0-5ceb-a85f-0680158355d3" "step-8e8b3083-de9c-5c24-abbc-5972258cafe1" "step-bf028505-d785-547c-b2c1-3e538df6ea05" "step-c3f47e54-d0df-5184-8c1d-5c275975c07c" "step-f12ef982-156d-583a-a725-578271e47f6a" "step-7310a483-c40f-5b26-a7a9-915a4dc0418c" "step-b52ce16c-4d5c-526a-b072-8ba9e02db153" "step-d6658c9b-d996-55e1-8b94-2b786d9fa9ac" "step-07db862f-07ff-550f-b7db-a94183dc2e6c"] :activity/first-step "step-3962c57b-8223-5237-84cb-966ed91f92a5" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3962c57b-8223-5237-84cb-966ed91f92a5" :step/id #uuid "3962c57b-8223-5237-84cb-966ed91f92a5" :step/content "question-76598254-2f11-555f-8a0a-b51f61e7748b" :step/next "step-315ebbfd-2ea0-5ceb-a85f-0680158355d3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-315ebbfd-2ea0-5ceb-a85f-0680158355d3" :step/id #uuid "315ebbfd-2ea0-5ceb-a85f-0680158355d3" :step/content "question-8ba50b21-9ef4-5151-a99b-e87a06229c81" :step/next "step-8e8b3083-de9c-5c24-abbc-5972258cafe1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8e8b3083-de9c-5c24-abbc-5972258cafe1" :step/id #uuid "8e8b3083-de9c-5c24-abbc-5972258cafe1" :step/content "question-f162fba1-f09f-5af9-a84b-e3875292cc2e" :step/next "step-bf028505-d785-547c-b2c1-3e538df6ea05" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bf028505-d785-547c-b2c1-3e538df6ea05" :step/id #uuid "bf028505-d785-547c-b2c1-3e538df6ea05" :step/content "question-9d2a64c1-9bb3-5573-99a2-5c763e9021bd" :step/next "step-c3f47e54-d0df-5184-8c1d-5c275975c07c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c3f47e54-d0df-5184-8c1d-5c275975c07c" :step/id #uuid "c3f47e54-d0df-5184-8c1d-5c275975c07c" :step/content "question-d65e17ea-2def-584a-904b-9397e33a3b17" :step/next "step-f12ef982-156d-583a-a725-578271e47f6a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f12ef982-156d-583a-a725-578271e47f6a" :step/id #uuid "f12ef982-156d-583a-a725-578271e47f6a" :step/content "question-71607034-c109-5c32-ac61-2615649524b9" :step/next "step-7310a483-c40f-5b26-a7a9-915a4dc0418c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7310a483-c40f-5b26-a7a9-915a4dc0418c" :step/id #uuid "7310a483-c40f-5b26-a7a9-915a4dc0418c" :step/content "question-84a619bd-064d-5238-b773-07631be56d2c" :step/next "step-b52ce16c-4d5c-526a-b072-8ba9e02db153" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b52ce16c-4d5c-526a-b072-8ba9e02db153" :step/id #uuid "b52ce16c-4d5c-526a-b072-8ba9e02db153" :step/content 17592186074427 :step/next "step-d6658c9b-d996-55e1-8b94-2b786d9fa9ac" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d6658c9b-d996-55e1-8b94-2b786d9fa9ac" :step/id #uuid "d6658c9b-d996-55e1-8b94-2b786d9fa9ac" :step/content "question-a8e40c21-fa80-544c-8d3a-4f03c8add411" :step/next "step-07db862f-07ff-550f-b7db-a94183dc2e6c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-07db862f-07ff-550f-b7db-a94183dc2e6c" :step/id #uuid "07db862f-07ff-550f-b7db-a94183dc2e6c" :step/content 17592186064767 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186061800 :answer-field/choices 17592186061801] [:db/retract 17592186061800 :answer-field/choices 17592186061802] [:db/retract 17592186061800 :answer-field/choices 17592186061803] [:db/retract 17592186061800 :answer-field/choices 17592186061804] [:db/retract 17592186061800 :answer-field/choices 17592186061805] [:db/retract 17592186063839 :answer-field/choices 17592186063840] [:db/retract 17592186063839 :answer-field/choices 17592186063841] [:db/retract 17592186063839 :answer-field/choices 17592186063842] [:db/retract 17592186063839 :answer-field/choices 17592186063843] [:db/retract 17592186063839 :answer-field/choices 17592186063844] {:db/id 17592186064291 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a8aac6cd-427e-5b7e-aea2-44c84e419786" "question-9c01ea5e-36e4-5dc4-b2f4-1bae38b20756" "question-748701f6-8780-55dc-bf9a-808d48cf28f4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064134 :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $4 \\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}.$ The radius of the cone is always one-third of its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $3 \\,\\textrm{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d h}{\\textrm d t} = 4\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t}$ when $h = 3\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is one-third of its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{3}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}h\\right)^2 \\cdot h = \\dfrac{1}{27}\\pi h^3.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (3)^{2}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064387 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cc3b1d02-1e54-57cd-be11-d438beee9721" "question-babb3260-0ebb-5378-81f6-fb356d66422e" "question-7cce7dd9-a52f-5429-96fd-d564ac81f3b8" "question-d9e85e6a-5a02-52f7-ba3c-489084bc4a6f" "question-9a637ece-bf3b-5e1e-8874-577affcbf215"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064031 :question/problem "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{32}\\pi \\, \\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr}.$ The height of the cone is always a quarter of its radius. At what rate is the radius of the base decreasing when the radius is $3 \\,\\textrm{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{1}{32} \\,\\textrm{m}^3\\!/\\textrm{hr},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}$ when $r = 3 \\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is a quarter of its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{1}{4}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}r\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{12}\\pi r^3.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{32}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{72}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{hr}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the base of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{1}{72} \\, \\textrm{m}/\\textrm{hr}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064290 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e05d5196-22d0-5390-9d3c-413318bc7364" "question-829ceea0-b4f0-5bef-b797-d4b161d8e247" "question-7d9fecf0-f483-51c1-b6ab-f1ee3ed71254" "question-734a1589-9b26-5ca8-b559-8254d0411564" "question-f35215fb-23bc-5523-aa38-ea3ed45464a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064133 :question/problem "A conical tank contains water. The height of the tank is $3\\,\\text{m}$ and the radius at the top is $4\\,\\text{m}.$ If water is leaking from the bottom of the tank at a rate of $6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min}$, how fast is the water level $h$ decreasing when $h=2\\,\\text{m}?$\n\n![](images/6d/6de28f823109694d40bfb02afef571c2b0d497b92efbbc738ccfa15dc7341234.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. We call $H=3\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the tank, and $R=4\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^3/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4h}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{4h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{16}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{16}{9}\\pi ({2}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -6\\pi  & =\\frac{64}{9}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{27}{32}\\,\\text{m/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of  $\\dfrac{27}{32}\\, \\text{m/min}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064388 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4ae8f6c-f256-5ee8-80b1-72f1337deae2" "question-9ddf5a2d-9bc3-5a54-8df4-57b5fda24b44" "question-d2f18815-e582-5459-b43c-b02a5b4a0da8" "question-a9bed4a9-ed2b-5603-85e6-df6b8285a648" "question-0c589335-1cfb-54d8-9a5c-d454270a6ef5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064032 :question/problem "A conical flask has a height of $20\\,\\text{cm}.$ The radius at the bottom of the flask is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$ The flask has a very narrow opening at the top, and a chemist is pouring a chemical into the flask. If the level (i.e., height) of the chemical in the flask is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}$, find the rate at which volume of the chemical is increasing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\n![](images/eb/eb454cec7cd4c38997ee5c8e9e29fed87f491f5f47066271613bb51e0aae5363.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the flask. We call $H=20\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical flask, and $R=10\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the chemical level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the flask to the surface of the chemical, $r$ the radius of the flask at the chemical surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the flask.  All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dh}{\\textrm dt} = -2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the flask to the chemical surface decreases as the chemist pours the chemical into the flask. \n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume being poured into the flask is changing when the chemical level is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ So, we wish to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dv}{\\textrm dt}$ when the level of the chemical is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-4 \\\\  & =20-4 \\\\  & =16\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the chemical must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {(16)}^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-128\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of chemical is increasing at a rate of $128\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{min}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064623 :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $3$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $3$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3h \\\\ 2r & =3h \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{2}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{4}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\pi (6)^{2}(5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186061800 :answer-field/correct "answer-57891982-57da-5fc3-b173-c9da485953c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-333c09d3-9ab7-5199-a6ee-8a0523c90ba3" "answer-0021e499-c9c9-5399-ab15-a808d440e793" "answer-a2ab9bda-f54f-5fd3-9a70-40b55b3c8caf" "answer-57891982-57da-5fc3-b173-c9da485953c7" "answer-d7ee1130-7728-5761-bf36-0adeb71dec78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-333c09d3-9ab7-5199-a6ee-8a0523c90ba3" :answer/id #uuid "333c09d3-9ab7-5199-a6ee-8a0523c90ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "135\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0021e499-c9c9-5399-ab15-a808d440e793" :answer/id #uuid "0021e499-c9c9-5399-ab15-a808d440e793" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "180\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2ab9bda-f54f-5fd3-9a70-40b55b3c8caf" :answer/id #uuid "a2ab9bda-f54f-5fd3-9a70-40b55b3c8caf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57891982-57da-5fc3-b173-c9da485953c7" :answer/id #uuid "57891982-57da-5fc3-b173-c9da485953c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "405\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7ee1130-7728-5761-bf36-0adeb71dec78" :answer/id #uuid "d7ee1130-7728-5761-bf36-0adeb71dec78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064964 :question/problem "The radius of the base of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h}.$ The height of the cone is always half its radius. At what rate is the cone's volume decreasing when the radius of its base is $12\\,\\text{m}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height of the cone is half its radius. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{r}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{r}{2})=\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{6}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi (12)^{2}(-4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063839 :answer-field/correct "answer-3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8bb8f76-f47c-5af3-a659-05c32947975b" "answer-2e3cb6f9-311e-5c6f-968f-d329ca876acf" "answer-3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" "answer-d59b9e51-13c3-51f1-9ffd-54c886c3a3a8" "answer-e5e695d2-b727-526d-9159-909e8a375b4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8bb8f76-f47c-5af3-a659-05c32947975b" :answer/id #uuid "a8bb8f76-f47c-5af3-a659-05c32947975b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e3cb6f9-311e-5c6f-968f-d329ca876acf" :answer/id #uuid "2e3cb6f9-311e-5c6f-968f-d329ca876acf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "256\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" :answer/id #uuid "3d8c52e3-c501-5c7c-b667-05e94d257b23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "288\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d59b9e51-13c3-51f1-9ffd-54c886c3a3a8" :answer/id #uuid "d59b9e51-13c3-51f1-9ffd-54c886c3a3a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "396\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5e695d2-b727-526d-9159-909e8a375b4d" :answer/id #uuid "e5e695d2-b727-526d-9159-909e8a375b4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "512\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a8aac6cd-427e-5b7e-aea2-44c84e419786" :question/id #uuid "a8aac6cd-427e-5b7e-aea2-44c84e419786" :question/math-academy-id "q-344979" :question/problem "The height of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume decrease when the height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer. \n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is decreasing at a rate of $6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-feafb06a-b02a-5dd2-94bc-ae63b61d224f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-feafb06a-b02a-5dd2-94bc-ae63b61d224f" :answer-field/id #uuid "feafb06a-b02a-5dd2-94bc-ae63b61d224f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fa70caeb-2f25-52b2-8b3f-fb7c469344cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fa70caeb-2f25-52b2-8b3f-fb7c469344cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa70caeb-2f25-52b2-8b3f-fb7c469344cc" :answer/id #uuid "fa70caeb-2f25-52b2-8b3f-fb7c469344cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9c01ea5e-36e4-5dc4-b2f4-1bae38b20756" :question/id #uuid "9c01ea5e-36e4-5dc4-b2f4-1bae38b20756" :question/math-academy-id "q-121577" :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. At what rate is the cone's volume increasing when its height is $6.25\\,\\text{ft}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=24\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6.25\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $\\frac{3}{5}$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{3}{5}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3}{5}h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi (6.25)^{2}(24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =337.5\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-280e8158-7d58-5a49-88aa-b2cc4ef5bccd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-280e8158-7d58-5a49-88aa-b2cc4ef5bccd" :answer-field/id #uuid "280e8158-7d58-5a49-88aa-b2cc4ef5bccd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0abcbebd-7c08-5f95-93f9-60a749c27aaa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70863b33-d7a3-56cd-b7b3-5735d6e956d4" "answer-62bf8dbe-cb88-555d-b459-07ea5c638011" "answer-0abcbebd-7c08-5f95-93f9-60a749c27aaa" "answer-bdd59757-1ec6-567f-a901-7ffce748ec99" "answer-6d03114f-fac2-542f-bc4d-f828eac18aac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70863b33-d7a3-56cd-b7b3-5735d6e956d4" :answer/id #uuid "70863b33-d7a3-56cd-b7b3-5735d6e956d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "162\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62bf8dbe-cb88-555d-b459-07ea5c638011" :answer/id #uuid "62bf8dbe-cb88-555d-b459-07ea5c638011" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "253.25\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0abcbebd-7c08-5f95-93f9-60a749c27aaa" :answer/id #uuid "0abcbebd-7c08-5f95-93f9-60a749c27aaa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "337.5\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bdd59757-1ec6-567f-a901-7ffce748ec99" :answer/id #uuid "bdd59757-1ec6-567f-a901-7ffce748ec99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "506.25\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d03114f-fac2-542f-bc4d-f828eac18aac" :answer/id #uuid "6d03114f-fac2-542f-bc4d-f828eac18aac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-748701f6-8780-55dc-bf9a-808d48cf28f4" :question/id #uuid "748701f6-8780-55dc-bf9a-808d48cf28f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-344973" :question/problem "The height of a cone is increasing at a rate of $4\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always $4$ times its height. Find the rate of the cone's volume increase when height is $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is $4$ times the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =4h \\\\ 2r & =4h \\\\ r & =2h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(2h)}^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's volume is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d0b2151-e0e8-5527-9aa9-647f2c498e2c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d0b2151-e0e8-5527-9aa9-647f2c498e2c" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d0b2151-e0e8-5527-9aa9-647f2c498e2c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dab41013-31c6-5b8c-b810-4bf4de38c3b2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dab41013-31c6-5b8c-b810-4bf4de38c3b2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dab41013-31c6-5b8c-b810-4bf4de38c3b2" :answer/id #uuid "dab41013-31c6-5b8c-b810-4bf4de38c3b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cc3b1d02-1e54-57cd-be11-d438beee9721" :question/id #uuid "cc3b1d02-1e54-57cd-be11-d438beee9721" :question/math-academy-id "q-345004" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3623402e5d5df9339f1a3bb615c92ff5fcff9ae8d96c3630c5cb847a3038eda.png)\n\nWater is leaking out of a cone-shaped container so that the volume of the water decreases at a rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The height of the container is three times its base radius. Find the rate of decrease of the radius of the water's surface when the radius is $6\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe radius is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the conical volume of water and $r$ be the radius of its surface. Both of these quantities, as well as the water's volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we want the rate of change, which is $r.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base radius. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =3\\pi (6)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -12 & =108\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the water's surface decreases at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b00b4530-77ae-52d8-a12b-a7e5a3f6636e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b00b4530-77ae-52d8-a12b-a7e5a3f6636e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b00b4530-77ae-52d8-a12b-a7e5a3f6636e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-11a5e07f-0354-53c6-9022-843d420732f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-11a5e07f-0354-53c6-9022-843d420732f7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11a5e07f-0354-53c6-9022-843d420732f7" :answer/id #uuid "11a5e07f-0354-53c6-9022-843d420732f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9\\pi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-babb3260-0ebb-5378-81f6-fb356d66422e" :question/id #uuid "babb3260-0ebb-5378-81f6-fb356d66422e" :question/math-academy-id "q-121579" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$ The radius of the cone is always $7$ times its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $2\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-14\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=2\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is $7$ times its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =7h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (7h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{49}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =49\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-14\\pi  & =49\\pi (2)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2d2e2d58-67fb-57e4-aef4-64ca1ad57e53"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2d2e2d58-67fb-57e4-aef4-64ca1ad57e53" :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2e2d58-67fb-57e4-aef4-64ca1ad57e53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-93180942-662e-521d-95f4-59f18fd03a19" :answer-field/choices ["answer-93180942-662e-521d-95f4-59f18fd03a19" "answer-fceac06e-7b17-533f-bd12-c185f7837e53" "answer-229bed8d-b5d3-58ef-a06f-be1e52de4063" "answer-a21f0c1b-6a7d-5506-8dce-b92d689a2837" "answer-b18d3479-01df-5337-bbb1-e4fa31aead16"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93180942-662e-521d-95f4-59f18fd03a19" :answer/id #uuid "93180942-662e-521d-95f4-59f18fd03a19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fceac06e-7b17-533f-bd12-c185f7837e53" :answer/id #uuid "fceac06e-7b17-533f-bd12-c185f7837e53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-229bed8d-b5d3-58ef-a06f-be1e52de4063" :answer/id #uuid "229bed8d-b5d3-58ef-a06f-be1e52de4063" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a21f0c1b-6a7d-5506-8dce-b92d689a2837" :answer/id #uuid "a21f0c1b-6a7d-5506-8dce-b92d689a2837" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{14}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b18d3479-01df-5337-bbb1-e4fa31aead16" :answer/id #uuid "b18d3479-01df-5337-bbb1-e4fa31aead16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{49}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7cce7dd9-a52f-5429-96fd-d564ac81f3b8" :question/id #uuid "7cce7dd9-a52f-5429-96fd-d564ac81f3b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-314057" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The diameter of the cone is always one third of its height.  Find the rate of the cone's height increase when the  height is $6\\,\\text{m}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe height is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4}\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the diameter is one third of the height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ 2r & =\\frac{1}{3}h \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{6}h)}^{2}h=\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the cone's height is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\text{ m/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e388d934-e923-52d0-a739-f7b5c411547b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e388d934-e923-52d0-a739-f7b5c411547b" :answer-field/id #uuid "e388d934-e923-52d0-a739-f7b5c411547b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0258576c-2f5d-548e-804e-389c4853daef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0258576c-2f5d-548e-804e-389c4853daef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0258576c-2f5d-548e-804e-389c4853daef" :answer/id #uuid "0258576c-2f5d-548e-804e-389c4853daef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d9e85e6a-5a02-52f7-ba3c-489084bc4a6f" :question/id #uuid "d9e85e6a-5a02-52f7-ba3c-489084bc4a6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-85828" :question/problem "The volume of a cone is decreasing at a rate of $72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ The radius of the cone is always equal to its height. At what rate is the height of the cone decreasing when its height is $6\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the cone and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-72\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nWe're told that the radius of the cone is equal to its height. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (h)^{2}\\cdot h=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating just $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-72 & =\\pi (6)^{2}(\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the cone is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a4767cd-3a6a-5b55-9fbe-f78b2bfcbb23"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a4767cd-3a6a-5b55-9fbe-f78b2bfcbb23" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a4767cd-3a6a-5b55-9fbe-f78b2bfcbb23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7c443a45-9404-54f4-93dd-6d71dc7f3bdf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-056a5d91-b8d1-5034-8b2c-8042c880a87f" "answer-7bf8fedf-c4d0-57a1-b87f-123455f19838" "answer-7c443a45-9404-54f4-93dd-6d71dc7f3bdf" "answer-83cce91b-baa0-5b4c-a9d5-d79bc959c759" "answer-b13b97bc-d673-581c-880d-84a73ebfe6a2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-056a5d91-b8d1-5034-8b2c-8042c880a87f" :answer/id #uuid "056a5d91-b8d1-5034-8b2c-8042c880a87f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bf8fedf-c4d0-57a1-b87f-123455f19838" :answer/id #uuid "7bf8fedf-c4d0-57a1-b87f-123455f19838" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c443a45-9404-54f4-93dd-6d71dc7f3bdf" :answer/id #uuid "7c443a45-9404-54f4-93dd-6d71dc7f3bdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83cce91b-baa0-5b4c-a9d5-d79bc959c759" :answer/id #uuid "83cce91b-baa0-5b4c-a9d5-d79bc959c759" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b13b97bc-d673-581c-880d-84a73ebfe6a2" :answer/id #uuid "b13b97bc-d673-581c-880d-84a73ebfe6a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a637ece-bf3b-5e1e-8874-577affcbf215" :question/id #uuid "9a637ece-bf3b-5e1e-8874-577affcbf215" :question/math-academy-id "q-121586" :question/problem "![](images/80/8036801735554767a33bdee0ff96a95d7e44bc3f97bacb396ec0de6d38f4aafc.png)\n\nA cone is being filled with ice-cream at a rate of $4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The height of the cone is always three times its base radius. At what rate is the radius of the ice cream volume increasing when the radius is $1\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $h$ the height of the ice cream volume and $r$ the radius of its base. Both of these quantities, as well as the volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=1\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $h$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $h$ in terms of $r.$\n\nWe're told that the height is always three times its base diameter. So, we have $h=3r.$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}(3r) \\\\  & =\\pi {r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $r,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\  & =3\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =3\\pi (1)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 4 & =3\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-125a201c-f016-58df-b9c9-cf8171479009"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-125a201c-f016-58df-b9c9-cf8171479009" :answer-field/id #uuid "125a201c-f016-58df-b9c9-cf8171479009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a085d722-ceba-5426-86f9-cd0b7a044c9b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c472d93-f8d7-582e-a35d-5be209a4a317" "answer-eed0c3f6-f826-5ceb-b4f8-53fcad93f069" "answer-a085d722-ceba-5426-86f9-cd0b7a044c9b" "answer-0e956afd-d5af-523e-bf93-e7cb03db6e05" "answer-35b62330-78eb-51e4-9620-36573cea83e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c472d93-f8d7-582e-a35d-5be209a4a317" :answer/id #uuid "0c472d93-f8d7-582e-a35d-5be209a4a317" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eed0c3f6-f826-5ceb-b4f8-53fcad93f069" :answer/id #uuid "eed0c3f6-f826-5ceb-b4f8-53fcad93f069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a085d722-ceba-5426-86f9-cd0b7a044c9b" :answer/id #uuid "a085d722-ceba-5426-86f9-cd0b7a044c9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e956afd-d5af-523e-bf93-e7cb03db6e05" :answer/id #uuid "0e956afd-d5af-523e-bf93-e7cb03db6e05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35b62330-78eb-51e4-9620-36573cea83e8" :answer/id #uuid "35b62330-78eb-51e4-9620-36573cea83e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e05d5196-22d0-5390-9d3c-413318bc7364" :question/id #uuid "e05d5196-22d0-5390-9d3c-413318bc7364" :question/math-academy-id "q-121673" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cf9fda11d138a5227307070d52fc0a27ce9decc35c91efbb026b89d4507f2ce7.png)\n\nA beverage is pumped into a conical dispenser at a constant rate of  $19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the tank is $70\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter and the height of the tank is $40\\,\\text{cm}.$ How fast is the beverage level increasing when the volume of the beverage in the dispenser is $12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}?$ Round your answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=40\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the tank, and $R=\\frac{70}{2}=35\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the solution level. We call $h$ the solution level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the solution. Both of these quantities, and the solution volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=19.6\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the solution must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{35}{40} \\\\ r & =\\frac{7h}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{7h}{8})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of the beverage in the dispenser is changing when $v=12.25\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12.25\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{192}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =48 \\\\ h & =2\\sqrt[3]{6}\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49}{64}\\pi (2\\sqrt[3]{6})^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 19.6\\pi  & =\\frac{49\\sqrt[3]{36}}{16}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & ≈1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the solution level is increasing at a rate of approximately $1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10f169f1-1b8d-5c6c-9710-963782923595"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10f169f1-1b8d-5c6c-9710-963782923595" :answer-field/id #uuid "10f169f1-1b8d-5c6c-9710-963782923595" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-eca9e441-c13f-5522-8094-775e21954936" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9de73f60-a26b-5029-90f2-6c89c972694e" "answer-67fb0ca3-d14d-5634-a340-353b7218f4cd" "answer-c184ced2-0b13-56b9-9636-16ecaf5ff14f" "answer-5a7f5af7-8937-5cd1-bd31-facbaffaf5a5" "answer-eca9e441-c13f-5522-8094-775e21954936"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9de73f60-a26b-5029-90f2-6c89c972694e" :answer/id #uuid "9de73f60-a26b-5029-90f2-6c89c972694e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.14\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-67fb0ca3-d14d-5634-a340-353b7218f4cd" :answer/id #uuid "67fb0ca3-d14d-5634-a340-353b7218f4cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.31\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c184ced2-0b13-56b9-9636-16ecaf5ff14f" :answer/id #uuid "c184ced2-0b13-56b9-9636-16ecaf5ff14f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.45\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a7f5af7-8937-5cd1-bd31-facbaffaf5a5" :answer/id #uuid "5a7f5af7-8937-5cd1-bd31-facbaffaf5a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.87\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eca9e441-c13f-5522-8094-775e21954936" :answer/id #uuid "eca9e441-c13f-5522-8094-775e21954936" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.94\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-829ceea0-b4f0-5bef-b797-d4b161d8e247" :question/id #uuid "829ceea0-b4f0-5bef-b797-d4b161d8e247" :question/math-academy-id "q-2558" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9c0d78ce05859db77dcc1e5198596a1a02c798c607ba9c4ff78e6fe1362b113.png)\n\nThe height of a conical cup is $6\\text{ cm}$ and the opening radius is $2\\text{ cm}.$ Water leaks out of the bottom at the rate of $2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If the water level is $3\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the water level decreasing at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cup, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{3})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi ({3}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2 & =\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0403bdc2-182d-5f43-a2bb-37b77dc8facc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0403bdc2-182d-5f43-a2bb-37b77dc8facc" :answer-field/id #uuid "0403bdc2-182d-5f43-a2bb-37b77dc8facc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-00e67d9a-a462-5f7e-b2cd-0ba68c332cb4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c7163626-4b88-545e-8c81-82995ff45ea0" "answer-d6781a0a-396a-5cdc-9fb4-752741391dc3" "answer-00e67d9a-a462-5f7e-b2cd-0ba68c332cb4" "answer-d10f03ec-49e1-57de-bfcd-f75d2f7decf7" "answer-468e364f-adb7-5316-81c4-30d5f46d5a70"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7163626-4b88-545e-8c81-82995ff45ea0" :answer/id #uuid "c7163626-4b88-545e-8c81-82995ff45ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6781a0a-396a-5cdc-9fb4-752741391dc3" :answer/id #uuid "d6781a0a-396a-5cdc-9fb4-752741391dc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00e67d9a-a462-5f7e-b2cd-0ba68c332cb4" :answer/id #uuid "00e67d9a-a462-5f7e-b2cd-0ba68c332cb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d10f03ec-49e1-57de-bfcd-f75d2f7decf7" :answer/id #uuid "d10f03ec-49e1-57de-bfcd-f75d2f7decf7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-468e364f-adb7-5316-81c4-30d5f46d5a70" :answer/id #uuid "468e364f-adb7-5316-81c4-30d5f46d5a70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{cm}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7d9fecf0-f483-51c1-b6ab-f1ee3ed71254" :question/id #uuid "7d9fecf0-f483-51c1-b6ab-f1ee3ed71254" :question/math-academy-id "q-51396" :question/problem "![](images/86/8676d9a562d254a1194502c5a45368d5345f81aee3a066c28acc87cb1f33812c.png)\n\nThe height of an ice cream cone is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $2\\,\\text{cm}.$ Ice cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice cream level is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice cream level decreasing at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=12\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=2\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{2}{12} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{6}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{6})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{108}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=6\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\pi ({6}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-beb4e4f6-e48c-5e57-a5a4-043092d6909c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-beb4e4f6-e48c-5e57-a5a4-043092d6909c" :answer-field/id #uuid "beb4e4f6-e48c-5e57-a5a4-043092d6909c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8b4c38d6-9e86-5118-af92-02f7b458a6e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8f8255a-7a62-5923-8d18-5d266361f1cf" "answer-9e13c728-dd08-55eb-9f0d-ac9fa0d1a989" "answer-80795005-7359-5d8c-8f47-fa8cce93244e" "answer-69fb2899-b036-54ad-a324-ab7b25c590cc" "answer-8b4c38d6-9e86-5118-af92-02f7b458a6e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8f8255a-7a62-5923-8d18-5d266361f1cf" :answer/id #uuid "b8f8255a-7a62-5923-8d18-5d266361f1cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e13c728-dd08-55eb-9f0d-ac9fa0d1a989" :answer/id #uuid "9e13c728-dd08-55eb-9f0d-ac9fa0d1a989" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80795005-7359-5d8c-8f47-fa8cce93244e" :answer/id #uuid "80795005-7359-5d8c-8f47-fa8cce93244e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69fb2899-b036-54ad-a324-ab7b25c590cc" :answer/id #uuid "69fb2899-b036-54ad-a324-ab7b25c590cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b4c38d6-9e86-5118-af92-02f7b458a6e7" :answer/id #uuid "8b4c38d6-9e86-5118-af92-02f7b458a6e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-734a1589-9b26-5ca8-b559-8254d0411564" :question/id #uuid "734a1589-9b26-5ca8-b559-8254d0411564" :question/math-academy-id "q-2840" :question/problem "![](images/77/7732722fc875e8cf13482394990baa0b5f8306a8151fca934491bb12dd181d9e.png)\n\nA  $10\\,\\text{cm}$ tall funnel containing water is being drained at a constant rate of  $10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ The mouth of the funnel is $12\\,\\text{cm}$ in diameter. How fast is the water level dropping when there is $15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of water remaining in the funnel?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=10\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=\\frac{12}{2}=6\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{6}{10} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{3h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=15\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\pi  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =125 \\\\ h & =5\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{9}{25}\\pi ({5}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -10 & =9\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-12d04c6e-e4aa-5010-b471-aea192835835"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-12d04c6e-e4aa-5010-b471-aea192835835" :answer-field/id #uuid "12d04c6e-e4aa-5010-b471-aea192835835" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b50bac0d-ca11-5f45-b354-8530b1736b79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b50bac0d-ca11-5f45-b354-8530b1736b79" "answer-0249d478-3dcc-5311-b2c6-8d70031c6a4c" "answer-c45c9a72-add8-5a9c-905f-a2e2391214d7" "answer-119c72b2-8cca-5163-9ca8-2e6e7e6248ae" "answer-27554380-74ae-59cc-9a00-1703f6d7f6b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b50bac0d-ca11-5f45-b354-8530b1736b79" :answer/id #uuid "b50bac0d-ca11-5f45-b354-8530b1736b79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{10}{9\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0249d478-3dcc-5311-b2c6-8d70031c6a4c" :answer/id #uuid "0249d478-3dcc-5311-b2c6-8d70031c6a4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c45c9a72-add8-5a9c-905f-a2e2391214d7" :answer/id #uuid "c45c9a72-add8-5a9c-905f-a2e2391214d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-119c72b2-8cca-5163-9ca8-2e6e7e6248ae" :answer/id #uuid "119c72b2-8cca-5163-9ca8-2e6e7e6248ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27554380-74ae-59cc-9a00-1703f6d7f6b1" :answer/id #uuid "27554380-74ae-59cc-9a00-1703f6d7f6b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f35215fb-23bc-5523-aa38-ea3ed45464a8" :question/id #uuid "f35215fb-23bc-5523-aa38-ea3ed45464a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121675" :question/problem "![](images/56/561e9e9bd41dddff575a95ed619d2ff3b91a48e0845960a6265c7c7cb1dc04fe.png)\n\nA  $50\\,\\text{cm}$ beverage dispenser is being emptied at a constant rate of  $16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$ The mouth of the dispenser is $50\\,\\text{cm}$ in radius. How fast is the beverage level dropping when there is $576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}$ of beverage remaining in the funnel?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the funnel. Let's call $H=50\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the funnel, and $R=50\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-16\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the funnel and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{50} \\\\ r & =h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(h)}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the level of water in the funnel is changing when $v=576\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}576\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {h}^{3} \\\\ {h}^{3} & =1\\,728 \\\\ h & =12\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values into the formula for $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\pi  & =\\pi (12)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -16 & =144\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the beverage level is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc7dbb10-4b31-5af6-9ef7-22087cd3d0f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc7dbb10-4b31-5af6-9ef7-22087cd3d0f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7dbb10-4b31-5af6-9ef7-22087cd3d0f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2a433a35-b2cc-5308-9b6c-9ce0311fa1f9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-624b5853-f054-5cfe-9d78-f4a308d6f488" "answer-7ce3d77b-948c-5d21-a770-9a08beecae0b" "answer-1c39d121-da05-557f-853d-ebcb21756f17" "answer-2a433a35-b2cc-5308-9b6c-9ce0311fa1f9" "answer-551882ea-b33c-50a8-a92c-3b8c3d1d3cb3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-624b5853-f054-5cfe-9d78-f4a308d6f488" :answer/id #uuid "624b5853-f054-5cfe-9d78-f4a308d6f488" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ce3d77b-948c-5d21-a770-9a08beecae0b" :answer/id #uuid "7ce3d77b-948c-5d21-a770-9a08beecae0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c39d121-da05-557f-853d-ebcb21756f17" :answer/id #uuid "1c39d121-da05-557f-853d-ebcb21756f17" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a433a35-b2cc-5308-9b6c-9ce0311fa1f9" :answer/id #uuid "2a433a35-b2cc-5308-9b6c-9ce0311fa1f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-551882ea-b33c-50a8-a92c-3b8c3d1d3cb3" :answer/id #uuid "551882ea-b33c-50a8-a92c-3b8c3d1d3cb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4ae8f6c-f256-5ee8-80b1-72f1337deae2" :question/id #uuid "e4ae8f6c-f256-5ee8-80b1-72f1337deae2" :question/math-academy-id "q-346239" :question/problem "![](images/01/01d6575ff056e7f99075597547583b4c648fbe6ad462d38a5d5fd76b8c73a564.png)\n\nA conical container has a height of $8\\,\\text{cm},$ and the radius at the bottom of the container is $4\\,\\text{cm}.$ Batter is being poured into the container. If the level of the batter in the container is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm/s}$, find the rate at which the volume of the batter is increasing when the radius of the batter surface is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume of batter is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=8\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=4\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the batter level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface, $r$ the radius of the cone at the surface of the batter, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{4}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nwhich is negative because the distance from the top of the container to the batter surface decreases as we pour the batter into the container.\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the batter must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{4}{8} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{2})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{12}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{h}{2} \\\\ h & =3\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\pi (3)^{2}(-\\frac{4}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of batter is increasing at a rate of $9\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5cf71d78-57d0-513b-a028-613da517c54d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5cf71d78-57d0-513b-a028-613da517c54d" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf71d78-57d0-513b-a028-613da517c54d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1c7c0f8d-b4b8-5c06-9ffe-1250080e6409" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c7c0f8d-b4b8-5c06-9ffe-1250080e6409"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c7c0f8d-b4b8-5c06-9ffe-1250080e6409" :answer/id #uuid "1c7c0f8d-b4b8-5c06-9ffe-1250080e6409" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9ddf5a2d-9bc3-5a54-8df4-57b5fda24b44" :question/id #uuid "9ddf5a2d-9bc3-5a54-8df4-57b5fda24b44" :question/math-academy-id "q-346630" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e56e31d999674075f3af880e8eadf14cd978f2dba73dd946dc103aae298f6681.png)\n\nAn inverted right circular conical tank contains sand. The height of the tank is $24\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter of its top base is $16\\,\\text{in}.$ The sand is draining from the bottom of the tank so that the height of the sand, $h,$ is decreasing at a constant rate of $6\\,\\text{in/h}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of sand is decreasing when the height of the sand is $9\\,\\text{in}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nHint: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe sand volume is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the tank. Let's call $H=24\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical tank, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top base. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the sand level. We call $h$ the height of the sand pile and $r$ the radius of the top surface of the sand. Both of these quantities, and the sand volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the tank and the remaining sand must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{24} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of sand is changing when $h=9\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (9)^{2}(-6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sand volume is decreasing at a rate of $54\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4a63ee4c-86fd-5c07-bea8-874c24302452"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4a63ee4c-86fd-5c07-bea8-874c24302452" :answer-field/id #uuid "4a63ee4c-86fd-5c07-bea8-874c24302452" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d4ec126f-fbd1-54e2-843e-0b31946cebb8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d4ec126f-fbd1-54e2-843e-0b31946cebb8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4ec126f-fbd1-54e2-843e-0b31946cebb8" :answer/id #uuid "d4ec126f-fbd1-54e2-843e-0b31946cebb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d2f18815-e582-5459-b43c-b02a5b4a0da8" :question/id #uuid "d2f18815-e582-5459-b43c-b02a5b4a0da8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121683" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f9998b94f05eb6345d375f35f4abc7f88edfc223c4a1af5e7bbc66c4b2a2f0bc.png)\n\nA conical paper cup is $15\\,\\text{m}$ tall with a diameter of $16\\,\\text{m}.$ The cup is being filled with water so that the water level rises at a rate of $1\\,\\text{m/h}.$ At what rate is the water volume increasing when the water level is $10\\,\\text{m}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=15\\,\\text{m}$ the height of the conical cup, and $R=\\frac{16}{2}=8\\,\\text{m}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,\\text{m}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{8}{15} \\\\ r & =\\frac{8}{15}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{8}{15}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{64}{675}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=10\\,\\text{m}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{64}{225}\\pi (10)^{2}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈28.4\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/\\text{h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the water volume increases at a rate of $28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce066dfd-c88a-512f-bf71-f1e9a3147622"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce066dfd-c88a-512f-bf71-f1e9a3147622" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce066dfd-c88a-512f-bf71-f1e9a3147622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12d068b6-eebb-56d2-b871-6bcee2d6ecbf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b8b705f5-d3b1-5098-b622-d4d8cebc73ea" "answer-8ef4b446-6b20-552f-a8fb-dd9ac2385b7b" "answer-5b0b6563-65dc-575c-a5c2-d7cfc1c7a1ec" "answer-ae90272c-b57b-51e7-a3c4-4d79de9d2898" "answer-12d068b6-eebb-56d2-b871-6bcee2d6ecbf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8b705f5-d3b1-5098-b622-d4d8cebc73ea" :answer/id #uuid "b8b705f5-d3b1-5098-b622-d4d8cebc73ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20.5\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ef4b446-6b20-552f-a8fb-dd9ac2385b7b" :answer/id #uuid "8ef4b446-6b20-552f-a8fb-dd9ac2385b7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22.3\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b0b6563-65dc-575c-a5c2-d7cfc1c7a1ec" :answer/id #uuid "5b0b6563-65dc-575c-a5c2-d7cfc1c7a1ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25.0\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae90272c-b57b-51e7-a3c4-4d79de9d2898" :answer/id #uuid "ae90272c-b57b-51e7-a3c4-4d79de9d2898" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27.6\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12d068b6-eebb-56d2-b871-6bcee2d6ecbf" :answer/id #uuid "12d068b6-eebb-56d2-b871-6bcee2d6ecbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28.4\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9bed4a9-ed2b-5603-85e6-df6b8285a648" :question/id #uuid "a9bed4a9-ed2b-5603-85e6-df6b8285a648" :question/math-academy-id "q-2858" :question/problem "![](images/51/51ee41eea6dfef1292c68a13a438f43c327639e01452e29998bbe513ac3838ba.png)\n\nA container has the shape of a right circular cone. The height of the container is $20\\,\\text{in}$ and the diameter at the top of the container is $10\\,\\text{in}.$ Water in the container is evaporating so that its depth, $h$, is decreasing at a constant rate of $\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in/h}.$ At what rate is the volume of water decreasing when the water depth is $12\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=20\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{10}{2}=5\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{10}{3}\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{5}{20} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{4})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the container is changing when $h=12\\,\\text{in}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi (12)^{2}(-\\frac{10}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =-30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water is decreasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-60ec6a45-4404-5e20-a3cb-94bf7320ffb4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-60ec6a45-4404-5e20-a3cb-94bf7320ffb4" :answer-field/id #uuid "60ec6a45-4404-5e20-a3cb-94bf7320ffb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e041c4f-bd1d-58b3-a192-9214f42c132b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5e041c4f-bd1d-58b3-a192-9214f42c132b" "answer-e72d9225-9845-59c5-9e04-07e4b8235685" "answer-8026da22-c9eb-5c97-bc44-dd272fdbe3e6" "answer-34f99a8a-1aff-5e48-8263-1c14bb296f09" "answer-3325c758-6521-5b3c-b952-88a1c7f3a5c3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e041c4f-bd1d-58b3-a192-9214f42c132b" :answer/id #uuid "5e041c4f-bd1d-58b3-a192-9214f42c132b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e72d9225-9845-59c5-9e04-07e4b8235685" :answer/id #uuid "e72d9225-9845-59c5-9e04-07e4b8235685" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8026da22-c9eb-5c97-bc44-dd272fdbe3e6" :answer/id #uuid "8026da22-c9eb-5c97-bc44-dd272fdbe3e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34f99a8a-1aff-5e48-8263-1c14bb296f09" :answer/id #uuid "34f99a8a-1aff-5e48-8263-1c14bb296f09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3325c758-6521-5b3c-b952-88a1c7f3a5c3" :answer/id #uuid "3325c758-6521-5b3c-b952-88a1c7f3a5c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "96\\pi {\\text{ in}}^{3}\\text{/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0c589335-1cfb-54d8-9a5c-d454270a6ef5" :question/id #uuid "0c589335-1cfb-54d8-9a5c-d454270a6ef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-121687" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d330e7c959ede9f969573752b9f67e3ce73c732a680b58cd2f9f466f1cf5db4c.png)\n\nA conical container is $21\\,\\text{ft}$ tall with a diameter of $14\\,\\text{ft}.$ The container is being emptied so that the liquid level lowers at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/min}.$ At what rate is the liquid volume decreasing when the liquid level is $16\\,\\text{ft}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=21\\,\\text{ft}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=\\frac{14}{2}=7\\,\\text{ft}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the liquid level. We call $h$ the height of the liquid and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the liquid. Both of these quantities, and the liquid volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the liquid must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{7}{21} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{1}{3}h)}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{27}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate at which the volume of water in the cup is changing when $h=16\\,\\text{ft}.$ \nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\pi (16)^{2}(-2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-56.9\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}/\\text{min}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the liquid volume decreases at a rate of approximately $56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-591430e3-f96f-5cc8-bf64-cc62f5a99def"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-591430e3-f96f-5cc8-bf64-cc62f5a99def" :answer-field/id #uuid "591430e3-f96f-5cc8-bf64-cc62f5a99def" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d7a46df-5161-5d18-a42e-a47e5c2a3b8e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6bc13e0-926a-5c5a-9aad-2a074883b5aa" "answer-da026eb5-0310-5dc3-beb3-c16b05bbfd8a" "answer-7d7a46df-5161-5d18-a42e-a47e5c2a3b8e" "answer-ab19582b-2dba-57b9-a8c1-04ac521939f0" "answer-f4822ccd-132b-598c-bd55-642e2fc02eba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6bc13e0-926a-5c5a-9aad-2a074883b5aa" :answer/id #uuid "d6bc13e0-926a-5c5a-9aad-2a074883b5aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32.6\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da026eb5-0310-5dc3-beb3-c16b05bbfd8a" :answer/id #uuid "da026eb5-0310-5dc3-beb3-c16b05bbfd8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "44.8\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d7a46df-5161-5d18-a42e-a47e5c2a3b8e" :answer/id #uuid "7d7a46df-5161-5d18-a42e-a47e5c2a3b8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "56.9\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab19582b-2dba-57b9-a8c1-04ac521939f0" :answer/id #uuid "ab19582b-2dba-57b9-a8c1-04ac521939f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "65.2\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4822ccd-132b-598c-bd55-642e2fc02eba" :answer/id #uuid "f4822ccd-132b-598c-bd55-642e2fc02eba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "69.5\\pi \\,\\,{\\text{ft}}^{3}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065389 :tutorial/content "We can use differentiation to solve related rates problems in the context of cones. In general, we follow the usual three steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n**Step 2:** Write down a relation between the time-dependent variables.\n\n**Step 3:** Differentiate the relation with respect to time and plug in the known values.\n\nBefore we start, recall that the volume $v$ of a right cone of radius $r$ and height $h$ (shown below) is given by the formula\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac13 \\pi r^2 h.\n$$\n\n![](images/cf/cf4e3b5b9f0d827bccaee008e006ad62d870fb2d782df8dd94f61bc4a3ca4050.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064437 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065330 :step/math-academy-id 631140 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065180 :step/math-academy-id 631141 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065331 :step/math-academy-id 631145 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065179 :step/math-academy-id 631148 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065332 :step/math-academy-id 631153 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124609 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362337 :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=1\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-1=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=1$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $x$ and $y.$ This gives\n\n$$\nx=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,y=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,1+\\sqrt{2}\\,$ | $\\,1-\\sqrt{2}\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}},-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}),$ and its maximum value is $1+\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124819 :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y)=2x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y-{x}^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global minimum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y-{x}^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2=-2\\lambda x \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{\\lambda } \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-1 & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(-1,1).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens upwards, there isn't a maximum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the minimum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(-1,1)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,-1\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,1),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121511 :answer-field/correct 17592186121516 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d" :question/id #uuid "a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162798" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y)=x+y\\,$ on the parabola $y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the global maximum of $f(x,y)$ exists on the parabola.*" :question/worked-solution "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=y+(x-2)^{2}=0.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2(x-2),1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}1=2\\lambda (x-2) \\\\ 1=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2\\lambda }+2 \\\\ \\lambda =1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now use $\\lambda $ to find $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}+2=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ into the condition $g(x,y)=0$ and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+{(\\frac{5}{2}-2)}^{2} & =0 \\\\ y+\\frac{1}{4} & =0 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has a possible extreme value at the point\n\n$$\n(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}).\n$$\n\nSince the given parabola opens downwards, there isn't a minimum value of $f$ on the parabola. Thus, the extreme value is the maximum of $f$ on the parabola. We evaluate $f$ at this point:\n\n| $\\,(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4})\\,$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $\\,\\frac{9}{4}\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(\\frac{5}{2},-\\frac{1}{4}),$ and its maximum value is $\\frac{9}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc58a902-969b-5c49-b676-fba60e52b489"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc58a902-969b-5c49-b676-fba60e52b489" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc58a902-969b-5c49-b676-fba60e52b489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4bee1f98-fa33-5093-87bc-2f00c2477543" "answer-2c8f830f-22a4-5f79-abbe-9554be0e81d9" "answer-43e96650-8ca0-524b-8e9f-31437aefa7cc" "answer-c43a5edc-f944-5fca-9515-c95ccf41820f" "answer-c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bee1f98-fa33-5093-87bc-2f00c2477543" :answer/id #uuid "4bee1f98-fa33-5093-87bc-2f00c2477543" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c8f830f-22a4-5f79-abbe-9554be0e81d9" :answer/id #uuid "2c8f830f-22a4-5f79-abbe-9554be0e81d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43e96650-8ca0-524b-8e9f-31437aefa7cc" :answer/id #uuid "43e96650-8ca0-524b-8e9f-31437aefa7cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c43a5edc-f944-5fca-9515-c95ccf41820f" :answer/id #uuid "c43a5edc-f944-5fca-9515-c95ccf41820f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7" :answer/id #uuid "c8eb3b1c-5d9a-5384-8da8-9f595ee3a2b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a8eccd29-e4a5-58ea-84f2-d615280c153b" :activity/id #uuid "a8eccd29-e4a5-58ea-84f2-d615280c153b" :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With One Constraint" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049390 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-277fd1b8-f855-56f4-9b8a-ec30a076c788" "step-b54506ce-0e0f-5fbb-b897-390b2885caf7" "step-65849aaa-3c3d-5a0b-9d2c-50bf3309805a" "step-48ac9f84-9cc5-5dd0-b18e-283909b67aa0" "step-87ea2bee-8725-54fe-b713-4f1f542f808d"] :activity/first-step "step-277fd1b8-f855-56f4-9b8a-ec30a076c788" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-277fd1b8-f855-56f4-9b8a-ec30a076c788" :step/id #uuid "277fd1b8-f855-56f4-9b8a-ec30a076c788" :step/content 17592186362337 :step/next "step-b54506ce-0e0f-5fbb-b897-390b2885caf7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b54506ce-0e0f-5fbb-b897-390b2885caf7" :step/id #uuid "b54506ce-0e0f-5fbb-b897-390b2885caf7" :step/content 17592186362338 :step/next "step-65849aaa-3c3d-5a0b-9d2c-50bf3309805a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-65849aaa-3c3d-5a0b-9d2c-50bf3309805a" :step/id #uuid "65849aaa-3c3d-5a0b-9d2c-50bf3309805a" :step/content 17592186281536 :step/next "step-48ac9f84-9cc5-5dd0-b18e-283909b67aa0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-48ac9f84-9cc5-5dd0-b18e-283909b67aa0" :step/id #uuid "48ac9f84-9cc5-5dd0-b18e-283909b67aa0" :step/content 17592186124819 :step/next "step-87ea2bee-8725-54fe-b713-4f1f542f808d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-87ea2bee-8725-54fe-b713-4f1f542f808d" :step/id #uuid "87ea2bee-8725-54fe-b713-4f1f542f808d" :step/content "question-a3c769ab-089f-58c3-aaa9-e70d964ccd5d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087842 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6baac323-becf-503f-bb38-28a603f7f3c3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087843 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-350d70bb-36ec-51d9-b44c-9369bfdb5b19" "question-a5878b34-7fd0-5b9f-89d8-91c764ed89b8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087844 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-86d3c939-36b2-5329-b38e-94ddc558ef8b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087544 :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x({x}^{2}+x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(x^2 +x+ 4) = 0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x = 0$ gives the solution $x = 0.$\n\n- The equation $x^2 +x+ 4 = 0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(1)^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =1-16 \\\\  & =-15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x = 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087546 :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)(x-3)}{({x}^{2}+1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-3)}}{({x}^{2}+1)\\cancel{(x-3)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\nx^2 + 1 = 0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions because the discriminant $\\mathcal D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-4(1)(1) \\\\  & =0-4 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-6baac323-becf-503f-bb38-28a603f7f3c3" :question/id #uuid "6baac323-becf-503f-bb38-28a603f7f3c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-278259" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x+1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0d48d2da-f7ad-5f3f-ad75-b0e9b627f848" "answer-field-92131e32-9ef2-589e-b0eb-e5ab25c20d08"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0d48d2da-f7ad-5f3f-ad75-b0e9b627f848" :answer-field/id #uuid "0d48d2da-f7ad-5f3f-ad75-b0e9b627f848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-150f071c-b071-52ef-88af-71bf6d63afd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-150f071c-b071-52ef-88af-71bf6d63afd4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-150f071c-b071-52ef-88af-71bf6d63afd4" :answer/id #uuid "150f071c-b071-52ef-88af-71bf6d63afd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-92131e32-9ef2-589e-b0eb-e5ab25c20d08" :answer-field/id #uuid "92131e32-9ef2-589e-b0eb-e5ab25c20d08" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1829539c-d3a1-5674-94e6-a734010a379a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1829539c-d3a1-5674-94e6-a734010a379a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1829539c-d3a1-5674-94e6-a734010a379a" :answer/id #uuid "1829539c-d3a1-5674-94e6-a734010a379a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-350d70bb-36ec-51d9-b44c-9369bfdb5b19" :question/id #uuid "350d70bb-36ec-51d9-b44c-9369bfdb5b19" :question/math-academy-id "q-6929" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2x+1)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(2x+1)(x+2)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions  $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1fb45bf1-efa9-5849-a776-e098b44b30ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1fb45bf1-efa9-5849-a776-e098b44b30ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "1fb45bf1-efa9-5849-a776-e098b44b30ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c34fe335-2676-5f50-ac6a-ad4e7a7bb927" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c34fe335-2676-5f50-ac6a-ad4e7a7bb927" "answer-ab84236c-d242-5f31-bc2e-1fab3d6a3a10" "answer-0ec569be-ba0a-519b-9bc2-090d3a2f231d" "answer-2888a284-8331-5647-a1fb-6327fa60225e" "answer-7ec6d5e0-dc19-53cc-b6c1-165c91fc49be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c34fe335-2676-5f50-ac6a-ad4e7a7bb927" :answer/id #uuid "c34fe335-2676-5f50-ac6a-ad4e7a7bb927" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab84236c-d242-5f31-bc2e-1fab3d6a3a10" :answer/id #uuid "ab84236c-d242-5f31-bc2e-1fab3d6a3a10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-2,\\,x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ec569be-ba0a-519b-9bc2-090d3a2f231d" :answer/id #uuid "0ec569be-ba0a-519b-9bc2-090d3a2f231d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-4,\\,x=-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2888a284-8331-5647-a1fb-6327fa60225e" :answer/id #uuid "2888a284-8331-5647-a1fb-6327fa60225e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-4,\\,x=-1,\\,x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ec6d5e0-dc19-53cc-b6c1-165c91fc49be" :answer/id #uuid "7ec6d5e0-dc19-53cc-b6c1-165c91fc49be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-8,\\,x=-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a5878b34-7fd0-5b9f-89d8-91c764ed89b8" :question/id #uuid "a5878b34-7fd0-5b9f-89d8-91c764ed89b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-70552" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is the only vertical asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-287da6b6-7396-5875-9477-af19bd2b3d94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-287da6b6-7396-5875-9477-af19bd2b3d94" :answer-field/id #uuid "287da6b6-7396-5875-9477-af19bd2b3d94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f6d6a03e-0c99-5db0-bcb9-636cd164f928" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8ff55830-2c39-53c2-b7e6-cfbcb904ef14" "answer-64e6a2ce-1827-54b2-abce-9bceebd0f865" "answer-f6d6a03e-0c99-5db0-bcb9-636cd164f928" "answer-05946333-97e1-583b-b079-89c0440789ce" "answer-7077a196-2c6e-5544-b0a6-fb1a04fcea93"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ff55830-2c39-53c2-b7e6-cfbcb904ef14" :answer/id #uuid "8ff55830-2c39-53c2-b7e6-cfbcb904ef14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64e6a2ce-1827-54b2-abce-9bceebd0f865" :answer/id #uuid "64e6a2ce-1827-54b2-abce-9bceebd0f865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6d6a03e-0c99-5db0-bcb9-636cd164f928" :answer/id #uuid "f6d6a03e-0c99-5db0-bcb9-636cd164f928" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05946333-97e1-583b-b079-89c0440789ce" :answer/id #uuid "05946333-97e1-583b-b079-89c0440789ce" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7077a196-2c6e-5544-b0a6-fb1a04fcea93" :answer/id #uuid "7077a196-2c6e-5544-b0a6-fb1a04fcea93" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-86d3c939-36b2-5329-b38e-94ddc558ef8b" :question/id #uuid "86d3c939-36b2-5329-b38e-94ddc558ef8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80681" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}-x+2=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =1-8 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da797173-8fb0-5fa8-932c-55153821e90c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da797173-8fb0-5fa8-932c-55153821e90c" :answer-field/id #uuid "da797173-8fb0-5fa8-932c-55153821e90c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25bb3972-ddaa-5e91-a6e5-423cf42073c1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffbd1c4d-40aa-55ef-a2ce-01968b4f8725" "answer-6326b577-cdc2-5a37-9ed8-b6dd08c069ba" "answer-eb772194-8f56-57ad-86a4-b559796a524b" "answer-7ff2983b-2294-55ca-b354-69dd5a064fd5" "answer-25bb3972-ddaa-5e91-a6e5-423cf42073c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffbd1c4d-40aa-55ef-a2ce-01968b4f8725" :answer/id #uuid "ffbd1c4d-40aa-55ef-a2ce-01968b4f8725" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-1,\\,x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6326b577-cdc2-5a37-9ed8-b6dd08c069ba" :answer/id #uuid "6326b577-cdc2-5a37-9ed8-b6dd08c069ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1,\\,x=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb772194-8f56-57ad-86a4-b559796a524b" :answer/id #uuid "eb772194-8f56-57ad-86a4-b559796a524b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ff2983b-2294-55ca-b354-69dd5a064fd5" :answer/id #uuid "7ff2983b-2294-55ca-b354-69dd5a064fd5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25bb3972-ddaa-5e91-a6e5-423cf42073c1" :answer/id #uuid "25bb3972-ddaa-5e91-a6e5-423cf42073c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186234067 :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc072fa3-a3a2-5452-b980-3ee1ebbabe24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc072fa3-a3a2-5452-b980-3ee1ebbabe24" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc072fa3-a3a2-5452-b980-3ee1ebbabe24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fc35e2f7-12e4-502a-98d0-05ca4dbbca92" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fc35e2f7-12e4-502a-98d0-05ca4dbbca92" "answer-a21c5c14-b3d6-5991-86ee-53ead623f9a5" "answer-6e47da48-817d-5b8a-a761-63f096255e59" "answer-02268068-5914-5785-8518-e18bf53b47ad" "answer-7894bf30-9f2a-5795-9fd3-911c5345c7bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc35e2f7-12e4-502a-98d0-05ca4dbbca92" :answer/id #uuid "fc35e2f7-12e4-502a-98d0-05ca4dbbca92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2},\\,x=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a21c5c14-b3d6-5991-86ee-53ead623f9a5" :answer/id #uuid "a21c5c14-b3d6-5991-86ee-53ead623f9a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e47da48-817d-5b8a-a761-63f096255e59" :answer/id #uuid "6e47da48-817d-5b8a-a761-63f096255e59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02268068-5914-5785-8518-e18bf53b47ad" :answer/id #uuid "02268068-5914-5785-8518-e18bf53b47ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7894bf30-9f2a-5795-9fd3-911c5345c7bb" :answer/id #uuid "7894bf30-9f2a-5795-9fd3-911c5345c7bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The function has no vertical asymptotes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088921 :tutorial/content "A **vertical asymptote** is a vertical line to which a function gets very close but never touches.\n\nFor example, let's consider the rational function \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{(x-2)(x+2)}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/79/79e02370d60aec1e1e7f9e21dafbb876ea1f9b9b2de2b3d2b8b6ba14ffabfe3d.png)\n\nThe two dotted lines $x=2$ and $x=-2$ are the vertical asymptotes of the function. The function gets closer and closer to these lines but never touches them.\n\nBut how can we calculate the vertical asymptotes of a function without using a graph?\n\nIn general, we can find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function using the following procedure:\n\n1\\. Factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors.\n\n2\\. Set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x .$\n\nIn our case, the function is already factored, and there are no common factors in the numerator and denominator. So, to find the vertical asymptotes, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x+2) = 0\n$$\n\nThe solutions to this equation are $x=2$ and $x=-2.$  Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$ This matches up with what we saw in the graph." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087895 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172042 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-061c757d-590b-5605-a6a4-5e9539c29dc6" "question-e9910a94-d71d-54e3-8cb5-b52ba8bdfed9" "question-f8cca8da-6044-5230-8f59-af0159f2bec9" "question-4dac21a9-fd23-5a65-b9b0-0999f6a3ac35" "question-17dce6e5-6992-5bd8-99ae-27a5145f8ae5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171848 :question/problem "A fair die has $12$ sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $12.$ What is the expected value of a random roll of this die?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number the die lands on. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 12\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\textrm E[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U \\{a, a + 1, \\ldots, b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U \\{ 1, 2, \\ldots, 12\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\dfrac{1+12}{2} = 6.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value is $6.5.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172043 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1e997cfb-3847-52d2-9127-67ab63d15dde" "question-2200f2ed-00f4-5dc2-a53c-43c556e4cd84" "question-56f939dc-3462-5544-b92f-c768cb817339" "question-d0deae3f-c105-5c17-970a-aab6db3301cd" "question-57d50a92-18de-56e3-a7c3-f6f999c0fbca"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171849 :question/problem "A fair die has sides labeled with numbers $4$ through $15.$ What is the variance of the numbers obtained when rolling this die?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number the die lands on. From the problem statement, we know that $X \\sim U \\{ 4, 5, \\ldots, 15 \\},$ and we wish to compute $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X \\sim U \\{a, a + 1, \\ldots, b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S = \\{a, a+1, \\ldots, b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =15-4+1 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X \\sim U \\{ 4, 5, \\ldots, 15 \\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{12^2 - 1}{12} = \\dfrac{143}{12}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance is $\\dfrac{143}{12}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-061c757d-590b-5605-a6a4-5e9539c29dc6" :question/id #uuid "061c757d-590b-5605-a6a4-5e9539c29dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-149461" :question/problem "In summer, the daily maximum temperature in a particular city (rounded to the nearest degree) varies between ${25}^{∘}C$ and ${36}^{∘}C,$ inclusive. Assuming that each maximum temperature value has the same probability of occurring on a given summer day, what is the expected value of the daily maximum temperature?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the daily maximum temperature (rounded to the nearest degree) on a randomly selected day. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{25,26,…,36\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{25+36}{2}=30.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the maximum temperature is ${30.5}^{∘}C.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fee83434-bea6-5d24-8061-9f7887df13cd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fee83434-bea6-5d24-8061-9f7887df13cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "fee83434-bea6-5d24-8061-9f7887df13cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b7ffb64-ed8a-5f80-9ea2-8337e02eab71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b79d5078-8977-524a-9cd8-7811d81ebe23" "answer-23e62e66-6874-55ab-ba69-892301b35036" "answer-5b7ffb64-ed8a-5f80-9ea2-8337e02eab71" "answer-41105bc3-efc1-57ce-a685-dc34b23250d4" "answer-8e8c9b42-2856-5eca-b358-7f3a1c9bfbf1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b79d5078-8977-524a-9cd8-7811d81ebe23" :answer/id #uuid "b79d5078-8977-524a-9cd8-7811d81ebe23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{29.5}^{∘}C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23e62e66-6874-55ab-ba69-892301b35036" :answer/id #uuid "23e62e66-6874-55ab-ba69-892301b35036" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{29}^{∘}C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b7ffb64-ed8a-5f80-9ea2-8337e02eab71" :answer/id #uuid "5b7ffb64-ed8a-5f80-9ea2-8337e02eab71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30.5}^{∘}C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41105bc3-efc1-57ce-a685-dc34b23250d4" :answer/id #uuid "41105bc3-efc1-57ce-a685-dc34b23250d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30}^{∘}C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e8c9b42-2856-5eca-b358-7f3a1c9bfbf1" :answer/id #uuid "8e8c9b42-2856-5eca-b358-7f3a1c9bfbf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{31.5}^{∘}C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9910a94-d71d-54e3-8cb5-b52ba8bdfed9" :question/id #uuid "e9910a94-d71d-54e3-8cb5-b52ba8bdfed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-149467" :question/problem "In a math test, students have to answer a multiple-choice question, where the answers are labeled from $0$ to $6.$ If Steph selects an answer at random, what is the expected value of her chosen answer?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of the chosen answer. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{0,1,…,6\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{0+6}{2}=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen answer is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73b46de2-2050-5a32-a2b1-20ea872f37a3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73b46de2-2050-5a32-a2b1-20ea872f37a3" :answer-field/id #uuid "73b46de2-2050-5a32-a2b1-20ea872f37a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-79275914-5d9c-5c8c-82f4-240ddc318b56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c5fd90f-c90f-54ef-8a7e-37270bd09def" "answer-79275914-5d9c-5c8c-82f4-240ddc318b56" "answer-445cdad2-f731-5550-954e-5375b1603f59" "answer-76a7eda3-f8be-53a8-8129-8e4f2f5b435d" "answer-50706252-4cc9-5b73-a0a1-41411c3dfaf1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c5fd90f-c90f-54ef-8a7e-37270bd09def" :answer/id #uuid "3c5fd90f-c90f-54ef-8a7e-37270bd09def" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79275914-5d9c-5c8c-82f4-240ddc318b56" :answer/id #uuid "79275914-5d9c-5c8c-82f4-240ddc318b56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-445cdad2-f731-5550-954e-5375b1603f59" :answer/id #uuid "445cdad2-f731-5550-954e-5375b1603f59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76a7eda3-f8be-53a8-8129-8e4f2f5b435d" :answer/id #uuid "76a7eda3-f8be-53a8-8129-8e4f2f5b435d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50706252-4cc9-5b73-a0a1-41411c3dfaf1" :answer/id #uuid "50706252-4cc9-5b73-a0a1-41411c3dfaf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8cca8da-6044-5230-8f59-af0159f2bec9" :question/id #uuid "f8cca8da-6044-5230-8f59-af0159f2bec9" :question/math-academy-id "q-97686" :question/problem "Lucy's marbles are labeled with numbers $-3$ through $8.$ If Lucy chooses a marble at random, what is the expected value of the chosen marble?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number on the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{-3,-2,…,8\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{-3,-2,…,8\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{-3+8}{2}=2.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen marble is $2.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a5e331db-3e94-5f66-9647-04d18ac848fa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5e331db-3e94-5f66-9647-04d18ac848fa" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e331db-3e94-5f66-9647-04d18ac848fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7d2ad557-2ff4-5a06-8c6b-c2198562c980" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20cf2347-734c-59f0-8d38-4914b0f59872" "answer-7d2ad557-2ff4-5a06-8c6b-c2198562c980" "answer-0bb18bb9-04e6-5364-9edf-e9f090bde6b8" "answer-443997de-1648-5657-9a9f-fbd252621d6b" "answer-6fd9a2fd-6bfa-526d-85b1-51dc29c80930"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20cf2347-734c-59f0-8d38-4914b0f59872" :answer/id #uuid "20cf2347-734c-59f0-8d38-4914b0f59872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d2ad557-2ff4-5a06-8c6b-c2198562c980" :answer/id #uuid "7d2ad557-2ff4-5a06-8c6b-c2198562c980" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0bb18bb9-04e6-5364-9edf-e9f090bde6b8" :answer/id #uuid "0bb18bb9-04e6-5364-9edf-e9f090bde6b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-443997de-1648-5657-9a9f-fbd252621d6b" :answer/id #uuid "443997de-1648-5657-9a9f-fbd252621d6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6fd9a2fd-6bfa-526d-85b1-51dc29c80930" :answer/id #uuid "6fd9a2fd-6bfa-526d-85b1-51dc29c80930" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4dac21a9-fd23-5a65-b9b0-0999f6a3ac35" :question/id #uuid "4dac21a9-fd23-5a65-b9b0-0999f6a3ac35" :question/math-academy-id "q-97653" :question/problem "Ann chooses a card at random from a deck of cards labeled $28$ through $65.$  What is the expected value of the chosen number?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the value of the number chosen. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{28,29,…,65\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{28,29,…,65\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{28+65}{2}=46.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen number is $46.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-64f16db9-01e1-5022-b628-d7fb9a1a158c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-64f16db9-01e1-5022-b628-d7fb9a1a158c" :answer-field/id #uuid "64f16db9-01e1-5022-b628-d7fb9a1a158c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80779f00-66da-5d77-9263-f46efafd4786" :answer-field/choices ["answer-300ddeda-7b55-5f6f-89a4-743bae59195a" "answer-80779f00-66da-5d77-9263-f46efafd4786" "answer-15942d05-c04c-5fec-acfd-214aa02bd9e2" "answer-cd8f4bb4-6117-5ad7-b11c-5e92b496e511" "answer-5f3dc81f-5e80-5290-a8f3-71e8b20db9e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-300ddeda-7b55-5f6f-89a4-743bae59195a" :answer/id #uuid "300ddeda-7b55-5f6f-89a4-743bae59195a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "46" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80779f00-66da-5d77-9263-f46efafd4786" :answer/id #uuid "80779f00-66da-5d77-9263-f46efafd4786" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "46.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15942d05-c04c-5fec-acfd-214aa02bd9e2" :answer/id #uuid "15942d05-c04c-5fec-acfd-214aa02bd9e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "47" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd8f4bb4-6117-5ad7-b11c-5e92b496e511" :answer/id #uuid "cd8f4bb4-6117-5ad7-b11c-5e92b496e511" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "47.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f3dc81f-5e80-5290-a8f3-71e8b20db9e4" :answer/id #uuid "5f3dc81f-5e80-5290-a8f3-71e8b20db9e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-17dce6e5-6992-5bd8-99ae-27a5145f8ae5" :question/id #uuid "17dce6e5-6992-5bd8-99ae-27a5145f8ae5" :question/math-academy-id "q-97661" :question/problem "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $70$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store does not have the first $10$ issues in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the expected value of the chosen issue?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of the selected issue. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{11,12…,70\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{E}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then $X$ has the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{a+b}{2}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{11,12,…,70\\},$ we have the following expected value:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11+70}{2}=40.5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected value of the chosen number is $40.5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8e0d0aa-1c21-5a25-b57e-4af99d80d340"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8e0d0aa-1c21-5a25-b57e-4af99d80d340" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8e0d0aa-1c21-5a25-b57e-4af99d80d340" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-42fc9fab-ee26-5c8e-8118-2f6cb80a2248" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9d487b6-ed79-5dc1-ab39-7e81e4eb1566" "answer-05f16bfe-9d21-56eb-b0eb-cf81ad7da361" "answer-2eb2d4ce-443f-5483-8106-4785809a785f" "answer-42fc9fab-ee26-5c8e-8118-2f6cb80a2248" "answer-7d4d83a3-f6df-5c00-9cba-9e140b768621"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9d487b6-ed79-5dc1-ab39-7e81e4eb1566" :answer/id #uuid "e9d487b6-ed79-5dc1-ab39-7e81e4eb1566" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05f16bfe-9d21-56eb-b0eb-cf81ad7da361" :answer/id #uuid "05f16bfe-9d21-56eb-b0eb-cf81ad7da361" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2eb2d4ce-443f-5483-8106-4785809a785f" :answer/id #uuid "2eb2d4ce-443f-5483-8106-4785809a785f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42fc9fab-ee26-5c8e-8118-2f6cb80a2248" :answer/id #uuid "42fc9fab-ee26-5c8e-8118-2f6cb80a2248" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d4d83a3-f6df-5c00-9cba-9e140b768621" :answer/id #uuid "7d4d83a3-f6df-5c00-9cba-9e140b768621" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "41" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1e997cfb-3847-52d2-9127-67ab63d15dde" :question/id #uuid "1e997cfb-3847-52d2-9127-67ab63d15dde" :question/math-academy-id "q-97677" :question/problem "A traditional festival is usually held on a single day after the $6$th and before the $15$th of September. The exact day is selected each year and depends on the weather conditions. Assuming that each day after the $6$th and before the $15$th of September has the same probability of being chosen, what is the standard deviation of the selected days? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the date of the selected day. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{7,8,…,14\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =14-7+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{7,8,…,14\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{8}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{63}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{63}{12}}≈2.29.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-869e1747-44a9-5a0c-bf12-3ac41ecf6be3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-869e1747-44a9-5a0c-bf12-3ac41ecf6be3" :answer-field/id #uuid "869e1747-44a9-5a0c-bf12-3ac41ecf6be3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a38f5599-d61b-5a09-a3d4-9a183da4b04f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a38f5599-d61b-5a09-a3d4-9a183da4b04f" "answer-a125b2c6-d0e3-5bca-82a7-a665091f0a9d" "answer-7a77fed5-2ba6-529c-b2b4-c567b5738f39" "answer-28d54e01-612b-51a9-95d4-bf586e1dafa4" "answer-a3e11fdc-0c7e-5f9a-89ed-ed5da8b344d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a38f5599-d61b-5a09-a3d4-9a183da4b04f" :answer/id #uuid "a38f5599-d61b-5a09-a3d4-9a183da4b04f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.29" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a125b2c6-d0e3-5bca-82a7-a665091f0a9d" :answer/id #uuid "a125b2c6-d0e3-5bca-82a7-a665091f0a9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.45" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a77fed5-2ba6-529c-b2b4-c567b5738f39" :answer/id #uuid "7a77fed5-2ba6-529c-b2b4-c567b5738f39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28d54e01-612b-51a9-95d4-bf586e1dafa4" :answer/id #uuid "28d54e01-612b-51a9-95d4-bf586e1dafa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.89" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3e11fdc-0c7e-5f9a-89ed-ed5da8b344d9" :answer/id #uuid "a3e11fdc-0c7e-5f9a-89ed-ed5da8b344d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2200f2ed-00f4-5dc2-a53c-43c556e4cd84" :question/id #uuid "2200f2ed-00f4-5dc2-a53c-43c556e4cd84" :question/math-academy-id "q-97687" :question/problem "Lucy's marbles are labeled with numbers $0$ through $9.$ If Lucy chooses a marble at random, what is the standard deviation of the numbers of the selected marbles? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of the chosen marble. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{0,1,…,9\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =9-0+1 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{0,1,…,9\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{10}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{99}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{99}{12}}≈2.87.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3cf40b77-3ab5-59b6-90e4-0f17d078e3d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cf40b77-3ab5-59b6-90e4-0f17d078e3d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf40b77-3ab5-59b6-90e4-0f17d078e3d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6f424c05-1480-5131-af5e-6301d6c236d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-79caa7ce-46ad-55e2-b7c2-043a0fad8141" "answer-7eedec42-febe-5b57-bb9d-ded0c88caada" "answer-6f424c05-1480-5131-af5e-6301d6c236d0" "answer-c5edd35f-5e6f-5071-859d-b0ea47da1df0" "answer-3f9a88d8-ca73-5ff5-8ab0-35488e393aea"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79caa7ce-46ad-55e2-b7c2-043a0fad8141" :answer/id #uuid "79caa7ce-46ad-55e2-b7c2-043a0fad8141" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eedec42-febe-5b57-bb9d-ded0c88caada" :answer/id #uuid "7eedec42-febe-5b57-bb9d-ded0c88caada" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f424c05-1480-5131-af5e-6301d6c236d0" :answer/id #uuid "6f424c05-1480-5131-af5e-6301d6c236d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.87" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5edd35f-5e6f-5071-859d-b0ea47da1df0" :answer/id #uuid "c5edd35f-5e6f-5071-859d-b0ea47da1df0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f9a88d8-ca73-5ff5-8ab0-35488e393aea" :answer/id #uuid "3f9a88d8-ca73-5ff5-8ab0-35488e393aea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-56f939dc-3462-5544-b92f-c768cb817339" :question/id #uuid "56f939dc-3462-5544-b92f-c768cb817339" :question/math-academy-id "q-149510" :question/problem "A spinner divided into eight equal sections numbered $2$ through $9$ is spun. What is the variance of the numbers obtained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number obtained by spinning the spinner. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{2,3,…,9\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =9-2+1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{2,3,…,9\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{8}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{63}{12}=5.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the variance of the numbers obtained is $5.25$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-03d527bc-ebb6-5c35-b1e7-c65135d3ef4a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-03d527bc-ebb6-5c35-b1e7-c65135d3ef4a" :answer-field/id #uuid "03d527bc-ebb6-5c35-b1e7-c65135d3ef4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6bd67d5-7c1f-5c2c-9a46-0ef1a20e4c70" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f65db99c-57e5-5e25-8db1-cffb700d0d10" "answer-fae0d576-f0f9-50db-9357-1c4257578253" "answer-ca6903e7-fb79-5a78-beff-0b8e832b93a8" "answer-a6bd67d5-7c1f-5c2c-9a46-0ef1a20e4c70" "answer-e4b719eb-6ae8-58bf-9dac-72caecc590b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f65db99c-57e5-5e25-8db1-cffb700d0d10" :answer/id #uuid "f65db99c-57e5-5e25-8db1-cffb700d0d10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fae0d576-f0f9-50db-9357-1c4257578253" :answer/id #uuid "fae0d576-f0f9-50db-9357-1c4257578253" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.05" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca6903e7-fb79-5a78-beff-0b8e832b93a8" :answer/id #uuid "ca6903e7-fb79-5a78-beff-0b8e832b93a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6bd67d5-7c1f-5c2c-9a46-0ef1a20e4c70" :answer/id #uuid "a6bd67d5-7c1f-5c2c-9a46-0ef1a20e4c70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4b719eb-6ae8-58bf-9dac-72caecc590b9" :answer/id #uuid "e4b719eb-6ae8-58bf-9dac-72caecc590b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d0deae3f-c105-5c17-970a-aab6db3301cd" :question/id #uuid "d0deae3f-c105-5c17-970a-aab6db3301cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-97662" :question/problem "Jack wants to buy a single issue of a particular comic series with $55$ issues. Unfortunately, the comic store does not have the first $7$ issues in stock. If Jack buys a comic at random from the available issues, what is the standard deviation of the issue numbers he could select? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of the issue Jack selects. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{8,9,…,55\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =55-8+1 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{8,9,…,55\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{48}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{2303}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{2303}{12}}≈13.85.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81f39eb4-0d43-54f8-9984-4333850219c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81f39eb4-0d43-54f8-9984-4333850219c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "81f39eb4-0d43-54f8-9984-4333850219c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-481a7a00-f069-5a6a-8af6-970d5cab079e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cabcbf3-2946-56f9-90a0-75fe304c4571" "answer-ecd40b35-713e-5902-b659-478c76c266f8" "answer-481a7a00-f069-5a6a-8af6-970d5cab079e" "answer-82a3d961-1417-5f80-8d5c-e3c1b0544af2" "answer-0a1203d0-1e7b-54e5-8193-b827f429d9cf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cabcbf3-2946-56f9-90a0-75fe304c4571" :answer/id #uuid "5cabcbf3-2946-56f9-90a0-75fe304c4571" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecd40b35-713e-5902-b659-478c76c266f8" :answer/id #uuid "ecd40b35-713e-5902-b659-478c76c266f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11.52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-481a7a00-f069-5a6a-8af6-970d5cab079e" :answer/id #uuid "481a7a00-f069-5a6a-8af6-970d5cab079e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.85" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82a3d961-1417-5f80-8d5c-e3c1b0544af2" :answer/id #uuid "82a3d961-1417-5f80-8d5c-e3c1b0544af2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14.85" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a1203d0-1e7b-54e5-8193-b827f429d9cf" :answer/id #uuid "0a1203d0-1e7b-54e5-8193-b827f429d9cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18.33" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-57d50a92-18de-56e3-a7c3-f6f999c0fbca" :question/id #uuid "57d50a92-18de-56e3-a7c3-f6f999c0fbca" :question/math-academy-id "q-97695" :question/problem "In a video game, the number of obstacles that show up during a game can vary between $80$ and $125,$ inclusive. Assuming that each number of obstacles has the same probability of occurring, what is the standard deviation of the number of obstacles? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the number of obstacles in a randomly selected game. From the problem statement, we know that $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ and we wish to compute $\\text{SD}[X].$\n\nIn general, if $X∼U\\{a,a+1,…,b\\},$ then the variance of $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\n$$\n\nwhere $S=\\{a,a+1,…,b\\}.$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|S| & =b-a+1 \\\\  & =125-80+1 \\\\  & =46.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for our random variable $X∼U\\{80,81,…,125\\},$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{{46}^{2}-1}{12}=\\frac{2\\,115}{12}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SD}[X]=\\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,115}{12}}≈13.28.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-acd72576-8d1e-5c1b-9d00-62b65f8647c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-acd72576-8d1e-5c1b-9d00-62b65f8647c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "acd72576-8d1e-5c1b-9d00-62b65f8647c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b162a2b7-4a87-587f-adb8-e0ce9c946199" :answer-field/choices ["answer-773abe72-877b-5c5e-bf53-c3b27d4edd5e" "answer-b162a2b7-4a87-587f-adb8-e0ce9c946199" "answer-bf2423de-5d8a-5b6f-866d-ecbbbc5a1ac5" "answer-10213f2b-2013-52dd-8671-4f8389a29c25" "answer-ae12b462-1a1a-5233-90f4-ad10dc2f9177"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-773abe72-877b-5c5e-bf53-c3b27d4edd5e" :answer/id #uuid "773abe72-877b-5c5e-bf53-c3b27d4edd5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11.20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b162a2b7-4a87-587f-adb8-e0ce9c946199" :answer/id #uuid "b162a2b7-4a87-587f-adb8-e0ce9c946199" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf2423de-5d8a-5b6f-866d-ecbbbc5a1ac5" :answer/id #uuid "bf2423de-5d8a-5b6f-866d-ecbbbc5a1ac5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13.86" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10213f2b-2013-52dd-8671-4f8389a29c25" :answer/id #uuid "10213f2b-2013-52dd-8671-4f8389a29c25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae12b462-1a1a-5233-90f4-ad10dc2f9177" :answer/id #uuid "ae12b462-1a1a-5233-90f4-ad10dc2f9177" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15.36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173220 :tutorial/content "We know that if a random variable $X$ follows a *discrete* uniform distribution, then it has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/0a/0abfcf5eccaaaf22dbc144f58098d394297a5819a1857ff316ce9e9d01e44f7d.svg)\n\nLet's now suppose that \n\n$$\nX\\sim U\\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are integers. In other words, we're concerned with the special case where the support $S$ of $X$ contains every integer between $a$ and $b$ inclusive. \n\nIn this case, it can be shown that the mean value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}.\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these results at the end of the lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173221 :tutorial/content "Here, we will show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{a+b}{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the probability mass function of $X$ is given by\n\n![](images/36/36e73322640df22386428fdc41d1940ef7469587566c2f4f24936158292c087c.svg)\n\nBy the definition of expected value for discrete random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}xf(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{|S|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}[a+(a+1)+(a+2)+⋯+b].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the term in the square brackets is an arithmetic series with the first term $a$ and the last term $b.$ Therefore, using the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\frac{1}{|S|}[a+(a+1)+(a+2)+⋯+b] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot \\frac{|S|}{2}\\cdot (a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{|S|}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{|S|}}{2}\\cdot (a+b) \\\\  & =\\frac{a+b}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173222 :tutorial/content "Let's now show that for $X\\sim \\{a, a+1, a+2, \\ldots, b\\},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{|S|^2-1}{12}.\n$$\n\nWe will assume, without loss of generality, that the distribution of our random variable $X$ is given by\n\n$$\nX\\sim\\{1, 2, 3, \\ldots, |S|\\}.\n$$\n\nWe do not lose generality here because, in general, the variance (i.e., spread) of a random variable is unaffected by translations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X + c] = \\textrm{Var}[X]\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy the definition of $\\textrm{E}[X^2],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}f(x) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{|S|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot {\\sum }_{x\\in S}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now make use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n i^2 = \\dfrac16 n (n+1)(2n+1)\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot [{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+⋯+|S{|}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{|S|}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot |S|\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\cancel{|S|}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot \\cancel{|S|}\\cdot (|S|+1)(2|S|+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\left(\\textrm{E}[X]\\right)^2, \\qquad \\textrm {E}[X] = \\dfrac{1+|S|}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-{(\\text{E}[X])}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-{(\\frac{|S|+1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{6}-\\frac{(|S|+1)^{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(|S|+1)(2|S|+1)}{12}-\\frac{3(|S|+1)^{2}}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(2(2|S|+1)-3(|S|+1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(4|S|+2-3|S|-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)}{12}(|S|-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{(|S|+1)(|S|-1)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{|S{|}^{2}-1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172192 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186165752 :answer-field/choices 17592186165753] [:db/retract 17592186165752 :answer-field/choices 17592186165754] [:db/retract 17592186165752 :answer-field/choices 17592186165755] [:db/retract 17592186165752 :answer-field/choices 17592186165756] [:db/retract 17592186165752 :answer-field/choices 17592186165757] {:db/id 17592186166762 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c4169769-2e08-50ac-8627-9cd65f653a9b" "question-9541bdac-540f-537e-a50e-b6a223ada95f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4169769-2e08-50ac-8627-9cd65f653a9b" :question/id #uuid "c4169769-2e08-50ac-8627-9cd65f653a9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95567" :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=-2\\operatorname{coth}⁡g,\\,g=4x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=2{csch}^{2}\\,g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2{csch}^{2}⁡g\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡g \\\\  & =8{csch}^{2}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d79f42f3-7a3e-5cfc-ae54-7f17a62555f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d79f42f3-7a3e-5cfc-ae54-7f17a62555f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "d79f42f3-7a3e-5cfc-ae54-7f17a62555f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b0b12e96-3aee-519e-afa6-98c880294233" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15d0f986-68fd-5c05-b61f-2cb59af8e4c9" "answer-cc4b8906-6e7c-5f46-b779-8abef094c635" "answer-0718ee2b-934a-5775-88b9-654a98a3df48" "answer-ea15c1a2-ef8e-52bd-843d-747ef10b4c18" "answer-b0b12e96-3aee-519e-afa6-98c880294233"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15d0f986-68fd-5c05-b61f-2cb59af8e4c9" :answer/id #uuid "15d0f986-68fd-5c05-b61f-2cb59af8e4c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sech}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc4b8906-6e7c-5f46-b779-8abef094c635" :answer/id #uuid "cc4b8906-6e7c-5f46-b779-8abef094c635" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8{sech}^{2}⁡4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0718ee2b-934a-5775-88b9-654a98a3df48" :answer/id #uuid "0718ee2b-934a-5775-88b9-654a98a3df48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x{csch}^{2}⁡4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea15c1a2-ef8e-52bd-843d-747ef10b4c18" :answer/id #uuid "ea15c1a2-ef8e-52bd-843d-747ef10b4c18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\operatorname{csch}⁡4x\\operatorname{coth}⁡4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0b12e96-3aee-519e-afa6-98c880294233" :answer/id #uuid "b0b12e96-3aee-519e-afa6-98c880294233" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{csch}^{2}⁡4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9541bdac-540f-537e-a50e-b6a223ada95f" :question/id #uuid "9541bdac-540f-537e-a50e-b6a223ada95f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95575" :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=3{csch}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=3{g}^{2},\\,g=\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=6g,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two results together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =6g(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =6(\\operatorname{csch}⁡x)(-\\operatorname{csch}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x) \\\\  & =-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3aa2d316-4c50-510d-84fd-3aca1c107d6d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3aa2d316-4c50-510d-84fd-3aca1c107d6d" :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa2d316-4c50-510d-84fd-3aca1c107d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7fd63de1-2c45-5706-8764-86c4c4a37694" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b333d000-5330-53c7-b6f3-3aea595f25e7" "answer-7fd63de1-2c45-5706-8764-86c4c4a37694" "answer-ced58843-851c-5823-a2b2-32f84d2a86b7" "answer-10bda358-e756-5a52-bd52-be83ca51ee6d" "answer-c03bde7f-fe4b-5a37-b96a-bf72de4e1aed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b333d000-5330-53c7-b6f3-3aea595f25e7" :answer/id #uuid "b333d000-5330-53c7-b6f3-3aea595f25e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7fd63de1-2c45-5706-8764-86c4c4a37694" :answer/id #uuid "7fd63de1-2c45-5706-8764-86c4c4a37694" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ced58843-851c-5823-a2b2-32f84d2a86b7" :answer/id #uuid "ced58843-851c-5823-a2b2-32f84d2a86b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3{csch}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10bda358-e756-5a52-bd52-be83ca51ee6d" :answer/id #uuid "10bda358-e756-5a52-bd52-be83ca51ee6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{csch}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c03bde7f-fe4b-5a37-b96a-bf72de4e1aed" :answer/id #uuid "c03bde7f-fe4b-5a37-b96a-bf72de4e1aed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\operatorname{csch}⁡x{coth}^{2}⁡x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166763 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-26dd88c1-a8ad-595c-af19-c13d906feda9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-26dd88c1-a8ad-595c-af19-c13d906feda9" :question/id #uuid "26dd88c1-a8ad-595c-af19-c13d906feda9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95587" :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=x{sech}^{2}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={sech}^{2}⁡x.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(x)}^{′}\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot {({sech}^{2}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {sech}^{2}⁡x+x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{sech}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x-2x{sech}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c10c01cc-e0f9-571f-a0fb-7e4e517f6d40"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c10c01cc-e0f9-571f-a0fb-7e4e517f6d40" :answer-field/id #uuid "c10c01cc-e0f9-571f-a0fb-7e4e517f6d40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a262826-fbb0-5320-bb2e-6379a76bc331" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc9239d3-c5f2-52a5-a408-c11d0f1f88ff" "answer-e28ecdb1-81b7-55ae-928b-208d2f35a6c8" "answer-a095eeaa-71bd-5a30-9655-035de8150b53" "answer-4a262826-fbb0-5320-bb2e-6379a76bc331" "answer-51c51e52-9010-5b3c-8047-10aa85488383"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc9239d3-c5f2-52a5-a408-c11d0f1f88ff" :answer/id #uuid "cc9239d3-c5f2-52a5-a408-c11d0f1f88ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(1+2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e28ecdb1-81b7-55ae-928b-208d2f35a6c8" :answer/id #uuid "e28ecdb1-81b7-55ae-928b-208d2f35a6c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sech}⁡x(1-2x{coth}^{2}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a095eeaa-71bd-5a30-9655-035de8150b53" :answer/id #uuid "a095eeaa-71bd-5a30-9655-035de8150b53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1+2x\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a262826-fbb0-5320-bb2e-6379a76bc331" :answer/id #uuid "4a262826-fbb0-5320-bb2e-6379a76bc331" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-51c51e52-9010-5b3c-8047-10aa85488383" :answer/id #uuid "51c51e52-9010-5b3c-8047-10aa85488383" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{sech}^{2}⁡x(1-2x{tanh}^{2}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166764 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-04ba3fd8-1522-5fe9-a412-8ecb77ad06a4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167424 :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡x$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)}^{′}\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot {({e}^{x})}^{′}}{{({e}^{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-{csch}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot {e}^{x}-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x\\cdot ({e}^{x})}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}({csch}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x)}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186165752 :answer-field/correct "answer-2d5366df-ea2b-5885-a020-a71b3a496864" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20d8011e-02b2-51c3-977c-1f56b7e9e7b2" "answer-2d5366df-ea2b-5885-a020-a71b3a496864" "answer-8b14f7f2-bbd6-5875-adfd-d43889b7fb7f" "answer-d65566be-873f-5b60-b8d3-57f1caa7ee23" "answer-0546a58c-c189-5300-98ab-59e9dfa06285"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20d8011e-02b2-51c3-977c-1f56b7e9e7b2" :answer/id #uuid "20d8011e-02b2-51c3-977c-1f56b7e9e7b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{csch}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d5366df-ea2b-5885-a020-a71b3a496864" :answer/id #uuid "2d5366df-ea2b-5885-a020-a71b3a496864" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8b14f7f2-bbd6-5875-adfd-d43889b7fb7f" :answer/id #uuid "8b14f7f2-bbd6-5875-adfd-d43889b7fb7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{{sech}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d65566be-873f-5b60-b8d3-57f1caa7ee23" :answer/id #uuid "d65566be-873f-5b60-b8d3-57f1caa7ee23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0546a58c-c189-5300-98ab-59e9dfa06285" :answer/id #uuid "0546a58c-c189-5300-98ab-59e9dfa06285" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{csch}^{2}⁡x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡x}{{e}^{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04ba3fd8-1522-5fe9-a412-8ecb77ad06a4" :question/id #uuid "04ba3fd8-1522-5fe9-a412-8ecb77ad06a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95894" :question/problem "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=2x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}^{′}\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot {(2x)}^{′}}{{(2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2{csch}^{2}⁡2x)\\cdot 2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x\\cdot (2)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x)}{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b899f25-79c8-5b1d-9f3a-fc090dc3cb6c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b899f25-79c8-5b1d-9f3a-fc090dc3cb6c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b899f25-79c8-5b1d-9f3a-fc090dc3cb6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bd8e33a9-dc6f-59b9-918f-d2977e259e1a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd8e33a9-dc6f-59b9-918f-d2977e259e1a" "answer-6cf34f11-a6ed-51e9-8bc5-739fb989d58d" "answer-58a1b07b-7bfc-5b97-8af6-3ca183bb6652" "answer-9cb97b6b-b48e-57d0-b4f2-a804af9d0be7" "answer-e33ea1d6-0d1e-5b38-a621-13b06c55032e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd8e33a9-dc6f-59b9-918f-d2977e259e1a" :answer/id #uuid "bd8e33a9-dc6f-59b9-918f-d2977e259e1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cf34f11-a6ed-51e9-8bc5-739fb989d58d" :answer/id #uuid "6cf34f11-a6ed-51e9-8bc5-739fb989d58d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2x{sech}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58a1b07b-7bfc-5b97-8af6-3ca183bb6652" :answer/id #uuid "58a1b07b-7bfc-5b97-8af6-3ca183bb6652" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2x{csch}^{2}⁡2x+\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{2{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cb97b6b-b48e-57d0-b4f2-a804af9d0be7" :answer/id #uuid "9cb97b6b-b48e-57d0-b4f2-a804af9d0be7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e33ea1d6-0d1e-5b38-a621-13b06c55032e" :answer/id #uuid "e33ea1d6-0d1e-5b38-a621-13b06c55032e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}{sech}^{2}⁡2x-\\operatorname{coth}⁡2x}{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-eb9e0140-b867-5e68-babf-1d38ffa4ad68" :activity/id #uuid "eb9e0140-b867-5e68-babf-1d38ffa4ad68" :activity/title "Differentiating Reciprocal Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050543 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-ad7abaa3-35f9-55c7-b149-f9e8e8e19cba" "step-e4dec1c3-78d1-5931-a7d8-f44f4625ddc5" "step-67b9f987-8c65-5ef3-b902-335deeb911ed" "step-ce8110a4-9d2c-5b3b-9898-9ddc1bc1bc21" "step-b6ba3a49-e9bd-51c0-9196-2a99672203a3"] :activity/first-step "step-ad7abaa3-35f9-55c7-b149-f9e8e8e19cba" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad7abaa3-35f9-55c7-b149-f9e8e8e19cba" :step/id #uuid "ad7abaa3-35f9-55c7-b149-f9e8e8e19cba" :step/content "question-c4169769-2e08-50ac-8627-9cd65f653a9b" :step/next "step-e4dec1c3-78d1-5931-a7d8-f44f4625ddc5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e4dec1c3-78d1-5931-a7d8-f44f4625ddc5" :step/id #uuid "e4dec1c3-78d1-5931-a7d8-f44f4625ddc5" :step/content "question-9541bdac-540f-537e-a50e-b6a223ada95f" :step/next "step-67b9f987-8c65-5ef3-b902-335deeb911ed" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-67b9f987-8c65-5ef3-b902-335deeb911ed" :step/id #uuid "67b9f987-8c65-5ef3-b902-335deeb911ed" :step/content "question-26dd88c1-a8ad-595c-af19-c13d906feda9" :step/next "step-ce8110a4-9d2c-5b3b-9898-9ddc1bc1bc21" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ce8110a4-9d2c-5b3b-9898-9ddc1bc1bc21" :step/id #uuid "ce8110a4-9d2c-5b3b-9898-9ddc1bc1bc21" :step/content 17592186167424 :step/next "step-b6ba3a49-e9bd-51c0-9196-2a99672203a3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b6ba3a49-e9bd-51c0-9196-2a99672203a3" :step/id #uuid "b6ba3a49-e9bd-51c0-9196-2a99672203a3" :step/content "question-04ba3fd8-1522-5fe9-a412-8ecb77ad06a4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054758 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cf9e8f5c-9d67-5259-afe2-aab0c57cf6c7" "question-b0314c88-d178-5a7a-83d0-8641a658389f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf9e8f5c-9d67-5259-afe2-aab0c57cf6c7" :question/id #uuid "cf9e8f5c-9d67-5259-afe2-aab0c57cf6c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-85403" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5})(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}-(\\sqrt{5})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{7-5} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5})}{2} & = \\\\ 2\\sqrt{7}+2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ad8e7c2f-1d55-5d7a-b0fa-0d52194e43c1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ad8e7c2f-1d55-5d7a-b0fa-0d52194e43c1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ad8e7c2f-1d55-5d7a-b0fa-0d52194e43c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-04c9a852-e246-5d02-8c8e-89e562e88723" :answer-field/choices ["answer-04c9a852-e246-5d02-8c8e-89e562e88723" "answer-bc41c789-d259-5c4e-96da-500ae024e7b9" "answer-d1896968-8316-53ce-b6a2-57163eef17bd" "answer-277ec95c-e9fb-5172-b6aa-157ff86fcf2f" "answer-e13fa96a-d1f6-5081-8b2d-b82ac6692cd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04c9a852-e246-5d02-8c8e-89e562e88723" :answer/id #uuid "04c9a852-e246-5d02-8c8e-89e562e88723" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}+2\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc41c789-d259-5c4e-96da-500ae024e7b9" :answer/id #uuid "bc41c789-d259-5c4e-96da-500ae024e7b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{7}+4\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1896968-8316-53ce-b6a2-57163eef17bd" :answer/id #uuid "d1896968-8316-53ce-b6a2-57163eef17bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\sqrt{7}-4\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-277ec95c-e9fb-5172-b6aa-157ff86fcf2f" :answer/id #uuid "277ec95c-e9fb-5172-b6aa-157ff86fcf2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e13fa96a-d1f6-5081-8b2d-b82ac6692cd0" :answer/id #uuid "e13fa96a-d1f6-5081-8b2d-b82ac6692cd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0314c88-d178-5a7a-83d0-8641a658389f" :question/id #uuid "b0314c88-d178-5a7a-83d0-8641a658389f" :question/math-academy-id "q-293620" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{6}+2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{(\\sqrt{6})^{2}-(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6-4\\cdot 3} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6-12} & = \\\\ \\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{(-6)} & = \\\\ -\\frac{8(\\sqrt{6}-2\\sqrt{3})}{6} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(2\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6})}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8fe0ed43-db8f-54cd-a271-1c201a6b718d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8fe0ed43-db8f-54cd-a271-1c201a6b718d" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fe0ed43-db8f-54cd-a271-1c201a6b718d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-41df7d9a-ec3e-5f59-99bb-cd2cedae5946" :answer-field/choices ["answer-655338a1-a2e7-599f-a75e-c996cca2d51d" "answer-23024f2b-1cf3-5a6b-bdad-17fe1468934b" "answer-0fd1eabe-6fb4-500f-8be1-ff96601a4dbe" "answer-41df7d9a-ec3e-5f59-99bb-cd2cedae5946" "answer-8ea56f0c-6a63-55f2-a590-10edcfd3d963"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-655338a1-a2e7-599f-a75e-c996cca2d51d" :answer/id #uuid "655338a1-a2e7-599f-a75e-c996cca2d51d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(2\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23024f2b-1cf3-5a6b-bdad-17fe1468934b" :answer/id #uuid "23024f2b-1cf3-5a6b-bdad-17fe1468934b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(8\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6})}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fd1eabe-6fb4-500f-8be1-ff96601a4dbe" :answer/id #uuid "0fd1eabe-6fb4-500f-8be1-ff96601a4dbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4(2\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{6})}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41df7d9a-ec3e-5f59-99bb-cd2cedae5946" :answer/id #uuid "41df7d9a-ec3e-5f59-99bb-cd2cedae5946" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4(2\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6})}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ea56f0c-6a63-55f2-a590-10edcfd3d963" :answer/id #uuid "8ea56f0c-6a63-55f2-a590-10edcfd3d963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054759 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1f231ab0-36cf-59c7-86c7-11a5d98e603e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1f231ab0-36cf-59c7-86c7-11a5d98e603e" :question/id #uuid "1f231ab0-36cf-59c7-86c7-11a5d98e603e" :question/math-academy-id "q-293702" :question/problem "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{3}-2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{3}-2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}+2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{\\sqrt{3}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{(5-\\sqrt{3})(\\sqrt{3}-2)}{(\\sqrt{3}+2)(\\sqrt{3}-2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{3}-10-(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+2\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}-(2)^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{3}-10-3}{3-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{7\\sqrt{3}-13}{-1} & = \\\\ 13-7\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-19de62f6-8bdc-5e1a-9324-111045c1341c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-19de62f6-8bdc-5e1a-9324-111045c1341c" :answer-field/id #uuid "19de62f6-8bdc-5e1a-9324-111045c1341c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bf310402-50e8-58d7-9dfe-6bd390350eee" :answer-field/choices ["answer-67e2b9da-2fe4-50ed-9618-993678c6f082" "answer-bf310402-50e8-58d7-9dfe-6bd390350eee" "answer-b9738106-7d51-517a-9063