" :step/id #uuid "56760cc4-3829-59a9-a759-c24a29d21680" :step/content "question-b0314c88-d178-5a7a-83d0-8641a658389f" :step/next "step-01296d4e-52f1-5b16-9285-4d32f1136eff" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-01296d4e-52f1-5b16-9285-4d32f1136eff" :step/id #uuid "01296d4e-52f1-5b16-9285-4d32f1136eff" :step/content "question-1f231ab0-36cf-59c7-86c7-11a5d98e603e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186100455 :answer-field/choices 17592186100456] [:db/retract 17592186100455 :answer-field/choices 17592186100457] [:db/retract 17592186100455 :answer-field/choices 17592186100458] [:db/retract 17592186100455 :answer-field/choices 17592186100459] [:db/retract 17592186100455 :answer-field/choices 17592186100460] [:db/retract 17592186100467 :answer-field/choices 17592186100468] [:db/retract 17592186100467 :answer-field/choices 17592186100469] [:db/retract 17592186100467 :answer-field/choices 17592186100470] [:db/retract 17592186100467 :answer-field/choices 17592186100471] [:db/retract 17592186100467 :answer-field/choices 17592186100472] {:db/id 17592186100943 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-893452ea-005e-5fa0-9579-b213ff801fba"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101549 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=500.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100455 :answer-field/correct "answer-cf651d75-4fcd-5021-9ec0-003f1d0c2149" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d85e959-a5d2-57fb-865e-ce7038ba9c8c" "answer-e6223966-2351-5f3e-86f0-c546dcff0000" "answer-3d57d462-ef3d-519d-9fd5-bf23c60439b5" "answer-cf651d75-4fcd-5021-9ec0-003f1d0c2149" "answer-7b3d47cb-be7b-539e-8964-2c16730aedcf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d85e959-a5d2-57fb-865e-ce7038ba9c8c" :answer/id #uuid "0d85e959-a5d2-57fb-865e-ce7038ba9c8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2(500-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6223966-2351-5f3e-86f0-c546dcff0000" :answer/id #uuid "e6223966-2351-5f3e-86f0-c546dcff0000" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d57d462-ef3d-519d-9fd5-bf23c60439b5" :answer/id #uuid "3d57d462-ef3d-519d-9fd5-bf23c60439b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf651d75-4fcd-5021-9ec0-003f1d0c2149" :answer/id #uuid "cf651d75-4fcd-5021-9ec0-003f1d0c2149" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2y(500-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b3d47cb-be7b-539e-8964-2c16730aedcf" :answer/id #uuid "7b3d47cb-be7b-539e-8964-2c16730aedcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=500-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-893452ea-005e-5fa0-9579-b213ff801fba" :question/id #uuid "893452ea-005e-5fa0-9579-b213ff801fba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83846" :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=20.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c929a608-d503-51e7-8e16-80118c54e14b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c929a608-d503-51e7-8e16-80118c54e14b" :answer-field/id #uuid "c929a608-d503-51e7-8e16-80118c54e14b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-55c32120-58ea-521e-945f-a4d006827dfc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3bfabfe5-1bde-5d94-bc4f-bdd3166c3f50" "answer-674fb894-2a3f-511b-92a2-711f65663bf9" "answer-80937afc-7ea6-5bca-890e-a680c897e7bc" "answer-46cc8677-e9f5-50f1-957a-d6c09372ed81" "answer-55c32120-58ea-521e-945f-a4d006827dfc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bfabfe5-1bde-5d94-bc4f-bdd3166c3f50" :answer/id #uuid "3bfabfe5-1bde-5d94-bc4f-bdd3166c3f50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=20-y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-674fb894-2a3f-511b-92a2-711f65663bf9" :answer/id #uuid "674fb894-2a3f-511b-92a2-711f65663bf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5(20-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80937afc-7ea6-5bca-890e-a680c897e7bc" :answer/id #uuid "80937afc-7ea6-5bca-890e-a680c897e7bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46cc8677-e9f5-50f1-957a-d6c09372ed81" :answer/id #uuid "46cc8677-e9f5-50f1-957a-d6c09372ed81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55c32120-58ea-521e-945f-a4d006827dfc" :answer/id #uuid "55c32120-58ea-521e-945f-a4d006827dfc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y(20-y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101551 :question/problem "An influenza epidemic is spreading through a community of $700\\,000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with the flu is jointly proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ measured in days,  and the number of uninfected people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.6,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the growth rate of infected people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have the flu at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected people, the number of infected people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.6,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=700\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have the flu is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000-P).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100467 :answer-field/correct "answer-cdb1b6f5-2157-5ffa-857f-384cd5652759" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4c7ca84a-cf9f-50f8-b341-b5cad100965c" "answer-cedd52a2-345c-58b2-928e-6c0218a680ab" "answer-cdb1b6f5-2157-5ffa-857f-384cd5652759" "answer-b251d084-9823-5f88-b5bc-44200f475b8a" "answer-6ee52ea6-c780-5145-af3b-00bf2afb2de4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c7ca84a-cf9f-50f8-b341-b5cad100965c" :answer/id #uuid "4c7ca84a-cf9f-50f8-b341-b5cad100965c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.6P(700\\,000+P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cedd52a2-345c-58b2-928e-6c0218a680ab" :answer/id #uuid "cedd52a2-345c-58b2-928e-6c0218a680ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000+P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdb1b6f5-2157-5ffa-857f-384cd5652759" :answer/id #uuid "cdb1b6f5-2157-5ffa-857f-384cd5652759" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.6P(700\\,000-P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b251d084-9823-5f88-b5bc-44200f475b8a" :answer/id #uuid "b251d084-9823-5f88-b5bc-44200f475b8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=700\\,000P(0.6+P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ee52ea6-c780-5145-af3b-00bf2afb2de4" :answer/id #uuid "6ee52ea6-c780-5145-af3b-00bf2afb2de4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{dP}{dt}=700\\,000P(0.6-P)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-85f9efd2-54dd-542f-9fb0-3457207d9896" :activity/id #uuid "85f9efd2-54dd-542f-9fb0-3457207d9896" :activity/title "Logistic Growth Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048578 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-845e97b0-decc-50f2-997f-93c6453f8cf7" "step-576ab541-b8f7-5797-923d-f3afc45640a1" "step-876b041e-48ee-59ed-9570-27aae1e87501" "step-c8f38971-defd-50cd-9dce-38d71497ce0f" "step-4245bf2c-f044-5000-a8dc-9e8cf792d0e0"] :activity/first-step "step-845e97b0-decc-50f2-997f-93c6453f8cf7" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-845e97b0-decc-50f2-997f-93c6453f8cf7" :step/id #uuid "845e97b0-decc-50f2-997f-93c6453f8cf7" :step/content 17592186101549 :step/next "step-576ab541-b8f7-5797-923d-f3afc45640a1" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-576ab541-b8f7-5797-923d-f3afc45640a1" :step/id #uuid "576ab541-b8f7-5797-923d-f3afc45640a1" :step/content "question-893452ea-005e-5fa0-9579-b213ff801fba" :step/next "step-876b041e-48ee-59ed-9570-27aae1e87501" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-876b041e-48ee-59ed-9570-27aae1e87501" :step/id #uuid "876b041e-48ee-59ed-9570-27aae1e87501" :step/content 17592186101551 :step/next "step-c8f38971-defd-50cd-9dce-38d71497ce0f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c8f38971-defd-50cd-9dce-38d71497ce0f" :step/id #uuid "c8f38971-defd-50cd-9dce-38d71497ce0f" :step/content 17592186253556 :step/next "step-4245bf2c-f044-5000-a8dc-9e8cf792d0e0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4245bf2c-f044-5000-a8dc-9e8cf792d0e0" :step/id #uuid "4245bf2c-f044-5000-a8dc-9e8cf792d0e0" :step/content 17592186253560 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092382 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-25f1b261-6dfa-5b14-a68c-dd89758e1b4c" "question-b262293b-c158-5061-ba4d-49a88b31e09c" "question-08550377-8abe-5e0e-9fca-2cd79ce06f39"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092383 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8bcad8aa-5196-5826-97b4-5ca478a3c38f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-25f1b261-6dfa-5b14-a68c-dd89758e1b4c" :question/id #uuid "25f1b261-6dfa-5b14-a68c-dd89758e1b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-342351" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}$ at the point  $(2,1).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\cdot 4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(2)$, which gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {2}^{3}=2.\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=2$ and the coordinates of our point $(2,1),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =2(x-2) \\\\ y & =2x-4+1 \\\\ y & =2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-db7afdf7-4a01-5e98-a4e2-00e845a3f731"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db7afdf7-4a01-5e98-a4e2-00e845a3f731" :answer-field/id #uuid "db7afdf7-4a01-5e98-a4e2-00e845a3f731" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e6ebadb2-b0d8-5d8c-881b-669e826c4db0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6ebadb2-b0d8-5d8c-881b-669e826c4db0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6ebadb2-b0d8-5d8c-881b-669e826c4db0" :answer/id #uuid "e6ebadb2-b0d8-5d8c-881b-669e826c4db0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b262293b-c158-5061-ba4d-49a88b31e09c" :question/id #uuid "b262293b-c158-5061-ba4d-49a88b31e09c" :question/math-academy-id "q-10838" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{2}+5x$ at the point $(-1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}+5x.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+5x)=2x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-1)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1)=2(-1)+5=3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=3$ and the coordinates of our point $(-1,-4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =3(x-(-1)) \\\\ y+4 & =3(x+1) \\\\ y & =3x+3-4 \\\\ y & =3x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48f4ddec-7d82-5ded-b4ef-c0b8b5d933e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48f4ddec-7d82-5ded-b4ef-c0b8b5d933e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "48f4ddec-7d82-5ded-b4ef-c0b8b5d933e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8cb0ab78-979b-5380-82ce-790749223f04" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ace4e43a-b577-57d5-86e7-009ce005e861" "answer-b03cce63-0693-5a60-aa91-ea45127e8ffe" "answer-c490d8c0-eb7b-5865-8a42-1e154ab559fa" "answer-8cb0ab78-979b-5380-82ce-790749223f04" "answer-54e4cc66-ca5e-5066-be7f-ca8af81230be"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ace4e43a-b577-57d5-86e7-009ce005e861" :answer/id #uuid "ace4e43a-b577-57d5-86e7-009ce005e861" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x+1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b03cce63-0693-5a60-aa91-ea45127e8ffe" :answer/id #uuid "b03cce63-0693-5a60-aa91-ea45127e8ffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=-3x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c490d8c0-eb7b-5865-8a42-1e154ab559fa" :answer/id #uuid "c490d8c0-eb7b-5865-8a42-1e154ab559fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=2x-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cb0ab78-979b-5380-82ce-790749223f04" :answer/id #uuid "8cb0ab78-979b-5380-82ce-790749223f04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54e4cc66-ca5e-5066-be7f-ca8af81230be" :answer/id #uuid "54e4cc66-ca5e-5066-be7f-ca8af81230be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=3x-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-08550377-8abe-5e0e-9fca-2cd79ce06f39" :question/id #uuid "08550377-8abe-5e0e-9fca-2cd79ce06f39" :question/math-academy-id "q-342365" :question/problem "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $y=-2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(4,-4).$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=-2\\sqrt{x}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(4)$, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{4}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the point-slope form with $m=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the coordinates of our point $(4,-4),$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-4) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-4) \\\\ y+4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2-4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-81cb03e6-1a48-5ebf-a4ee-b237e415735b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-81cb03e6-1a48-5ebf-a4ee-b237e415735b" :answer-field/id #uuid "81cb03e6-1a48-5ebf-a4ee-b237e415735b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2d6a12bc-5df9-5ffb-a1d6-d59cc5be5827" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d6a12bc-5df9-5ffb-a1d6-d59cc5be5827"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d6a12bc-5df9-5ffb-a1d6-d59cc5be5827" :answer/id #uuid "2d6a12bc-5df9-5ffb-a1d6-d59cc5be5827" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{x}{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092505 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{5}{x}-2x.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{x}-2x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{-1}-2x) \\\\  & =-5{x}^{-2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-5(1)^{-2}-2=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(3) & =-7(x-1) \\\\ y & =-7x+7+3 \\\\ y & =-7x+10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $x$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-7(0)+10 \\\\ y & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,10).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-8bcad8aa-5196-5826-97b4-5ca478a3c38f" :question/id #uuid "8bcad8aa-5196-5826-97b4-5ca478a3c38f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342378" :question/problem "Find where the tangent to the curve $y={x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ at the point  $(-1,-2)$ intersects the $y$-axis.\n\nThe tangent intersects the $y$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Let's first calculate the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}-{x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-(-2{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={f}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =3(-1)^{2}+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{3}} \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =1\\cdot [x-(-1)] \\\\ y+2 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x+1-2 \\\\ y & =x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intercepts the $y$-axis, we set $x=0$ and solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0-1 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(0,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4f19ea6a-73e1-5c5e-b2a8-31f8cd789cfa" "answer-field-f1dfc637-f59d-5645-b5ba-dba9a1d16ea2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4f19ea6a-73e1-5c5e-b2a8-31f8cd789cfa" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f19ea6a-73e1-5c5e-b2a8-31f8cd789cfa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-43d0b8c0-c0d0-53db-bf82-544dfd04bbd5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-43d0b8c0-c0d0-53db-bf82-544dfd04bbd5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-43d0b8c0-c0d0-53db-bf82-544dfd04bbd5" :answer/id #uuid "43d0b8c0-c0d0-53db-bf82-544dfd04bbd5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f1dfc637-f59d-5645-b5ba-dba9a1d16ea2" :answer-field/id #uuid "f1dfc637-f59d-5645-b5ba-dba9a1d16ea2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f860ae67-ca2e-5e42-9e11-aa9ed6864a39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f860ae67-ca2e-5e42-9e11-aa9ed6864a39"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f860ae67-ca2e-5e42-9e11-aa9ed6864a39" :answer/id #uuid "f860ae67-ca2e-5e42-9e11-aa9ed6864a39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092460 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092384 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ccfa4d84-7836-5556-9446-f447928d0c91" "question-e108591f-1dbb-58f4-aca2-6f29ab0389ab"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-77bfba8d-0add-517b-b669-b916e6dff897"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a153fbfd-b7f1-5770-9660-ea9e18ac215d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ccfa4d84-7836-5556-9446-f447928d0c91" :question/id #uuid "ccfa4d84-7836-5556-9446-f447928d0c91" :question/math-academy-id "q-103539" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}$ at the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}.$ Computing the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute ${f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}),$ which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{-3}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c700890-f7ec-5058-9334-5a60b70722d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c700890-f7ec-5058-9334-5a60b70722d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c700890-f7ec-5058-9334-5a60b70722d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8e9d1766-796c-5f7e-b81b-0f2e720704a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b6dcd76-9c75-5cab-a7e3-98ec9a075e7c" "answer-8e9d1766-796c-5f7e-b81b-0f2e720704a4" "answer-f09476ea-f87a-5f66-921f-9f3fefea5c34" "answer-3a4af37e-6b2f-57b0-a6f0-ff63aa93a160" "answer-fbd47810-d0f2-5cff-a8e5-e4945ca20335"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b6dcd76-9c75-5cab-a7e3-98ec9a075e7c" :answer/id #uuid "3b6dcd76-9c75-5cab-a7e3-98ec9a075e7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e9d1766-796c-5f7e-b81b-0f2e720704a4" :answer/id #uuid "8e9d1766-796c-5f7e-b81b-0f2e720704a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f09476ea-f87a-5f66-921f-9f3fefea5c34" :answer/id #uuid "f09476ea-f87a-5f66-921f-9f3fefea5c34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a4af37e-6b2f-57b0-a6f0-ff63aa93a160" :answer/id #uuid "3a4af37e-6b2f-57b0-a6f0-ff63aa93a160" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbd47810-d0f2-5cff-a8e5-e4945ca20335" :answer/id #uuid "fbd47810-d0f2-5cff-a8e5-e4945ca20335" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e108591f-1dbb-58f4-aca2-6f29ab0389ab" :question/id #uuid "e108591f-1dbb-58f4-aca2-6f29ab0389ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-71526" :question/problem "Find the slope of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-7x$ at the point $(-1,5).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-7x.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3}-7x)=6{x}^{2}-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(-1)=6(-1)^{2}-7=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-1)}=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fa8e2a76-bef8-546c-bf0c-5ea711bff307"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fa8e2a76-bef8-546c-bf0c-5ea711bff307" :answer-field/id #uuid "fa8e2a76-bef8-546c-bf0c-5ea711bff307" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7676dc7b-ce9f-5ee4-b0f0-1d0b1d1ad93a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d57dc05b-2779-515b-aecf-82edefe5f6a7" "answer-54c886c0-75b9-56f6-8629-ffc88d7f38eb" "answer-7676dc7b-ce9f-5ee4-b0f0-1d0b1d1ad93a" "answer-f442a990-1c89-5b31-9a80-2c9fda10deea" "answer-9992b9e3-55cb-534c-8fd0-21fbd64e8078"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d57dc05b-2779-515b-aecf-82edefe5f6a7" :answer/id #uuid "d57dc05b-2779-515b-aecf-82edefe5f6a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54c886c0-75b9-56f6-8629-ffc88d7f38eb" :answer/id #uuid "54c886c0-75b9-56f6-8629-ffc88d7f38eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7676dc7b-ce9f-5ee4-b0f0-1d0b1d1ad93a" :answer/id #uuid "7676dc7b-ce9f-5ee4-b0f0-1d0b1d1ad93a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f442a990-1c89-5b31-9a80-2c9fda10deea" :answer/id #uuid "f442a990-1c89-5b31-9a80-2c9fda10deea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9992b9e3-55cb-534c-8fd0-21fbd64e8078" :answer/id #uuid "9992b9e3-55cb-534c-8fd0-21fbd64e8078" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186233912 :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+1.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+1)=4x\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,9):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(2)=4(2)=8\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-72 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8\\cdot y & =74.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-77bfba8d-0add-517b-b669-b916e6dff897" :question/id #uuid "77bfba8d-0add-517b-b669-b916e6dff897" :question/math-academy-id "q-47412" :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,3)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p-q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{-3}) \\\\  & =-9{x}^{-4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{9}{(1)^{4}}=-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(3) & =\\frac{1}{9}(x-1) \\\\ 9y-27 & =x-1 \\\\ x-9y & =-26.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p-q=-9-(-26)=17.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6769aa7f-4e0f-5ed2-acb1-f447e8594699"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6769aa7f-4e0f-5ed2-acb1-f447e8594699" :answer-field/id #uuid "6769aa7f-4e0f-5ed2-acb1-f447e8594699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0df0f97f-4f39-5b1c-b10c-447e782fc12f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81b9bb21-5627-5934-b71d-4ea33aa8e42b" "answer-c53b0336-4b5b-5bc7-a56d-5493c7cb1d3b" "answer-0df0f97f-4f39-5b1c-b10c-447e782fc12f" "answer-6dae38db-68cf-5488-8070-c06480732642" "answer-f64ac374-af28-5718-ae03-405b4bfb5794"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81b9bb21-5627-5934-b71d-4ea33aa8e42b" :answer/id #uuid "81b9bb21-5627-5934-b71d-4ea33aa8e42b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c53b0336-4b5b-5bc7-a56d-5493c7cb1d3b" :answer/id #uuid "c53b0336-4b5b-5bc7-a56d-5493c7cb1d3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0df0f97f-4f39-5b1c-b10c-447e782fc12f" :answer/id #uuid "0df0f97f-4f39-5b1c-b10c-447e782fc12f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6dae38db-68cf-5488-8070-c06480732642" :answer/id #uuid "6dae38db-68cf-5488-8070-c06480732642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f64ac374-af28-5718-ae03-405b4bfb5794" :answer/id #uuid "f64ac374-af28-5718-ae03-405b4bfb5794" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a153fbfd-b7f1-5770-9660-ea9e18ac215d" :question/id #uuid "a153fbfd-b7f1-5770-9660-ea9e18ac215d" :question/math-academy-id "q-308821" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{3}$ at the point $(1,1)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=3(1)^{2}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-(1)) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,4,0\\,).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-49acc5c5-2120-5767-aa7d-12757fa05e62" "answer-field-6cb1979e-e5cb-53ff-86e6-e698dd51091e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-49acc5c5-2120-5767-aa7d-12757fa05e62" :answer-field/id #uuid "49acc5c5-2120-5767-aa7d-12757fa05e62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5bd5c750-5279-5330-b4bd-2cd5bbc27ec7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5bd5c750-5279-5330-b4bd-2cd5bbc27ec7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bd5c750-5279-5330-b4bd-2cd5bbc27ec7" :answer/id #uuid "5bd5c750-5279-5330-b4bd-2cd5bbc27ec7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6cb1979e-e5cb-53ff-86e6-e698dd51091e" :answer-field/id #uuid "6cb1979e-e5cb-53ff-86e6-e698dd51091e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0d4a964b-fc42-590a-ab02-6266c93e2655" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d4a964b-fc42-590a-ab02-6266c93e2655"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d4a964b-fc42-590a-ab02-6266c93e2655" :answer/id #uuid "0d4a964b-fc42-590a-ab02-6266c93e2655" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092461 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186174502 :answer-field/choices 17592186174503] [:db/retract 17592186174502 :answer-field/choices 17592186174505] [:db/retract 17592186174502 :answer-field/choices 17592186174507] [:db/retract 17592186174496 :answer-field/choices 17592186174499] [:db/retract 17592186174496 :answer-field/choices 17592186174500] [:db/retract 17592186174496 :answer-field/choices 17592186174501] {:db/id 17592186176006 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1c8173cc-49c9-5f6d-b758-9380f0f1613b" "question-4bec72aa-1d05-5e53-b2ca-0df4a3dfbe51" "question-c246e56e-54f4-5ef4-b7e3-0ed423a3b4da" "question-d405caca-881b-5a05-9419-094e813c3d9c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175859 :question/problem "![](images/46/46508c4856bb4a5086f7b21c3bf65758a0f9157179c661e63ae42944bd252cb9.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/9e/9eb73315957a9315c2ec10cbfa5e86c3f0bdd01617e8e68b3b1a8039b570b280.svg)\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right)$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/aa/aa4e587b8951f264d1f22392fd8fea7ae3397010376b82bbcd3ffab0d8dc50b8.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF\\left(2, \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = P\\left(X<                2, Y<                \\dfrac{5}{2}\\right) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{5/2} \\int_{-\\infty}^{2} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 2 \\leq y \\leq \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{5/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}\\sqrt{u}v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{5}{u}^{1/2}v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{[\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}v]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}{[\\frac{2}{5}{u}^{3/2}v]}_{u=0}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{5/2}\\frac{2}{5}v\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{5}]}_{2}^{5/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176007 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2ebdf296-e322-505e-9c4f-5149cacb6a7e" "question-e662cd14-0105-5064-96eb-7a2f86c8b78d" "question-419b7449-2db5-5f6c-9da4-4ea57433c3f1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175860 :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x, y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,5) = 0.9, \\quad\nF(5,2) = 0.3, \\quad\nF(0,5) = 0.45, \\quad\nF(0,2) = 0.15.\n$$\n\nFind $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 5, 2 \\leq Y \\leq 5).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/18/181df1b4d320fac30e8fc774975c8e1ae3fa6f4d360a49a38d23f1de96ebf7cc.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,5).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $\\color{red}(5,2)$ and ${\\color{red}{(0,5)}}.$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at ${\\color{blue}{(0,2)}}.$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 5,2\\le Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](5,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 2)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,5)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at [math](0,2)[/math]}}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 0,Y\\le 2)}} \\\\  & =F(5,5)-F(5,2)-F(0,5)+F(0,2) \\\\  & =0.9-0.3-0.45+0.15 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176036 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7ed10907-9632-53b1-9eec-d2c7cfd4e80a" "question-b7fd97ca-9b98-50c0-b683-53f1d4a2aa1d" "question-f06b7511-18eb-5f50-9aea-3636371eb6fb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175891 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/f7/f700fb9e7da84b3eb8d162847cada4ad69ae96b48da8b6e31f461272c19d0a17.svg)\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x > 1,\\; y > 4.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x > 1,\\; y >4,$ and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/0e/0e8ad9c26a96468741d20e886a3c18c56e248e529e90fa5908b0b472efd01e75.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta = D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $\\Delta$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176041 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e58401f9-a4c1-5465-9216-a1679c479a59" "question-b1b6ab32-2ee9-5286-82fb-e9633b19fc28" "question-e52f8c43-5915-56b1-841a-9a33ac8f7470" "question-61f58f19-0648-54c0-b55e-2caca7ea1507" "question-27951774-1d49-5cba-9914-8523d1f1e8cb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175688 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n![](images/ef/ef996369f785405f40f576aafc3b0ec3a5045a38ee36636908c3ea91f976d94f.svg) \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $y\\gt 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y) = P(X \\leq x, Y \\leq y) = \\int_{-\\infty}^y \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u \\, \\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0 \\leq x \\leq 2,\\;  y \\gt 1$ and a region $\\Delta$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a7/a7b43fd1a1622f406b7679d9f0f34552e046b9bc94b75a3504677f30b347d283.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $\\Delta$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD \\cap \\Delta =\\left\\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq u \\leq x, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 1 \\right\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(u+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+xv]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2}+\\frac{x{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}({x}^{2}+x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176276 :question/problem "![](images/c6/c64cfa701997c2b9e5e54f7eb77afb9927b878065af9c32fed86f09f140a4ec4.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/05/056fcbe25f83f488e1a88988d577535d0ef3776442f83e7d81560c6145f466a3.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{5})=P(X<\\frac{1}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{5})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{5}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/5}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}{\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(u+v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=0}^{u=1/2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/5}(\\frac{1}{8}+\\frac{v}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{v}{8}+\\frac{{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/5} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{40}+\\frac{1}{100}]-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{200}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173914 :answer-field/correct 17592186173917 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-1c8173cc-49c9-5f6d-b758-9380f0f1613b" :question/id #uuid "1c8173cc-49c9-5f6d-b758-9380f0f1613b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143618" :question/problem "![](images/48/483c019e9c68f857379192d2b40ab84803c4360ca4e0158adc0a8a3bc1636c4e.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{8}{9}xy, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/ff/ffba85fc6ae71a1a5c250a90d686e8852c77756eebd228f3d3a2a9622cba01e1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<\\frac{1}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be65f47b-87aa-5df5-8d18-766c3d2afc54"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be65f47b-87aa-5df5-8d18-766c3d2afc54" :answer-field/id #uuid "be65f47b-87aa-5df5-8d18-766c3d2afc54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-39315a2d-6ace-5432-91f2-e18c46b76e68" :answer-field/choices ["answer-39315a2d-6ace-5432-91f2-e18c46b76e68" "answer-0a5da53a-1db4-5289-abe3-b4086691e9a9" "answer-7ba9022d-dd18-5b7c-bc84-be4052ae1cfe" "answer-ab1c3cb0-0b20-5706-8e4d-e09a3517859d" "answer-798df700-363e-5cc5-8534-6ccc98b7f5df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39315a2d-6ace-5432-91f2-e18c46b76e68" :answer/id #uuid "39315a2d-6ace-5432-91f2-e18c46b76e68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a5da53a-1db4-5289-abe3-b4086691e9a9" :answer/id #uuid "0a5da53a-1db4-5289-abe3-b4086691e9a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ba9022d-dd18-5b7c-bc84-be4052ae1cfe" :answer/id #uuid "7ba9022d-dd18-5b7c-bc84-be4052ae1cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab1c3cb0-0b20-5706-8e4d-e09a3517859d" :answer/id #uuid "ab1c3cb0-0b20-5706-8e4d-e09a3517859d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-798df700-363e-5cc5-8534-6ccc98b7f5df" :answer/id #uuid "798df700-363e-5cc5-8534-6ccc98b7f5df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4bec72aa-1d05-5e53-b2ca-0df4a3dfbe51" :question/id #uuid "4bec72aa-1d05-5e53-b2ca-0df4a3dfbe51" :question/math-academy-id "q-143739" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/a5/a5f876c9351040845fcc5e6839f4ca776fe22988a5e4c814d4a7b063692f083c.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(0,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<0,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=0}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}(0+\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-71eb090d-6e48-50da-bb66-1c6f0856fb96"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-71eb090d-6e48-50da-bb66-1c6f0856fb96" :answer-field/id #uuid "71eb090d-6e48-50da-bb66-1c6f0856fb96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e718edfc-229b-5e95-b802-2ff85ca21e24" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e718edfc-229b-5e95-b802-2ff85ca21e24" "answer-7ea56258-291c-5f2c-9c35-b323b146d447" "answer-a61dcb51-4a14-5f5f-82c4-141f3d9ad22f" "answer-03ba8c61-c212-52ee-888a-00441515fd5a" "answer-cfa696f6-6f20-540d-a235-7ceafd2611d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e718edfc-229b-5e95-b802-2ff85ca21e24" :answer/id #uuid "e718edfc-229b-5e95-b802-2ff85ca21e24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ea56258-291c-5f2c-9c35-b323b146d447" :answer/id #uuid "7ea56258-291c-5f2c-9c35-b323b146d447" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a61dcb51-4a14-5f5f-82c4-141f3d9ad22f" :answer/id #uuid "a61dcb51-4a14-5f5f-82c4-141f3d9ad22f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03ba8c61-c212-52ee-888a-00441515fd5a" :answer/id #uuid "03ba8c61-c212-52ee-888a-00441515fd5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfa696f6-6f20-540d-a235-7ceafd2611d0" :answer/id #uuid "cfa696f6-6f20-540d-a235-7ceafd2611d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c246e56e-54f4-5ef4-b7e3-0ed423a3b4da" :question/id #uuid "c246e56e-54f4-5ef4-b7e3-0ed423a3b4da" :question/math-academy-id "q-143742" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/53/53d0466b98c4b9b2704cc2f7610e7f28cee6ec27aaf2ea94be75c5b3f32a8257.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(1,\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<1,Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{1}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\frac{9}{2}{u}^{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}{[\\frac{3}{2}{u}^{3}{v}^{2}]}_{u=-1}^{u=1}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}[\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2}-(-\\frac{3}{2}{v}^{2})]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}3{v}^{2}\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[{v}^{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0348f5f0-90e0-585b-8e6d-413d4121229b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0348f5f0-90e0-585b-8e6d-413d4121229b" :answer-field/id #uuid "0348f5f0-90e0-585b-8e6d-413d4121229b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9a77a2f3-aba1-542a-a9a0-676775a31c88" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b254c28d-2f4a-5f0f-92fd-f787e8b75821" "answer-48826592-f39f-5544-b033-af627b3ec9d2" "answer-cd9a463b-d985-5b34-bc90-9079f8752c87" "answer-02f1848b-2067-5ef4-a435-25645f66885f" "answer-9a77a2f3-aba1-542a-a9a0-676775a31c88"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b254c28d-2f4a-5f0f-92fd-f787e8b75821" :answer/id #uuid "b254c28d-2f4a-5f0f-92fd-f787e8b75821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-48826592-f39f-5544-b033-af627b3ec9d2" :answer/id #uuid "48826592-f39f-5544-b033-af627b3ec9d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd9a463b-d985-5b34-bc90-9079f8752c87" :answer/id #uuid "cd9a463b-d985-5b34-bc90-9079f8752c87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-02f1848b-2067-5ef4-a435-25645f66885f" :answer/id #uuid "02f1848b-2067-5ef4-a435-25645f66885f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a77a2f3-aba1-542a-a9a0-676775a31c88" :answer/id #uuid "9a77a2f3-aba1-542a-a9a0-676775a31c88" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d405caca-881b-5a05-9419-094e813c3d9c" :question/id #uuid "d405caca-881b-5a05-9419-094e813c3d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-143740" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb5f10ce887ba9d167b2753618064fcdbf8d1f42371d733aacd56a8d8cb5e667.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the point $(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$ on our diagram. Let's also consider the region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/dd/dd7024bcd41ce8312f484763e0988ebcceb54e7154cd8ee3c209991bce5727c1.png)\n\nUsing the definition of the joint CDF, we have\n\n$$\nF(-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2})=P(X<-\\frac{3}{2},Y<\\frac{1}{2})={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nHowever, $f(u,v)$ is zero everywhere inside the region $Δ.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1/2}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3/2}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8afeb2a5-b592-5221-9a34-afffd9137833"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8afeb2a5-b592-5221-9a34-afffd9137833" :answer-field/id #uuid "8afeb2a5-b592-5221-9a34-afffd9137833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e2b551eb-8620-5f76-bb82-a5c648a8788e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2b551eb-8620-5f76-bb82-a5c648a8788e" "answer-caccaafe-7667-5ccc-8d3c-ece95431f0c1" "answer-39d570d1-ff8c-5514-a7e3-6aca29e5688a" "answer-23310bfc-17e6-5580-a55e-e246c24629fb" "answer-f803b7aa-c839-5645-a503-8f8949428530"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2b551eb-8620-5f76-bb82-a5c648a8788e" :answer/id #uuid "e2b551eb-8620-5f76-bb82-a5c648a8788e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caccaafe-7667-5ccc-8d3c-ece95431f0c1" :answer/id #uuid "caccaafe-7667-5ccc-8d3c-ece95431f0c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39d570d1-ff8c-5514-a7e3-6aca29e5688a" :answer/id #uuid "39d570d1-ff8c-5514-a7e3-6aca29e5688a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23310bfc-17e6-5580-a55e-e246c24629fb" :answer/id #uuid "23310bfc-17e6-5580-a55e-e246c24629fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f803b7aa-c839-5645-a503-8f8949428530" :answer/id #uuid "f803b7aa-c839-5645-a503-8f8949428530" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ebdf296-e322-505e-9c4f-5149cacb6a7e" :question/id #uuid "2ebdf296-e322-505e-9c4f-5149cacb6a7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-143707" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(5,4)=1,\\,F(5,1)=\\frac{2}{9},\\,F(4,4)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,F(4,1)=\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/13/137e3d2e3344f5d1f57f36d470564a518c359bb46d5278382b7242e73999e80e.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(5,4).$\n\n2\\. Subtract the infinite rectangles that have their top-right corners at $(5,1)$ and $(4,4).$\n\n3\\. Finally, we need to *add back* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(4,1).$ This is because we subtracted this region twice in step 2.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(4\\le X\\le 5,1\\le Y\\le 4) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(5,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 5,Y\\le 1)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & \\,+\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(4,1)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 4,Y\\le 1)}} \\\\  & =F(5,4)-F(5,1)-F(4,4)+F(4,1) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{9}-\\frac{2}{9}-\\frac{3}{9}+\\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3cf65c66-4c8d-5602-8e69-2970d52a51bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cf65c66-4c8d-5602-8e69-2970d52a51bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf65c66-4c8d-5602-8e69-2970d52a51bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1d8baf0c-cd7e-581a-93c7-c3900b854b6e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe36d242-dab0-5d15-a25f-23aecf853bd6" "answer-62c8319f-eff2-571e-bcb6-00ff9af3442a" "answer-ed965288-4b09-507b-9790-a2fd183c8104" "answer-61c6ad87-fa8a-5d9c-8da2-1164e2fede9f" "answer-1d8baf0c-cd7e-581a-93c7-c3900b854b6e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe36d242-dab0-5d15-a25f-23aecf853bd6" :answer/id #uuid "fe36d242-dab0-5d15-a25f-23aecf853bd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62c8319f-eff2-571e-bcb6-00ff9af3442a" :answer/id #uuid "62c8319f-eff2-571e-bcb6-00ff9af3442a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed965288-4b09-507b-9790-a2fd183c8104" :answer/id #uuid "ed965288-4b09-507b-9790-a2fd183c8104" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61c6ad87-fa8a-5d9c-8da2-1164e2fede9f" :answer/id #uuid "61c6ad87-fa8a-5d9c-8da2-1164e2fede9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d8baf0c-cd7e-581a-93c7-c3900b854b6e" :answer/id #uuid "1d8baf0c-cd7e-581a-93c7-c3900b854b6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e662cd14-0105-5064-96eb-7a2f86c8b78d" :question/id #uuid "e662cd14-0105-5064-96eb-7a2f86c8b78d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143701" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(8,10)=\\frac{9}{10},\\,F(8,8)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,F(8,4)=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/d3/d31cdb5e3f201d9dd5b3b622d883f67bb2c8bc0a5036905695a5cc5ceb3bda07.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,8)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(8,4)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/62/626d5c4cba7b41d6b6f2da77ca1d62c5bcd442990ce31cf630e69882dd6580b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8,4\\le Y\\le 8) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,8)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 8)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(8,4)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 8,Y\\le 4)}} \\\\  & =F(8,8)-F(8,4) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3aeeeffd-771c-5e22-8635-b25572ba714b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3aeeeffd-771c-5e22-8635-b25572ba714b" :answer-field/id #uuid "3aeeeffd-771c-5e22-8635-b25572ba714b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5d43c59-6f14-5ddc-adb1-7ca44d5b7cc1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-537999eb-f702-5e5a-b808-1d5e4897e7d4" "answer-e5d43c59-6f14-5ddc-adb1-7ca44d5b7cc1" "answer-8d670cde-225e-5b9b-8c8d-2fc3c8a36857" "answer-880d5f55-fa39-5e77-a30f-7b1bc2813874" "answer-c25461e4-3b0a-58dd-8b88-4a3086a23bca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-537999eb-f702-5e5a-b808-1d5e4897e7d4" :answer/id #uuid "537999eb-f702-5e5a-b808-1d5e4897e7d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5d43c59-6f14-5ddc-adb1-7ca44d5b7cc1" :answer/id #uuid "e5d43c59-6f14-5ddc-adb1-7ca44d5b7cc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d670cde-225e-5b9b-8c8d-2fc3c8a36857" :answer/id #uuid "8d670cde-225e-5b9b-8c8d-2fc3c8a36857" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-880d5f55-fa39-5e77-a30f-7b1bc2813874" :answer/id #uuid "880d5f55-fa39-5e77-a30f-7b1bc2813874" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c25461e4-3b0a-58dd-8b88-4a3086a23bca" :answer/id #uuid "c25461e4-3b0a-58dd-8b88-4a3086a23bca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{25}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-419b7449-2db5-5f6c-9da4-4ea57433c3f1" :question/id #uuid "419b7449-2db5-5f6c-9da4-4ea57433c3f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-143703" :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(1,5)=0.63,\\,F(0,5)=0.5,\\,F(-2,5)=0.27.\n$$\n\nFind $P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/03/0378964a9f167554e4faeba228ebae485de94dda83fbd6704a864a06b8abe77a.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(1,5)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(-2,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/90/9079baa6464df773cacb820a6bb4a8e2b8f4ecc19755619a9b5c191a0c48507f.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-2\\le X\\le 1,Y\\le 5) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(1,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 5)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(-2,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le -2,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(1,5)-F(-2,5) \\\\  & =0.63-0.27 \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2e041ab-9982-589c-bc56-123e99e4c001"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2e041ab-9982-589c-bc56-123e99e4c001" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2e041ab-9982-589c-bc56-123e99e4c001" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84f8b6ae-7ca4-5a99-bf55-083348f055a5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a89e5a41-027a-5495-9e5b-b017f651256b" "answer-6c4eb767-1066-5a82-b1b3-22a8a24eafab" "answer-84f8b6ae-7ca4-5a99-bf55-083348f055a5" "answer-297d7533-8470-571e-ba87-7ac9cbd8c6b3" "answer-1220b7d0-ad4f-50da-a224-1d43a9374c98"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a89e5a41-027a-5495-9e5b-b017f651256b" :answer/id #uuid "a89e5a41-027a-5495-9e5b-b017f651256b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c4eb767-1066-5a82-b1b3-22a8a24eafab" :answer/id #uuid "6c4eb767-1066-5a82-b1b3-22a8a24eafab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84f8b6ae-7ca4-5a99-bf55-083348f055a5" :answer/id #uuid "84f8b6ae-7ca4-5a99-bf55-083348f055a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-297d7533-8470-571e-ba87-7ac9cbd8c6b3" :answer/id #uuid "297d7533-8470-571e-ba87-7ac9cbd8c6b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.77" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1220b7d0-ad4f-50da-a224-1d43a9374c98" :answer/id #uuid "1220b7d0-ad4f-50da-a224-1d43a9374c98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176357 :question/problem "Let the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y$ be $F(x,y),$ where\n\n$$\nF(10,9)=0.7,\\,F(9,5)=0.4,\\,F(10,5)=0.2.\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 10,5\\le Y\\le 9).$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $F(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)$ is the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\nLet's sketch the region corresponding to the probability we wish to find.\n\n![](images/a3/a3f2d39dd5b14494c726d5bc758fb9d89c5cdc18831fc900cae68da4cf5b6dcf.png)\n\nGeometrically, this region can be obtained by taking the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,9)$ (the blue region below) and *subtracting* the infinite rectangle that has its top-right corner at $(10,5)$ (the red region below).\n\n![](images/fa/fa3c9972c42d877a17609ead70776226ab4effccc991ecece596f542efea8cad.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 10,5\\le Y\\le 9) & =\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,9)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 9)}}-\\overset{\\text{top-right corner at }(10,5)}{\\overset{⏞}{P(X\\le 10,Y\\le 5)}} \\\\  & =F(10,9)-F(10,5) \\\\  & =0.7-0.2 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174400 :answer-field/correct 17592186174404 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176374 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,1<y<3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0,\\,1<y<3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/30/3035c8fb56868a761b7c57d0f86077b796fe573ac289726414bc5b75ea91ab58.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174502 :answer-field/correct 17592186174504 :answer-field/choices ["answer-a291d4a2-1c32-59ce-a889-698e12ba88cd" "answer-f2954d4e-2ef2-52c9-a341-a49445314421" "answer-906a169c-2edf-5f14-a67e-d3e7a3dc6d4d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a291d4a2-1c32-59ce-a889-698e12ba88cd" :answer/id #uuid "a291d4a2-1c32-59ce-a889-698e12ba88cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2954d4e-2ef2-52c9-a341-a49445314421" :answer/id #uuid "f2954d4e-2ef2-52c9-a341-a49445314421" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-906a169c-2edf-5f14-a67e-d3e7a3dc6d4d" :answer/id #uuid "906a169c-2edf-5f14-a67e-d3e7a3dc6d4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7ed10907-9632-53b1-9eec-d2c7cfd4e80a" :question/id #uuid "7ed10907-9632-53b1-9eec-d2c7cfd4e80a" :question/math-academy-id "q-143657" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x<0,\\,y>\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/4f/4f5cfe5e819f9479199ba1af6116627b31d03c88d8aa4c6e049bb375316c2c10.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3e14d43-5876-53c9-8df1-5009e3c0d56b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3e14d43-5876-53c9-8df1-5009e3c0d56b" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3e14d43-5876-53c9-8df1-5009e3c0d56b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-546f9ed9-007b-5e13-b2e5-6decca60ae2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-546f9ed9-007b-5e13-b2e5-6decca60ae2d" "answer-f7dc7e2b-3b42-543d-837d-5072c514a774" "answer-8679b652-02ec-5390-bd6e-d39302137cb1" "answer-69fa510b-bc1f-55f2-8bbc-4c5cbb321279" "answer-63533c4d-d24b-59fa-ba96-39e3f6c1e18b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-546f9ed9-007b-5e13-b2e5-6decca60ae2d" :answer/id #uuid "546f9ed9-007b-5e13-b2e5-6decca60ae2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7dc7e2b-3b42-543d-837d-5072c514a774" :answer/id #uuid "f7dc7e2b-3b42-543d-837d-5072c514a774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8679b652-02ec-5390-bd6e-d39302137cb1" :answer/id #uuid "8679b652-02ec-5390-bd6e-d39302137cb1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69fa510b-bc1f-55f2-8bbc-4c5cbb321279" :answer/id #uuid "69fa510b-bc1f-55f2-8bbc-4c5cbb321279" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63533c4d-d24b-59fa-ba96-39e3f6c1e18b" :answer/id #uuid "63533c4d-d24b-59fa-ba96-39e3f6c1e18b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b7fd97ca-9b98-50c0-b683-53f1d4a2aa1d" :question/id #uuid "b7fd97ca-9b98-50c0-b683-53f1d4a2aa1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143656" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y<0.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1,\\,y<0,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/1232f75c56b548896fc3c0371e36201eb24c71811d59d185440a964e393818be.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=∅.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo there is nowhere in our region $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{Δ}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5b6198ea-775e-5856-8cd6-6fdd6ff63947"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5b6198ea-775e-5856-8cd6-6fdd6ff63947" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b6198ea-775e-5856-8cd6-6fdd6ff63947" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0b19ecfd-a627-5697-a910-45b2a219ffd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0b19ecfd-a627-5697-a910-45b2a219ffd0" "answer-52b58bdc-da06-5743-a21c-ed03d5de980f" "answer-8f0ad379-42d9-5008-b164-1f7676c50f92" "answer-f64a556c-0af0-5d31-a438-8927146cd5fd" "answer-690a125b-4b50-52a3-a7c4-98a2461af416"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b19ecfd-a627-5697-a910-45b2a219ffd0" :answer/id #uuid "0b19ecfd-a627-5697-a910-45b2a219ffd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52b58bdc-da06-5743-a21c-ed03d5de980f" :answer/id #uuid "52b58bdc-da06-5743-a21c-ed03d5de980f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f0ad379-42d9-5008-b164-1f7676c50f92" :answer/id #uuid "8f0ad379-42d9-5008-b164-1f7676c50f92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f64a556c-0af0-5d31-a438-8927146cd5fd" :answer/id #uuid "f64a556c-0af0-5d31-a438-8927146cd5fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{30}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-690a125b-4b50-52a3-a7c4-98a2461af416" :answer/id #uuid "690a125b-4b50-52a3-a7c4-98a2461af416" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(x+y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f06b7511-18eb-5f50-9aea-3636371eb6fb" :question/id #uuid "f06b7511-18eb-5f50-9aea-3636371eb6fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143658" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}, & -1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>2,\\,y>\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/19/19510c5eb927edbc93ff077f13e645820548e9074da0e1101d9ef641650642bb.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e1873516-1ea7-5025-ba34-62e81accf8f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e1873516-1ea7-5025-ba34-62e81accf8f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1873516-1ea7-5025-ba34-62e81accf8f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3cf84118-2059-5f36-ba3c-60ecf7e48225" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dec1957e-5a0e-5556-92fc-2e137c12ed35" "answer-3cf84118-2059-5f36-ba3c-60ecf7e48225" "answer-b8a87f55-8422-523f-8f58-b46dfbaf6a1c" "answer-071c8f49-d804-5f98-8a95-def2b9471668" "answer-94a42c42-afe5-541a-ba5c-8988133fd11f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dec1957e-5a0e-5556-92fc-2e137c12ed35" :answer/id #uuid "dec1957e-5a0e-5556-92fc-2e137c12ed35" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cf84118-2059-5f36-ba3c-60ecf7e48225" :answer/id #uuid "3cf84118-2059-5f36-ba3c-60ecf7e48225" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8a87f55-8422-523f-8f58-b46dfbaf6a1c" :answer/id #uuid "b8a87f55-8422-523f-8f58-b46dfbaf6a1c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x{e}^{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-071c8f49-d804-5f98-8a95-def2b9471668" :answer/id #uuid "071c8f49-d804-5f98-8a95-def2b9471668" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{e}^{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94a42c42-afe5-541a-ba5c-8988133fd11f" :answer/id #uuid "94a42c42-afe5-541a-ba5c-8988133fd11f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{x}^{2}{e}^{y}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176373 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>1,\\,y>3.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>1,\\,y>3,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/67/677dee4fb11cc609f089c8660ee16da5f9ac7572b27c9b0a3e97818e870b8db9.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=D.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo our region $Δ$ covers the entire region where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={∬}_{D}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174496 :answer-field/correct 17592186174498 :answer-field/choices ["answer-d12e9958-4ff9-5e58-90db-4cd906dea6d2" "answer-3255e560-bc47-508d-8647-48857909d6ab" "answer-747225ec-e6bd-529a-b8f8-19e90acd91dc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d12e9958-4ff9-5e58-90db-4cd906dea6d2" :answer/id #uuid "d12e9958-4ff9-5e58-90db-4cd906dea6d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3255e560-bc47-508d-8647-48857909d6ab" :answer/id #uuid "3255e560-bc47-508d-8647-48857909d6ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-747225ec-e6bd-529a-b8f8-19e90acd91dc" :answer/id #uuid "747225ec-e6bd-529a-b8f8-19e90acd91dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}{x}^{3}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e58401f9-a4c1-5465-9216-a1679c479a59" :question/id #uuid "e58401f9-a4c1-5465-9216-a1679c479a59" :question/math-academy-id "q-143695" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12}x{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/e4/e4aef49bebaf250fa93e0ad1daccbad324bf714c7e5627f5245ea8387bc8da87.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{12}u{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}u\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{12}{v}^{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{y}{v}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{[\\frac{{v}^{3}}{3}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e7581f6-409c-526a-8e01-7cfa799ca69b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e7581f6-409c-526a-8e01-7cfa799ca69b" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e7581f6-409c-526a-8e01-7cfa799ca69b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-29c0dbe5-4efd-5b3d-8117-33fcf80cf8ca" :answer-field/choices ["answer-415fdd0e-bb48-5e8c-82e5-fb1aeaf72830" "answer-ecdc4d77-589b-57d8-8d92-72fa69c4b672" "answer-209bfb2e-af2f-5d6a-840c-05fd1de20ca5" "answer-29c0dbe5-4efd-5b3d-8117-33fcf80cf8ca" "answer-ed1ed0e2-8bf5-5e15-a29a-2e70d2fbbef1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-415fdd0e-bb48-5e8c-82e5-fb1aeaf72830" :answer/id #uuid "415fdd0e-bb48-5e8c-82e5-fb1aeaf72830" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}{x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecdc4d77-589b-57d8-8d92-72fa69c4b672" :answer/id #uuid "ecdc4d77-589b-57d8-8d92-72fa69c4b672" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}xy" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-209bfb2e-af2f-5d6a-840c-05fd1de20ca5" :answer/id #uuid "209bfb2e-af2f-5d6a-840c-05fd1de20ca5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29c0dbe5-4efd-5b3d-8117-33fcf80cf8ca" :answer/id #uuid "29c0dbe5-4efd-5b3d-8117-33fcf80cf8ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{72}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed1ed0e2-8bf5-5e15-a29a-2e70d2fbbef1" :answer/id #uuid "ed1ed0e2-8bf5-5e15-a29a-2e70d2fbbef1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}{x}^{2}y" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1b6ab32-2ee9-5286-82fb-e9633b19fc28" :question/id #uuid "b1b6ab32-2ee9-5286-82fb-e9633b19fc28" :question/math-academy-id "q-143693" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}({y}^{2}+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfa6842026fc1635efe939525d60a9c83c89735a1a1e69d32d1b31c8431f02c.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}{[\\frac{3}{5}({v}^{2}u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2})]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{y}\\frac{3}{5}(x{v}^{2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{[\\frac{x{v}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-10efcb21-a5fa-5adc-bb8a-dc7d1ccdc194"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-10efcb21-a5fa-5adc-bb8a-dc7d1ccdc194" :answer-field/id #uuid "10efcb21-a5fa-5adc-bb8a-dc7d1ccdc194" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3b0afbaa-d117-5fda-b304-a0c098057560" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14c8cd64-1d0a-54bd-9709-4c36bc06ddae" "answer-3b0afbaa-d117-5fda-b304-a0c098057560" "answer-b5e4bb67-f0ed-58a6-b574-7f6f135ea50b" "answer-1095f72c-8905-575a-b1e8-c4f151588556" "answer-2494f35b-fb23-5fe3-a9a9-ec37d5670ef4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14c8cd64-1d0a-54bd-9709-4c36bc06ddae" :answer/id #uuid "14c8cd64-1d0a-54bd-9709-4c36bc06ddae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{xy}{4}+x{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b0afbaa-d117-5fda-b304-a0c098057560" :answer/id #uuid "3b0afbaa-d117-5fda-b304-a0c098057560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{4})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5e4bb67-f0ed-58a6-b574-7f6f135ea50b" :answer/id #uuid "b5e4bb67-f0ed-58a6-b574-7f6f135ea50b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1095f72c-8905-575a-b1e8-c4f151588556" :answer/id #uuid "1095f72c-8905-575a-b1e8-c4f151588556" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}(\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}+x{y}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2494f35b-fb23-5fe3-a9a9-ec37d5670ef4" :answer/id #uuid "2494f35b-fb23-5fe3-a9a9-ec37d5670ef4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}+x{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e52f8c43-5915-56b1-841a-9a33ac8f7470" :question/id #uuid "e52f8c43-5915-56b1-841a-9a33ac8f7470" :question/math-academy-id "q-143716" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{2}+y, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>1$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/12/12e26559137d7c494c5ddc6f1add55f646dd84236f355221042448ddeacd2879.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{x}u{v}^{2}+v\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+uv]}_{u=1}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{2}+xv-\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}-v]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\frac{{v}^{2}({x}^{2}-1)}{2}+v(x-1)]\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{v}^{3}({x}^{2}-1)}{6}+\\frac{{v}^{2}(x-1)}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{6}+\\frac{x-1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-93696133-c36c-56da-ad2a-2f1497fe6ebb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-93696133-c36c-56da-ad2a-2f1497fe6ebb" :answer-field/id #uuid "93696133-c36c-56da-ad2a-2f1497fe6ebb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-271ba238-8f69-525e-bb35-98eee22b398a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-65557329-98b5-5b38-93db-0154d8a2d280" "answer-bec80840-8b96-572a-96e8-64bd7c631522" "answer-2fb1b36a-bb03-5898-8c00-7b52f0a1cf6f" "answer-271ba238-8f69-525e-bb35-98eee22b398a" "answer-4c52e4cf-f222-5624-9b6b-2a72692caecb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65557329-98b5-5b38-93db-0154d8a2d280" :answer/id #uuid "65557329-98b5-5b38-93db-0154d8a2d280" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bec80840-8b96-572a-96e8-64bd7c631522" :answer/id #uuid "bec80840-8b96-572a-96e8-64bd7c631522" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fb1b36a-bb03-5898-8c00-7b52f0a1cf6f" :answer/id #uuid "2fb1b36a-bb03-5898-8c00-7b52f0a1cf6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-271ba238-8f69-525e-bb35-98eee22b398a" :answer/id #uuid "271ba238-8f69-525e-bb35-98eee22b398a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c52e4cf-f222-5624-9b6b-2a72692caecb" :answer/id #uuid "4c52e4cf-f222-5624-9b6b-2a72692caecb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{2}+x-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-61f58f19-0648-54c0-b55e-2caca7ea1507" :question/id #uuid "61f58f19-0648-54c0-b55e-2caca7ea1507" :question/math-academy-id "q-143712" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}, & 1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>3$ and $1\\le y\\le 2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $x>3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/1f/1fbbe9e4728cd5aefcc456a6c8666e3e2e6c0d0bc832f471677d908cf6c012e3.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le y\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{v}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v{[-\\frac{1}{u}]}_{u=1}^{u=3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}v(1-\\frac{1}{3})\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{y}\\frac{2}{3}v\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=1}^{v=y} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-845d7c96-a14e-530a-9ba3-0bbcb5ec1e7c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-845d7c96-a14e-530a-9ba3-0bbcb5ec1e7c" :answer-field/id #uuid "845d7c96-a14e-530a-9ba3-0bbcb5ec1e7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-16b612e0-50c4-52a8-8946-ce06bbf20ed4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c924db7-4b2c-5c7b-b0e5-9e7980807592" "answer-16b612e0-50c4-52a8-8946-ce06bbf20ed4" "answer-56b42f84-5509-5bcb-9980-f9dbb75f1963" "answer-da9bdc95-8e4f-54c4-8a48-14f10b9adc06" "answer-bb0b9f91-9543-5641-bb77-95c2a131753a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c924db7-4b2c-5c7b-b0e5-9e7980807592" :answer/id #uuid "2c924db7-4b2c-5c7b-b0e5-9e7980807592" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-16b612e0-50c4-52a8-8946-ce06bbf20ed4" :answer/id #uuid "16b612e0-50c4-52a8-8946-ce06bbf20ed4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({y}^{2}-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56b42f84-5509-5bcb-9980-f9dbb75f1963" :answer/id #uuid "56b42f84-5509-5bcb-9980-f9dbb75f1963" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-da9bdc95-8e4f-54c4-8a48-14f10b9adc06" :answer/id #uuid "da9bdc95-8e4f-54c4-8a48-14f10b9adc06" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}(y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb0b9f91-9543-5641-bb77-95c2a131753a" :answer/id #uuid "bb0b9f91-9543-5641-bb77-95c2a131753a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}({y}^{2}+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-27951774-1d49-5cba-9914-8523d1f1e8cb" :question/id #uuid "27951774-1d49-5cba-9914-8523d1f1e8cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-143717" :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy), & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2.$" :question/worked-solution "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the corresponding joint CDF is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X\\le x,Y\\le y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v.\n$$\n\nLet's draw the region $D$ in the $uv$-plane where the function $f(u,v)$ is nonzero. Let's also consider a point $(x,y)$ where $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $y>2$ and a region $Δ$ that lies below and to the left of this point.\n\n![](images/f5/f5ceaa24d74de28cc24de05ab352fbe4586c4c83630d6163f4871ccbe74e2cd2.png)\n\nFor the purposes of computation, we're only interested in the region inside $Δ$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero. This is given by\n\n$$\nD\\cap Δ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le x,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{y}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(u,v)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{3}(1+uv)\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{[u+\\frac{{u}^{2}v}{2}]}_{u=0}^{u=x}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+\\frac{{x}^{2}v}{2})\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[xv+\\frac{{x}^{2}{v}^{2}}{4}]}_{v=0}^{v=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+{x}^{2}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+2x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-de7929f6-25fe-54ba-932a-cb75244104ed"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-de7929f6-25fe-54ba-932a-cb75244104ed" :answer-field/id #uuid "de7929f6-25fe-54ba-932a-cb75244104ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dcb81a74-72a1-5f19-bc38-b39dca6a150f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1aaefb20-e0f8-53e5-a3f4-1888bb386733" "answer-831f8dad-5a3f-5eed-a9cb-51007d1c3429" "answer-7bcc536f-5146-5eb2-9bfc-3649d9de4a20" "answer-dcb81a74-72a1-5f19-bc38-b39dca6a150f" "answer-f71f5628-605d-5c10-95cc-aabea9c6e8e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aaefb20-e0f8-53e5-a3f4-1888bb386733" :answer/id #uuid "1aaefb20-e0f8-53e5-a3f4-1888bb386733" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-831f8dad-5a3f-5eed-a9cb-51007d1c3429" :answer/id #uuid "831f8dad-5a3f-5eed-a9cb-51007d1c3429" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bcc536f-5146-5eb2-9bfc-3649d9de4a20" :answer/id #uuid "7bcc536f-5146-5eb2-9bfc-3649d9de4a20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(2{x}^{2}+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcb81a74-72a1-5f19-bc38-b39dca6a150f" :answer/id #uuid "dcb81a74-72a1-5f19-bc38-b39dca6a150f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+2x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f71f5628-605d-5c10-95cc-aabea9c6e8e1" :answer/id #uuid "f71f5628-605d-5c10-95cc-aabea9c6e8e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}-x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186177010 :tutorial/content "The **joint cumulative distribution function** (or joint CDF) of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(X \\leq x,Y \\leq y).\n$$\n\nThe joint CDF gives the probability that the pair $(X,Y)$ will lie inside the infinite rectangular region $\\Delta$ that has its top-right corner at $(x,y)$ and whose sides are parallel to the coordinate axes.\n\n![](images/ee/ee82c6b810f02f8b8380bd97c94cbe491095d17dbadc685ffdca1a54cf9828b8.png)\n\nThe joint CDF satisfies the following properties:\n\n- $0\\leq F(x,y)\\leq 1$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $X$ is given by $F_X(x)=F(x,\\infty).$\n\n- The **marginal CDF** of $Y$ is given by $F_Y(y)=F(\\infty,y).$\n\n- $F(\\infty, \\infty)=1$\n\n- $F(-\\infty ,y)=F(x,-\\infty)=0$\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables, then \n$F(x,y)=F_X(x)\\cdot F_Y(y).$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x, y)$, we have the following relationship between the joint PDF and joint CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^y_{-\\infty}\\int^x_{-\\infty} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v.\n$$\n\nConsequently, if $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables that are nonzero only over the rectangle $[a, b] \\times [c, d]$, then the joint CDF is \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\int^d_{c}\\int^b_{a} f(u,v)\\; \\textrm{d}u\\textrm{d}v.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176157 :activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186063486 :answer-field/choices 17592186063488] [:db/retract 17592186063486 :answer-field/choices 17592186063489] [:db/retract 17592186063486 :answer-field/choices 17592186063490] [:db/retract 17592186063486 :answer-field/choices 17592186063491] [:db/retract 17592186063486 :answer-field/choices 17592186063487] {:db/id 17592186064385 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7145361b-50f5-5e70-a538-4868d130565b" "question-f624216d-33b0-5bec-b567-6dca0950cf8b" "question-e817aceb-adb1-5216-bfe1-e48f45d3b767" "question-c70a238b-9801-5892-a5eb-17f62e1925f8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064029 :question/problem "Maria is flying a kite $K$ at an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ to the horizontal. The kite's shadow $S$ is projected onto the ground vertically below the kite. If the kite is moving towards Maria with a speed of $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ what is the speed of the kite's shadow?\n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\n![](images/1a/1a00ce10db2d8a0fa292bd3ca67744e7cbaafcf1861cbbdc049832d287926d28.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/69/69402f16f81a6653e1fdd313c314ac72db7f5514501af1ac147b5e499b305233.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between María and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between Maria and the kite.\n\nWe want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm dt} = -5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}$ (negative because the distance between Maria and the kite is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm dt} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot (-5) = -2.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $2.5\\,\\textrm{ft/s}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064386 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e9479f5b-c4df-5efe-97d6-27688a7060c1" "question-5a9f47ed-553f-5a71-99da-2ec914b9fd73" "question-8512c354-4d05-539a-b5eb-ac69014916bb" "question-14bd2170-88ec-5846-a2ce-ea9d86c753dc" "question-82671b33-6326-52d6-9a89-56b07cb04dfe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064030 :question/problem "Yurii is flying a kite $K$ with a string of length $45\\,\\textrm{m}.$ The angle of elevation of the kite $\\theta$ decreases at a rate of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ radians per second. At what rate is the altitude of the kite is decreasing at the moment when $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}?$\n\n![](images/83/83f63ada35f2d9f64cd4b5013ccfcc86a97e48c7d19b49c403d21222a2e14d7b.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/04/04784c267488ef2073c526aed683e86ca0a00d00af64eb994ce596c0cce629d9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Yurii. The quantity $z=45$ meters does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{45} \\\\ y & =45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(45\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t} = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{15}$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(45\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =45(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})(-\\frac{\\pi }{15}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate that the kite is losing altitude is $\\dfrac{3\\pi\\sqrt3}{2} \\,\\text{m/s}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064288 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-419bbacd-9dab-5629-96fc-19c329c4d054" "question-edce8cb0-3424-5fe2-ac49-b2f607c023b1" "question-579cea6b-f87a-550f-8093-6a43bd1625a5" "question-6bda9e93-d670-52bc-bf28-dd5b8dfcba8a" "question-017bbabc-f897-558b-b361-4f91d645fa28"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064128 :question/problem "Peter is standing on the bank of a river. He observes a speed boat $B$ going downstream at a constant distance of $10\\,\\textrm{m}$ from the bank. If the boat moves at a constant speed of $8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}$ parallel to the bank, at what rate is the angle $\\theta,$ between the observer's line of sight to the boat and the bank, decreasing when $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6?$ Assume the angle of elevation is decreasing at a **positive** rate, and do not round the answer.\n\n![](images/ab/abf063d149ae14c11b9ca62208a4390b32ae191418c2e0fe77f6038479fd5625.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/113109cb44fd2d10cd3b88324ed58d2ff6455a25c81b4f62eb1862b7ced03b52.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the distance between the boat and the bank and $x$ is the horizontal distance traveled by that boat after passing Peter. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the boat is at a constant distance from the bank.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}\\theta}{\\textrm{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=8$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}6.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & ={(2)}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{5} & =4\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle $\\theta$ is decreasing is $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ radians per second." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7145361b-50f5-5e70-a538-4868d130565b" :question/id #uuid "7145361b-50f5-5e70-a538-4868d130565b" :question/math-academy-id "q-53161" :question/problem "![](images/06/0640883625711f20e1441946461abd0f352ed1418065b325f48ea2b709fef230.png)\n\nA cableway system is descending at an angle of ${45}^{∘},$ and the speed of its cabin $C$ is $10\\,\\text{mi/h}.$ The cabin casts a shadow $S$ vertically downwards to the ground. Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, find the rate at which the distance between the cableway's shadow to the landing point $L$ is decreasing." :question/worked-solution "![](images/4d/4d2a9ab6bc60aa5733be4dc29aea2b03d16edbebed371963b795297a4c79a676.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance from the cableway's shadow to the landing point, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the cabin, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\,\\text{mi/h}$ (negative because the distance between the cableway's shadow and the landing point is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-10)=-5\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal distance is decreasing at a rate of $5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5479eb5b-fe35-5bb6-87a9-6ac02c1066b1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5479eb5b-fe35-5bb6-87a9-6ac02c1066b1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5479eb5b-fe35-5bb6-87a9-6ac02c1066b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0552b87f-9e0e-5fad-9e7d-9912b470a176" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b7643b39-11ad-58a4-92de-90e602e97f9f" "answer-36356009-3cdd-5659-8ec9-cead4b18b76b" "answer-0552b87f-9e0e-5fad-9e7d-9912b470a176" "answer-105c6107-f0ae-5027-9f7f-377577430bfb" "answer-cf65a21a-c75d-54d3-b6d0-20ebce85da28"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7643b39-11ad-58a4-92de-90e602e97f9f" :answer/id #uuid "b7643b39-11ad-58a4-92de-90e602e97f9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36356009-3cdd-5659-8ec9-cead4b18b76b" :answer/id #uuid "36356009-3cdd-5659-8ec9-cead4b18b76b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0552b87f-9e0e-5fad-9e7d-9912b470a176" :answer/id #uuid "0552b87f-9e0e-5fad-9e7d-9912b470a176" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-105c6107-f0ae-5027-9f7f-377577430bfb" :answer/id #uuid "105c6107-f0ae-5027-9f7f-377577430bfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf65a21a-c75d-54d3-b6d0-20ebce85da28" :answer/id #uuid "cf65a21a-c75d-54d3-b6d0-20ebce85da28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\text{mi/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f624216d-33b0-5bec-b567-6dca0950cf8b" :question/id #uuid "f624216d-33b0-5bec-b567-6dca0950cf8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-121345" :question/problem "![](images/96/96b70c61e20b693a29a125b5f03243a75d3bfd0aeb051a2f6f74525cfdb3da78.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and flies at a constant angle of ${45}^{∘}$ to the horizontal. How fast is the drone gaining altitude if its speed is $42\\,\\text{km/h}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/40/40e0ae889d241c45464492ba3842d586effe528ab93b3554f6dbfb01d215c308.png)\n\nLet $y$ be the drone's altitude and $z$ be the actual distance between the drone and its take-off point, as shown below.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={45}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=42\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot 42=21\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7f038ecd-566c-52cf-a265-319e6fd62a6a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7f038ecd-566c-52cf-a265-319e6fd62a6a" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f038ecd-566c-52cf-a265-319e6fd62a6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ab3f5b7c-0be1-525e-a404-ab3cebcbea47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-268fe4b1-9904-5930-be01-e227a74d03fc" "answer-ab3f5b7c-0be1-525e-a404-ab3cebcbea47" "answer-f3a95e63-c6a3-5bfa-b132-f79f7862b533" "answer-7af3fffa-7393-5026-be78-1811f8567119" "answer-5854e880-7428-5210-a058-b6505ae99e7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-268fe4b1-9904-5930-be01-e227a74d03fc" :answer/id #uuid "268fe4b1-9904-5930-be01-e227a74d03fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab3f5b7c-0be1-525e-a404-ab3cebcbea47" :answer/id #uuid "ab3f5b7c-0be1-525e-a404-ab3cebcbea47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3a95e63-c6a3-5bfa-b132-f79f7862b533" :answer/id #uuid "f3a95e63-c6a3-5bfa-b132-f79f7862b533" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7af3fffa-7393-5026-be78-1811f8567119" :answer/id #uuid "7af3fffa-7393-5026-be78-1811f8567119" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5854e880-7428-5210-a058-b6505ae99e7c" :answer/id #uuid "5854e880-7428-5210-a058-b6505ae99e7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "42\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e817aceb-adb1-5216-bfe1-e48f45d3b767" :question/id #uuid "e817aceb-adb1-5216-bfe1-e48f45d3b767" :question/math-academy-id "q-53196" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d486581ea48bc96adc1269fbf40d1d71a547e92977f71e3a74336a147ef9b222.png)\n\nA potato $P$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians from the horizontal. The horizontal distance the projectile travels increases at a rate of $18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ If the angle of elevation does not change, what is the speed of the potato?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/8b/8b5af04f58fe65b46adaae47d97b857d1e8765122a811179cf25ba22733e6415.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the horizontal distance from the potato to the cannon and $z$ be the actual distance between them, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=18\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 18=12\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the potato is $12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce76d2c6-da78-53ff-8c24-134a2e050d9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce76d2c6-da78-53ff-8c24-134a2e050d9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce76d2c6-da78-53ff-8c24-134a2e050d9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2f3a66d2-ada2-57e6-bc94-a83355eb8c99" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9828920c-e8c5-59e0-8330-a343e0ffcdf0" "answer-2f3a66d2-ada2-57e6-bc94-a83355eb8c99" "answer-dc4333fa-407f-57e6-8e89-7c8e98ab48ca" "answer-023efee2-ebd9-53fc-8796-286eca083d4c" "answer-4919ab2f-fbd2-574b-9eda-ce5259c7401d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9828920c-e8c5-59e0-8330-a343e0ffcdf0" :answer/id #uuid "9828920c-e8c5-59e0-8330-a343e0ffcdf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f3a66d2-ada2-57e6-bc94-a83355eb8c99" :answer/id #uuid "2f3a66d2-ada2-57e6-bc94-a83355eb8c99" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc4333fa-407f-57e6-8e89-7c8e98ab48ca" :answer/id #uuid "dc4333fa-407f-57e6-8e89-7c8e98ab48ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-023efee2-ebd9-53fc-8796-286eca083d4c" :answer/id #uuid "023efee2-ebd9-53fc-8796-286eca083d4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4919ab2f-fbd2-574b-9eda-ce5259c7401d" :answer/id #uuid "4919ab2f-fbd2-574b-9eda-ce5259c7401d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064905 :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa0da0d113d1077b1a394f2f45fe839f8845d1bbe544e2497582df18e079c52a.png)\n\nA drone $D$ is approaching a landing point $L$ on the ground at an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to the horizontal. The drone's shadow $S$ is projected onto the ground vertically below the drone. If the drone is approaching the ground with a speed of $6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ what is the speed of the drone's shadow?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9c/9cb94ea5cab43d48da6b836026d302504d7e63fe1260aa81a2b577436b6b4e98.png)\n\nLet $x$ be the distance between the landing point and the shadow, and let $z$ be the distance between the landing point and the drone, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}$ (negative because the distance between the landing point and the drone is decreasing). Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot (-6)=-3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the shadow is $3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186063486 :answer-field/correct "answer-a65dd8fb-bbd1-58b7-ac02-a42d4c6ccf90" :answer-field/choices ["answer-329a50a1-1867-5bec-a6de-7ff85577816f" "answer-a65dd8fb-bbd1-58b7-ac02-a42d4c6ccf90" "answer-66fcb494-c0d9-5ffb-979e-79b874aa5657" "answer-2f5ba0a7-0f58-5b5c-822b-751a3fca36dc" "answer-a74ae6ab-9a62-5384-a6ec-5a1fa233a9c8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-329a50a1-1867-5bec-a6de-7ff85577816f" :answer/id #uuid "329a50a1-1867-5bec-a6de-7ff85577816f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a65dd8fb-bbd1-58b7-ac02-a42d4c6ccf90" :answer/id #uuid "a65dd8fb-bbd1-58b7-ac02-a42d4c6ccf90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66fcb494-c0d9-5ffb-979e-79b874aa5657" :answer/id #uuid "66fcb494-c0d9-5ffb-979e-79b874aa5657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f5ba0a7-0f58-5b5c-822b-751a3fca36dc" :answer/id #uuid "2f5ba0a7-0f58-5b5c-822b-751a3fca36dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a74ae6ab-9a62-5384-a6ec-5a1fa233a9c8" :answer/id #uuid "a74ae6ab-9a62-5384-a6ec-5a1fa233a9c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c70a238b-9801-5892-a5eb-17f62e1925f8" :question/id #uuid "c70a238b-9801-5892-a5eb-17f62e1925f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-121348" :question/problem "![](images/5e/5e96676aca66b99fd1e080d6c0cbbe79a5197125a3bcc8f04e67ca406947af10.png)\n\nA bullet $B$ is fired from a cannon $C$ at an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ from the horizontal. Given that the bullet's horizontal speed is $300\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$ calculate the actual speed of the bullet in the direction it is traveling." :question/worked-solution "![](images/fe/fea05a36487813579d4633d9134bdd1d8c4d4bef4e9f57ba997b7b8715d46f32.png)\n\nLet $x$ denote the horizontal distance from the bullet's projection to the cannon, and $z$ be the distance between the bullet and the cannon, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ z & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=300\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2\\cdot 300=600.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the plane is $600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f706d85-4abb-551b-ac2a-41285f5f5b00"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f706d85-4abb-551b-ac2a-41285f5f5b00" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f706d85-4abb-551b-ac2a-41285f5f5b00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f6450d8-f3a6-54ab-9544-7b06bd9a9110" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad6a3b6c-1c88-50bf-8e79-4cc6e65bfc32" "answer-e0be432a-fe44-5397-8f82-aad61839983d" "answer-9c21f96f-97a0-5e7e-b2ad-fbd0cbe2b848" "answer-8f6450d8-f3a6-54ab-9544-7b06bd9a9110" "answer-f6424a43-72c3-5b4d-aca6-aa3f6a38edb4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad6a3b6c-1c88-50bf-8e79-4cc6e65bfc32" :answer/id #uuid "ad6a3b6c-1c88-50bf-8e79-4cc6e65bfc32" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "150\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0be432a-fe44-5397-8f82-aad61839983d" :answer/id #uuid "e0be432a-fe44-5397-8f82-aad61839983d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c21f96f-97a0-5e7e-b2ad-fbd0cbe2b848" :answer/id #uuid "9c21f96f-97a0-5e7e-b2ad-fbd0cbe2b848" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "300\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f6450d8-f3a6-54ab-9544-7b06bd9a9110" :answer/id #uuid "8f6450d8-f3a6-54ab-9544-7b06bd9a9110" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "600\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6424a43-72c3-5b4d-aca6-aa3f6a38edb4" :answer/id #uuid "f6424a43-72c3-5b4d-aca6-aa3f6a38edb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "750\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e9479f5b-c4df-5efe-97d6-27688a7060c1" :question/id #uuid "e9479f5b-c4df-5efe-97d6-27688a7060c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-83678" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a37ae024b0472dfc38ecff01b898d8d3ed2384d90acf432d747efee1568520e.png)\n\nA car $C$ drives toward a $108\\,\\text{m}$ high building $B$ following a straight path. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the car and the top of the building $T$ is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ radians per second. What is the speed of the car when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f2/f28ca3385d2179984c29d27a06926806e445c1dbab2cfa5f9fd6857149b632bb.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the car and the building, and $y$ is the building's height. The quantity $y=108$ meters does not change with time because the building's height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{y} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{108} \\\\ x & =108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(108\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{36}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-108{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}))\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-108{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{36} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-3\\pi (\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car is $4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c764bd62-dac9-5c30-b3ea-6e5444aa2fdd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c764bd62-dac9-5c30-b3ea-6e5444aa2fdd" :answer-field/id #uuid "c764bd62-dac9-5c30-b3ea-6e5444aa2fdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a859c172-a2a7-57b0-955b-6cfdff5733c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a505aa4e-e343-59d5-ac42-7ab4c259cfad" "answer-a859c172-a2a7-57b0-955b-6cfdff5733c0" "answer-7f3a5aba-80e0-512f-9adf-de9315810712" "answer-5aae7333-f8d9-59d1-980d-c32006c2344a" "answer-31679908-2281-5375-8e4f-ab234176aa59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a505aa4e-e343-59d5-ac42-7ab4c259cfad" :answer/id #uuid "a505aa4e-e343-59d5-ac42-7ab4c259cfad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a859c172-a2a7-57b0-955b-6cfdff5733c0" :answer/id #uuid "a859c172-a2a7-57b0-955b-6cfdff5733c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f3a5aba-80e0-512f-9adf-de9315810712" :answer/id #uuid "7f3a5aba-80e0-512f-9adf-de9315810712" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aae7333-f8d9-59d1-980d-c32006c2344a" :answer/id #uuid "5aae7333-f8d9-59d1-980d-c32006c2344a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31679908-2281-5375-8e4f-ab234176aa59" :answer/id #uuid "31679908-2281-5375-8e4f-ab234176aa59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5a9f47ed-553f-5a71-99da-2ec914b9fd73" :question/id #uuid "5a9f47ed-553f-5a71-99da-2ec914b9fd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-314884" :question/problem "![](images/bf/bf0aa0508025b800bb0707b74ffdc89b08052ad6fced401b8071d7ad741f8e0a.png)\n\nA lighthouse $L$ shines a beam that hits the surface of the sea at point $B.$ The light is mounted at a height of $10\\,\\text{m}$ above the water. As the beam is tilted downward, the angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the beam and the point $B$ is increasing at a rate of $0.12$ radians per second. What is the speed of the light spot as a function of $\\theta ?$ \n\n*Hint*: Remember that speed is always positive or zero.\n\nThe speed of the light spot is \n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/bd/bd5afbe33a3a82ec439c5807aa99d1d28639ed74126c2dd483093ca511b253c9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the height of the lighthouse above the water, and $x$ is the horizontal distance from the lighthouse base to the light spot on the water. The quantity $y=10$ meters does not change with time because the light’s height is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{10}{x} \\\\ x & =\\frac{10}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta } \\\\ x & =10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.12.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(0.12) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the light spot toward the lighthouse is $1.2{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c8015e3-4339-5833-a2d3-370dba776e3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c8015e3-4339-5833-a2d3-370dba776e3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c8015e3-4339-5833-a2d3-370dba776e3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cc2bd856-1319-532a-a82f-52ad02474213" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc2bd856-1319-532a-a82f-52ad02474213"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc2bd856-1319-532a-a82f-52ad02474213" :answer/id #uuid "cc2bd856-1319-532a-a82f-52ad02474213" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.2\\csc^{2}(\\theta)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8512c354-4d05-539a-b5eb-ac69014916bb" :question/id #uuid "8512c354-4d05-539a-b5eb-ac69014916bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-83679" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d2e2666baf2931dc4e0d8ba097a364b7b7a1bba75e65c8d802a60180e9a94854.png)\n\nNeah is flying a kite $K$ with a string of length $112\\,\\text{ft}.$ The angle of elevation $\\theta $ of the kite is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{28}$ radians per second. Find, as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the kite's altitude is increasing." :question/worked-solution "![](images/8c/8c3c9e40f56d0b269562c37fd2d87c7e8f0394c8f55dca596e5e751f5e96157b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the kite, and $z$ is the distance between the kite and Neah. The quantity $z=112\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the kite string's length is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{112} \\\\ y & =112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(112\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{28}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(112\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\pi }{28} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the kite is gaining altitude is $4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b235a92b-fff1-5573-96d9-db640270afae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b235a92b-fff1-5573-96d9-db640270afae" :answer-field/id #uuid "b235a92b-fff1-5573-96d9-db640270afae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0655097e-96d7-5165-8639-97ef17c063f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e53e56f8-da78-5344-b9a6-bc6a67f4c209" "answer-5bcdd1a8-0496-5b15-ae0c-0eae44361812" "answer-0655097e-96d7-5165-8639-97ef17c063f1" "answer-8be20d7e-4faa-5503-9ffc-8df5111f4c28" "answer-d14ce059-5af9-5b4b-a55f-442b30db578a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e53e56f8-da78-5344-b9a6-bc6a67f4c209" :answer/id #uuid "e53e56f8-da78-5344-b9a6-bc6a67f4c209" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bcdd1a8-0496-5b15-ae0c-0eae44361812" :answer/id #uuid "5bcdd1a8-0496-5b15-ae0c-0eae44361812" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0655097e-96d7-5165-8639-97ef17c063f1" :answer/id #uuid "0655097e-96d7-5165-8639-97ef17c063f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8be20d7e-4faa-5503-9ffc-8df5111f4c28" :answer/id #uuid "8be20d7e-4faa-5503-9ffc-8df5111f4c28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d14ce059-5af9-5b4b-a55f-442b30db578a" :answer/id #uuid "d14ce059-5af9-5b4b-a55f-442b30db578a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{ft/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-14bd2170-88ec-5846-a2ce-ea9d86c753dc" :question/id #uuid "14bd2170-88ec-5846-a2ce-ea9d86c753dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86335" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce513880934a99b289c22f734e8a455c3534bddd911968cd06b7fe88f65441aa.png)\n\nA hot air balloon $B$ is flying horizontally to the observer $O$ at a constant height of $300\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the balloon and an observer is increasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the balloon when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/81/813a4a253bdac3c44fe66da2728ad01bf3f1db2e0612093ce8f6a81b60e50161.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the altitude of the hot air balloon, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the balloon and the observer. The quantity $y=300\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the hot air balloon stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{300}{x} \\\\ x & =300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(300\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=0.05$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-300(2)^{2}(0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the hot air balloon is $60\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0708b89d-7f7a-5637-8493-1ca6f099288c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0708b89d-7f7a-5637-8493-1ca6f099288c" :answer-field/id #uuid "0708b89d-7f7a-5637-8493-1ca6f099288c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7195e49e-14d0-5d15-b093-d2bc20c75842" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9adf3a93-53a6-5212-9c75-fbcceecb6e8b" "answer-7195e49e-14d0-5d15-b093-d2bc20c75842" "answer-32c27343-2ffe-5ef0-95ae-6705c24c596c" "answer-be8f074c-4255-5799-bd18-aae998046419" "answer-86eef243-02da-54f6-8ff5-46912fa718e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9adf3a93-53a6-5212-9c75-fbcceecb6e8b" :answer/id #uuid "9adf3a93-53a6-5212-9c75-fbcceecb6e8b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "54\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7195e49e-14d0-5d15-b093-d2bc20c75842" :answer/id #uuid "7195e49e-14d0-5d15-b093-d2bc20c75842" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32c27343-2ffe-5ef0-95ae-6705c24c596c" :answer/id #uuid "32c27343-2ffe-5ef0-95ae-6705c24c596c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be8f074c-4255-5799-bd18-aae998046419" :answer/id #uuid "be8f074c-4255-5799-bd18-aae998046419" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "72\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86eef243-02da-54f6-8ff5-46912fa718e7" :answer/id #uuid "86eef243-02da-54f6-8ff5-46912fa718e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-82671b33-6326-52d6-9a89-56b07cb04dfe" :question/id #uuid "82671b33-6326-52d6-9a89-56b07cb04dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-343159" :question/problem "![](images/55/55e96238e8263a1744c117bad09d1b11947f70a8bf1b837c52af18cf2e244ab7.png)\n\nA park ranger $P$ stands $900\\,\\text{ft}$ away from the base of a lookout tower $T$ to track a drone $D$ that is ascending vertically. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the ranger and the drone is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ radians per second. At what rate is the drone gaining altitude at the moment when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone’s altitude is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/d3/d30d2927efb6e85c408ba113499e043f72d726267e114e853690ac788c3946b1.png)\n\n \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between the ranger and the drone's takeoff position, and $y$ is the altitude of the drone. The quantity $x=900\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between the ranger and the takeoff position is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{900} \\\\ y & =900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(900\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\pi }{60}$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{sec}^{2}⁡({45}^{∘})(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =900{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{60}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =30\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is increasing at a rate of $30\\pi \\,\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d870faa5-187d-5160-bfd1-02d58f3d6875"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d870faa5-187d-5160-bfd1-02d58f3d6875" :answer-field/id #uuid "d870faa5-187d-5160-bfd1-02d58f3d6875" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c15fca13-90c5-5a81-bbbf-80c06db848a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c15fca13-90c5-5a81-bbbf-80c06db848a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c15fca13-90c5-5a81-bbbf-80c06db848a3" :answer/id #uuid "c15fca13-90c5-5a81-bbbf-80c06db848a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-419bbacd-9dab-5629-96fc-19c329c4d054" :question/id #uuid "419bbacd-9dab-5629-96fc-19c329c4d054" :question/math-academy-id "q-2910" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a51d0d8ef1be2e7fc06aa34fe1440e77a79d8bee654703c1e931332390741c32.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ ladder leans against the side of a building, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. Given that the foot of the ladder $F$ is being pulled away from the building at the rate of $0.5\\,\\text{ft/s}$, at what rate is $\\theta $ decreasing at the moment when the top of the ladder $T$ is $12\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7b/7b923b76e90d665a722643684ce94fff50500a7be8b70aa821410a7969e4bb79.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the foot of the ladder to the wall of the building, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the ladder. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the ladder's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=12$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.5.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{26} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{12}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{26}\\cdot \\frac{13}{12}=-\\frac{1}{24}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle $\\theta $ is *decreasing* at a rate of $\\frac{1}{24}$ radians per second." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48c6c883-58d6-591a-815a-6edbc95ab9ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48c6c883-58d6-591a-815a-6edbc95ab9ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "48c6c883-58d6-591a-815a-6edbc95ab9ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05d15c54-d685-5399-aff3-46571533a8a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-88970476-3499-5b1f-b573-1a1ad887eb08" "answer-dc57e585-10be-5f7b-846b-54fad41e6666" "answer-05d15c54-d685-5399-aff3-46571533a8a6" "answer-70755312-5bed-52eb-a77d-bfe44df9ed49" "answer-615761ef-1628-5799-88a8-d90bf4101a9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-88970476-3499-5b1f-b573-1a1ad887eb08" :answer/id #uuid "88970476-3499-5b1f-b573-1a1ad887eb08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc57e585-10be-5f7b-846b-54fad41e6666" :answer/id #uuid "dc57e585-10be-5f7b-846b-54fad41e6666" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05d15c54-d685-5399-aff3-46571533a8a6" :answer/id #uuid "05d15c54-d685-5399-aff3-46571533a8a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70755312-5bed-52eb-a77d-bfe44df9ed49" :answer/id #uuid "70755312-5bed-52eb-a77d-bfe44df9ed49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-615761ef-1628-5799-88a8-d90bf4101a9e" :answer/id #uuid "615761ef-1628-5799-88a8-d90bf4101a9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-edce8cb0-3424-5fe2-ac49-b2f607c023b1" :question/id #uuid "edce8cb0-3424-5fe2-ac49-b2f607c023b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-343202" :question/problem "![](images/30/305398b239bbc784fb5e5a1ec7ef2020a19a033dcda150e84f9c4a2a72a96adf.png)\n\nA lifeguard $L$ is watching a weather balloon $W$ rise vertically from a point $S$ on the beach. The point $S$ is $10\\,\\text{m}$ away from the lifeguard. If the balloon is rising at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ formed by the lifeguard and the balloon is increasing at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b4/b4b48ffa8202e15042190b98b8fc11bb02bc06547abcfbfba87b1433e5161d9b.png)\n\n  \n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance between the lifeguard and the point on the beach directly beneath the balloon, and $y$ is the balloon's height. The quantity $x=10\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the lifeguard does not move.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ y & =10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(10\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =10{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10{(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =10\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{40}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{40}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{40}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea67d905-4e13-5fb0-9a4b-c810c57cacc6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea67d905-4e13-5fb0-9a4b-c810c57cacc6" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea67d905-4e13-5fb0-9a4b-c810c57cacc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6951626e-b13c-5bfd-b94e-4811c1c0288e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6951626e-b13c-5bfd-b94e-4811c1c0288e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6951626e-b13c-5bfd-b94e-4811c1c0288e" :answer/id #uuid "6951626e-b13c-5bfd-b94e-4811c1c0288e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{40}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-579cea6b-f87a-550f-8093-6a43bd1625a5" :question/id #uuid "579cea6b-f87a-550f-8093-6a43bd1625a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-86352" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8f4638863dbc6bdfbff00d20d2153bb73eb7779719bbc7235d730539cb92329e.png)\n\nAngela observes that a drone is flying horizontally at a constant height of $5\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. If the drone is flying in the opposite direction from Angela's location at a speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ decreases at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/80/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d828883d-a3eb-5584-b5bd-f62f8f377e78"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d828883d-a3eb-5584-b5bd-f62f8f377e78" :answer-field/id #uuid "d828883d-a3eb-5584-b5bd-f62f8f377e78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dddb86d6-5fec-56b1-9fda-ca96845e548d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-368ef356-d80b-56d6-b93f-d58e6e4b200b" "answer-dddb86d6-5fec-56b1-9fda-ca96845e548d" "answer-69614c5f-1731-5732-8a72-f2c31ba8aa90" "answer-9bbc4914-cd92-5ba6-9302-86b96db75dc2" "answer-1493380f-c708-5ae2-ad63-0ba124cacc49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-368ef356-d80b-56d6-b93f-d58e6e4b200b" :answer/id #uuid "368ef356-d80b-56d6-b93f-d58e6e4b200b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dddb86d6-5fec-56b1-9fda-ca96845e548d" :answer/id #uuid "dddb86d6-5fec-56b1-9fda-ca96845e548d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69614c5f-1731-5732-8a72-f2c31ba8aa90" :answer/id #uuid "69614c5f-1731-5732-8a72-f2c31ba8aa90" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bbc4914-cd92-5ba6-9302-86b96db75dc2" :answer/id #uuid "9bbc4914-cd92-5ba6-9302-86b96db75dc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1493380f-c708-5ae2-ad63-0ba124cacc49" :answer/id #uuid "1493380f-c708-5ae2-ad63-0ba124cacc49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6bda9e93-d670-52bc-bf28-dd5b8dfcba8a" :question/id #uuid "6bda9e93-d670-52bc-bf28-dd5b8dfcba8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-121763" :question/problem "![](images/22/2264aa2a4f3d33b04190608ab638604dbe09dfd278035b2b764aa3d85d947624.png)\n\nGreta is flying a kite $K.$ The kite is  $150\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and moving horizontally towards Greta at a speed of $10\\,\\text{in/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing." :question/worked-solution "![](images/43/430d9bccfa7d9063ef3be511b894f23227fc248eb6afde9a25dc78546dde32aa.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between Greta and the kite. The quantity $y=150$ inches does not change with time because the kite height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{150}{x} \\\\ x & =150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-10.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =(-150{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate at which the angle between the kite string and the horizontal is increasing is $\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}$ radians per second." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da00c2cd-b023-5b35-af39-4086a9fdeebd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da00c2cd-b023-5b35-af39-4086a9fdeebd" :answer-field/id #uuid "da00c2cd-b023-5b35-af39-4086a9fdeebd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b75d5654-537e-53c4-bc29-9857e0f9d0c3" "answer-b03d0bbd-2440-5230-9491-512dd5859d1e" "answer-9a2721fb-1174-5306-a632-bbe89f079355" "answer-73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" "answer-91812201-758f-5436-91d6-fd93a8c56e30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b75d5654-537e-53c4-bc29-9857e0f9d0c3" :answer/id #uuid "b75d5654-537e-53c4-bc29-9857e0f9d0c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b03d0bbd-2440-5230-9491-512dd5859d1e" :answer/id #uuid "b03d0bbd-2440-5230-9491-512dd5859d1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a2721fb-1174-5306-a632-bbe89f079355" :answer/id #uuid "9a2721fb-1174-5306-a632-bbe89f079355" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{12}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" :answer/id #uuid "73404ea7-4aaa-507d-83eb-4a840f9f6a67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{15}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91812201-758f-5436-91d6-fd93a8c56e30" :answer/id #uuid "91812201-758f-5436-91d6-fd93a8c56e30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{18}\\,\\text{rad/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-017bbabc-f897-558b-b361-4f91d645fa28" :question/id #uuid "017bbabc-f897-558b-b361-4f91d645fa28" :question/math-academy-id "q-343195" :question/problem "![](images/22/2242cf493c6307d8c6f2dac778efe6f89ca4ba6f04203940076360613c8dc9b0.png)\n\nA $13\\,\\text{ft}$ support pole leans against a vertical wall, forming an angle $\\theta $ with the ground. The base of the pole $B$ is being pulled toward the wall at a rate of $2\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ At what rate is $\\theta $ increasing at the moment when the top of the pole $T$ is $5\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e08cddcde0d709ebe455b8d8935174955798b877f054dab0de74d06035accc91.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the horizontal distance from the base of the pole to the wall, $y$ is the vertical distance between the top of the pole and the ground, and $z$ is the length of the pole. The quantity $z=13$ feet does not change with time because the pole's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}$ when $y=5$ feet. To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{13} \\\\ x & =13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(13\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-2$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(-13\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{2}{13} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{13}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRelative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{13} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{13}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{5}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\text{rad/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-af64dd03-c64b-5792-87c8-369472ee1a77"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-af64dd03-c64b-5792-87c8-369472ee1a77" :answer-field/id #uuid "af64dd03-c64b-5792-87c8-369472ee1a77" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2fa527cb-58ec-5808-8a78-746b3d613e7b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2fa527cb-58ec-5808-8a78-746b3d613e7b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fa527cb-58ec-5808-8a78-746b3d613e7b" :answer/id #uuid "2fa527cb-58ec-5808-8a78-746b3d613e7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065387 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a jet takes off and climbs with an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ radians to the horizontal. The speed of the jet is $462\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}$ and the angle of elevation does not change. How fast is the altitude of the jet increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a diagram depicting the given information.\n\n![](images/70/708ba0a80dd7c8e3e58e1456ad643338a8e630ec45aedb8955b042ddac92c329.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the jet's height from the ground (i.e., its altitude), $z$ is the jet's distance from its take-off point. The quantity that we're required to calculate is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{2}z) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 462\\,\\textrm{km/h},$ so plugging this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot 462 = 231.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the altitude is increasing at a rate of $231\\,\\textrm{km/h}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065388 :tutorial/content "Consider a $2\\,\\text{m}$ ladder that leans against a wall, forming an angle $\\theta$ with the ground. The bottom of the ladder is pushed toward the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ as shown below. How fast is the angle $\\theta$ increasing?\n\nLet's begin by drawing a figure according to the given information.\n\n![](images/16/16996176a8557dc71fe3f3a3e0d2a0c91e5b164b9eeeeeb6cf76b44952642a47.png)\n\nIn the figure,  $z$ is the length of the ladder and $x$ the distance of the bottom of the ladder from the wall. The quantity $z=2$ does not change with time because the length of the ladder is fixed.\n\nOur job is to calculate\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d \\theta}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta,$ the sides $x$ and $z$ are the adjacent and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the bottom of the ladder is pushed against the wall at a rate of $\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\text{m}/\\text{s},$ we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064455 :activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129161 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1d879b56-35ea-5e82-93d4-20d74ef5904c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1d879b56-35ea-5e82-93d4-20d74ef5904c" :question/id #uuid "1d879b56-35ea-5e82-93d4-20d74ef5904c" :question/math-academy-id "q-71580" :question/problem "Which of the following describes the level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{8}{x-2y+3z}?$" :question/worked-solution "The level surfaces of the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ \\frac{8}{x-2y+3z} & =c \\\\ 8 & =c(x-2y+3z) \\\\ cx-2cy+3cz-8 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)$ can't be zero. Therefore, we get a family of planes:\n\n$$\ncx-2cy+3cz-8=0,\\,c\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68ec3658-a0f2-5681-81c6-416ad622e049"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68ec3658-a0f2-5681-81c6-416ad622e049" :answer-field/id #uuid "68ec3658-a0f2-5681-81c6-416ad622e049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ad86ebff-441b-5209-b2d3-1c3dab69f227" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad86ebff-441b-5209-b2d3-1c3dab69f227" "answer-8d365ca5-d6b7-5a84-ba10-86854503958a" "answer-9cf199a7-82d2-5691-843f-b4613a15cdc9" "answer-12c0e790-edc4-5281-84d1-9f28d659a041" "answer-ee37a463-0aef-5b56-9825-659d53f1bd2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad86ebff-441b-5209-b2d3-1c3dab69f227" :answer/id #uuid "ad86ebff-441b-5209-b2d3-1c3dab69f227" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A family of planes: $cx-2cy+3cz-8=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d365ca5-d6b7-5a84-ba10-86854503958a" :answer/id #uuid "8d365ca5-d6b7-5a84-ba10-86854503958a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A family of planes: $cx-2cy+cz-5=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cf199a7-82d2-5691-843f-b4613a15cdc9" :answer/id #uuid "9cf199a7-82d2-5691-843f-b4613a15cdc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A family of planes: $cx-3cz-8=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12c0e790-edc4-5281-84d1-9f28d659a041" :answer/id #uuid "12c0e790-edc4-5281-84d1-9f28d659a041" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=\\frac{8}{3(x-2y)}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee37a463-0aef-5b56-9825-659d53f1bd2c" :answer/id #uuid "ee37a463-0aef-5b56-9825-659d53f1bd2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A family of surfaces given by $z=\\frac{8}{x-2y+3}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1868e5b3-0002-5b64-a83c-33270988b244" :activity/id #uuid "1868e5b3-0002-5b64-a83c-33270988b244" :activity/title "Level Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050045 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-3ec340fb-b360-5bd8-86e3-701724277846" "step-a8426626-e25b-5b05-8748-176a2ced70ca" "step-e5aa404d-92d7-5a45-a795-3e7554f12ed2" "step-e843abce-8cea-520c-9a02-ef0bc083e948" "step-fb039e19-7eb7-5d70-b683-aac31eac7d5d"] :activity/first-step "step-3ec340fb-b360-5bd8-86e3-701724277846" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-3ec340fb-b360-5bd8-86e3-701724277846" :step/id #uuid "3ec340fb-b360-5bd8-86e3-701724277846" :step/content 17592186338963 :step/next "step-a8426626-e25b-5b05-8748-176a2ced70ca" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a8426626-e25b-5b05-8748-176a2ced70ca" :step/id #uuid "a8426626-e25b-5b05-8748-176a2ced70ca" :step/content "question-1d879b56-35ea-5e82-93d4-20d74ef5904c" :step/next "step-e5aa404d-92d7-5a45-a795-3e7554f12ed2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e5aa404d-92d7-5a45-a795-3e7554f12ed2" :step/id #uuid "e5aa404d-92d7-5a45-a795-3e7554f12ed2" :step/content 17592186403482 :step/next "step-e843abce-8cea-520c-9a02-ef0bc083e948" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e843abce-8cea-520c-9a02-ef0bc083e948" :step/id #uuid "e843abce-8cea-520c-9a02-ef0bc083e948" :step/content 17592186338965 :step/next "step-fb039e19-7eb7-5d70-b683-aac31eac7d5d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fb039e19-7eb7-5d70-b683-aac31eac7d5d" :step/id #uuid "fb039e19-7eb7-5d70-b683-aac31eac7d5d" :step/content 17592186409922 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186095958 :answer-field/choices 17592186095959] [:db/retract 17592186095958 :answer-field/choices 17592186095960] [:db/retract 17592186095958 :answer-field/choices 17592186095961] [:db/retract 17592186095958 :answer-field/choices 17592186095962] [:db/retract 17592186095958 :answer-field/choices 17592186095963] [:db/retract 17592186094135 :answer-field/choices 17592186094136] [:db/retract 17592186094135 :answer-field/choices 17592186094137] [:db/retract 17592186094135 :answer-field/choices 17592186094138] [:db/retract 17592186094135 :answer-field/choices 17592186094139] [:db/retract 17592186094135 :answer-field/choices 17592186094140] {:db/id 17592186096794 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-73e1cb61-57dc-5ce3-bb9e-62d28b4019a8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096647 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-95308c1b-5af9-5c02-9df1-10249ec99ede" "question-ae6e233b-a8cc-54d7-a535-be00579ec6e5" "question-eccc4fe9-82af-5d0c-a3d1-d04e0240d4c4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2ccb8873-58fb-5db3-858e-4560c5c188b7" "question-a225df54-1e5c-59a8-9085-a55a59c0af5b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096486 :question/problem "![](images/24/2420154d44b786c6aad54212760e9a37ce67dbdce94a7043039f0e824c5ef9bc.png)\n\nIn parallelogram $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following equalities are true?\n\nI. $\\overrightarrow{CB}+\\overrightarrow{BA}=\\overrightarrow{CA}$\n\nII. $\\overrightarrow{CB}-\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\overrightarrow{AC}$\n\nIII. $\\overrightarrow{DC}=\\overrightarrow{BA}$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-73e1cb61-57dc-5ce3-bb9e-62d28b4019a8" :question/id #uuid "73e1cb61-57dc-5ce3-bb9e-62d28b4019a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-69510" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b441f7b59f59240141380711d5d380ea58cafa01f8a288f1e5c8bc7685650cf.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=b,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ in terms of $a,b$ and $c.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $P$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=\\overset{\\to }{PS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1bf9ce3f20027a3ce358f8a0a9d72107f087021ca698e677df367e05a7c3e5bb.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=b.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n \n\n![](images/6b/6b37dcafa2b2b913d7e5328e19257c4d873c5877186df24626033fec8216da05.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{PS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ} \\\\  & =a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-66571194-3cfa-5339-aeab-67de746108ae"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-66571194-3cfa-5339-aeab-67de746108ae" :answer-field/id #uuid "66571194-3cfa-5339-aeab-67de746108ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-62659260-1e32-5c5e-b88e-8601cb732e49" :answer-field/choices ["answer-62659260-1e32-5c5e-b88e-8601cb732e49" "answer-585ac67c-f323-55e2-a9f2-ac9a32ef9526" "answer-04f65fe2-47e2-5951-8c41-5a049b5e1467" "answer-37cafaee-d6a9-58e0-a53c-93c9c0fc2d7f" "answer-0286ec20-cb3e-596c-b47d-f7e1f3c57be4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62659260-1e32-5c5e-b88e-8601cb732e49" :answer/id #uuid "62659260-1e32-5c5e-b88e-8601cb732e49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=a+b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-585ac67c-f323-55e2-a9f2-ac9a32ef9526" :answer/id #uuid "585ac67c-f323-55e2-a9f2-ac9a32ef9526" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=a+c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04f65fe2-47e2-5951-8c41-5a049b5e1467" :answer/id #uuid "04f65fe2-47e2-5951-8c41-5a049b5e1467" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=a-b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-37cafaee-d6a9-58e0-a53c-93c9c0fc2d7f" :answer/id #uuid "37cafaee-d6a9-58e0-a53c-93c9c0fc2d7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=b-a" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0286ec20-cb3e-596c-b47d-f7e1f3c57be4" :answer/id #uuid "0286ec20-cb3e-596c-b47d-f7e1f3c57be4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{PQ}=c-b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097339 :question/problem "![](images/51/511855eb6fdfc8f7766017a4fb458a0351a44e33e78db94f3cc3442469f023a6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{QP}=b,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QT}$ in terms of $a,b,$ and $c.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2fafbd5822fd6edb578a2ada55a5787cc6f32b301911332115011b69cf0d664a.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QP}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/be/be4803fec4af9e0009ea0d5a5683e17141a2086812a9df20bfdf976e849cc778.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QT} & =\\overset{\\to }{QP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT} \\\\  & =b+c\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186095958 :answer-field/correct "answer-0d57bbe9-f1a1-5014-b537-538c6b437010" :answer-field/choices ["answer-22c411fb-500c-581a-8f35-9dde4a1aa540" "answer-be5b8288-5835-5d8b-b790-6c9f30dc261e" "answer-0d57bbe9-f1a1-5014-b537-538c6b437010" "answer-a19310af-dccf-5aec-850d-6cb4bc5437c2" "answer-721132c1-d56d-5142-8045-58fde14da8b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22c411fb-500c-581a-8f35-9dde4a1aa540" :answer/id #uuid "22c411fb-500c-581a-8f35-9dde4a1aa540" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a+b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be5b8288-5835-5d8b-b790-6c9f30dc261e" :answer/id #uuid "be5b8288-5835-5d8b-b790-6c9f30dc261e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=a+c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d57bbe9-f1a1-5014-b537-538c6b437010" :answer/id #uuid "0d57bbe9-f1a1-5014-b537-538c6b437010" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b+c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a19310af-dccf-5aec-850d-6cb4bc5437c2" :answer/id #uuid "a19310af-dccf-5aec-850d-6cb4bc5437c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b-c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-721132c1-d56d-5142-8045-58fde14da8b7" :answer/id #uuid "721132c1-d56d-5142-8045-58fde14da8b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=b-d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-95308c1b-5af9-5c02-9df1-10249ec99ede" :question/id #uuid "95308c1b-5af9-5c02-9df1-10249ec99ede" :question/math-academy-id "q-332559" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b65d3044591af808c6f27f54e7bfb723b053b66c335929e96c18624c94233b3.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{BA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{FA}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{FE}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{CE}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{CB}$ in terms of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{CB}=$   \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $C$ to $B$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CB}=\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}+\\overset{\\to }{FA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18b8d39937dd23f03a030727e642729259ec5ab0c6fcb438c8bf82a2b7aaf08d.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{CE}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{EF}=-c,\\,\\overset{\\to }{FA}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}=-a.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/6f/6f1c084747ad1b0de2ab28979fee8e14db9463f1d82d07ab787581789bcb6e37.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =\\overset{\\to }{CE}+\\overset{\\to }{EF}+\\overset{\\to }{FA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB} \\\\  & =d-c+b-a.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4f6fb77a-3236-5ed3-8a05-0105cce190eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4f6fb77a-3236-5ed3-8a05-0105cce190eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6fb77a-3236-5ed3-8a05-0105cce190eb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-543fc7ef-7426-502e-9e08-f734a83baccc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-543fc7ef-7426-502e-9e08-f734a83baccc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-543fc7ef-7426-502e-9e08-f734a83baccc" :answer/id #uuid "543fc7ef-7426-502e-9e08-f734a83baccc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-a+b-c+d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097027 :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b441f7b59f59240141380711d5d380ea58cafa01f8a288f1e5c8bc7685650cf.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{PS}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{SQ}=b,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{QT}$ in terms of $a,b,$ and $c.$" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $Q$ to $T$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,$ and $c.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QT}=\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{SP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/f7/f728becaa8903fc0aa17ea9d15dd7199447dd70eff5b4a3d2be5982f1eeca8a5.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{QS}=-b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{SP}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{PT}=c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/05/055bb2c63c42defaa9f624f20b8ad92dd80f3d34b0d689def859b34785318749.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{QT} & =\\overset{\\to }{QS}+\\overset{\\to }{SP}+\\overset{\\to }{PT} \\\\  & =-b+(-a)+c \\\\  & =c-a-b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186094135 :answer-field/correct "answer-87397bfe-0e30-5921-8557-cb215a45b5bd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-46c4d8ad-5f55-5ce6-83f3-6a8835fd2263" "answer-a5798b1a-1da6-5a8e-82f0-4ba4c3d308de" "answer-785556b5-a9bc-5d2d-aa4a-8e78cf699f11" "answer-945d9463-cce8-5a6e-94de-eeeeb592a448" "answer-87397bfe-0e30-5921-8557-cb215a45b5bd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46c4d8ad-5f55-5ce6-83f3-6a8835fd2263" :answer/id #uuid "46c4d8ad-5f55-5ce6-83f3-6a8835fd2263" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=-c+a+b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5798b1a-1da6-5a8e-82f0-4ba4c3d308de" :answer/id #uuid "a5798b1a-1da6-5a8e-82f0-4ba4c3d308de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=-c+a-b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-785556b5-a9bc-5d2d-aa4a-8e78cf699f11" :answer/id #uuid "785556b5-a9bc-5d2d-aa4a-8e78cf699f11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=c+a+b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-945d9463-cce8-5a6e-94de-eeeeb592a448" :answer/id #uuid "945d9463-cce8-5a6e-94de-eeeeb592a448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=c+a-b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87397bfe-0e30-5921-8557-cb215a45b5bd" :answer/id #uuid "87397bfe-0e30-5921-8557-cb215a45b5bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{QT}=c-a-b" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae6e233b-a8cc-54d7-a535-be00579ec6e5" :question/id #uuid "ae6e233b-a8cc-54d7-a535-be00579ec6e5" :question/math-academy-id "q-328092" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4c895d3f634102ff747342616f572b6a2fa8a57c86215f20b47d2187e6e7f96d.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{DA}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{AB}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{BC}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DE}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{DC}$ in terms of vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{DC}=$  \n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $D$ to $C$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DC}=\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/cd/cd94c2f43b5da5252879f8acdcc14fc2f951f9a899903482bd3947ca8ec6921e.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{DA}=a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AB}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{BC}=c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/e0/e0f53eb04be491e8b84e2c7d5d252ab2c04bdfe372a93a503788a1ee94f63675.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DC} & =\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =a+b+c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3b88b3c0-25b5-5b3c-b29d-2c7183c7d6ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3b88b3c0-25b5-5b3c-b29d-2c7183c7d6ba" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b88b3c0-25b5-5b3c-b29d-2c7183c7d6ba" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-356e33e8-c745-5aad-839c-9497e2a771c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-356e33e8-c745-5aad-839c-9497e2a771c5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-356e33e8-c745-5aad-839c-9497e2a771c5" :answer/id #uuid "356e33e8-c745-5aad-839c-9497e2a771c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a+b+c" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-eccc4fe9-82af-5d0c-a3d1-d04e0240d4c4" :question/id #uuid "eccc4fe9-82af-5d0c-a3d1-d04e0240d4c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-335834" :question/problem "![](images/83/837da5064b1f09449bf10caa6738dbe276d71e922a5a4fa96566010fb8ae320a.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above, where $\\overset{\\to }{AE}=a,$ $\\overset{\\to }{DB}=b,$ $\\overset{\\to }{CB}=c,$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}=d.$ Find $\\overset{\\to }{EC}$ in terms of  $a,b,c,$ and $d.$ To denote the corresponding vectors, use $a$, $b,$ $c,$ and $d.$\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{EC}=$   \n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We want to find a \"path\" from $E$ to $C$ consisting only of the vectors $a,b,c,$ and $d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{EC}=\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8b76bf9fc26742d8ba05c962e59986e9f7b596f0b02c8e71c4ead4b45cf542a.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{EA}=-a,\\,\\overset{\\to }{AD}=d,\\,\\overset{\\to }{DB}=b,\\,\\overset{\\to }{BC}=-c.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/fe/fe663b4bfacc4ef2078e014af664e99123cb803fd26d03a449bf9bf6198629cc.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{EC} & =\\overset{\\to }{EA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}+\\overset{\\to }{DB}+\\overset{\\to }{BC} \\\\  & =-a+d+b+(-c) \\\\  & =-a+d+b-c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-86338cc0-f301-5ecc-b080-e1a93da8083a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-86338cc0-f301-5ecc-b080-e1a93da8083a" :answer-field/id #uuid "86338cc0-f301-5ecc-b080-e1a93da8083a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-646276d7-3118-5a12-8f30-753005b5d0c6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-646276d7-3118-5a12-8f30-753005b5d0c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-646276d7-3118-5a12-8f30-753005b5d0c6" :answer/id #uuid "646276d7-3118-5a12-8f30-753005b5d0c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-a+b-c+d" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ccb8873-58fb-5db3-858e-4560c5c188b7" :question/id #uuid "2ccb8873-58fb-5db3-858e-4560c5c188b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-143003" :question/problem "![](images/3a/3abecc8d3b60b6c9796fc9c9449888c700d205aad90786fa9e4fee854cdc07e1.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{BO}?$\n\nI. $\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}-\\overset{\\to }{OD}$\n\nII. $\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC}$\n\nIII. $\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}$" :question/worked-solution "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD}-\\overset{\\to }{OD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AD})+\\overset{\\to }{DO} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DO} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BO}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OD}-\\overset{\\to }{OC} & =\\overset{\\to }{OD}+\\overset{\\to }{CO} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BO}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{OC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =\\overset{\\to }{OB} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BO}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea2b4e97-a8a3-5689-bdb9-c780153e0b1c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea2b4e97-a8a3-5689-bdb9-c780153e0b1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea2b4e97-a8a3-5689-bdb9-c780153e0b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4c8f1b84-13b6-5114-bb9e-ab24fa1f8c54" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45c1bb40-2367-52da-b024-e48e8c24cd1a" "answer-090bb1db-63a6-54df-9f65-16399002d7b4" "answer-4c8f1b84-13b6-5114-bb9e-ab24fa1f8c54" "answer-e777df4b-9aba-50b2-9b75-be3f2a7d5536" "answer-2dd743ae-6000-5ea5-bd2f-d16d353506d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45c1bb40-2367-52da-b024-e48e8c24cd1a" :answer/id #uuid "45c1bb40-2367-52da-b024-e48e8c24cd1a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-090bb1db-63a6-54df-9f65-16399002d7b4" :answer/id #uuid "090bb1db-63a6-54df-9f65-16399002d7b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c8f1b84-13b6-5114-bb9e-ab24fa1f8c54" :answer/id #uuid "4c8f1b84-13b6-5114-bb9e-ab24fa1f8c54" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e777df4b-9aba-50b2-9b75-be3f2a7d5536" :answer/id #uuid "e777df4b-9aba-50b2-9b75-be3f2a7d5536" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dd743ae-6000-5ea5-bd2f-d16d353506d3" :answer/id #uuid "2dd743ae-6000-5ea5-bd2f-d16d353506d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a225df54-1e5c-59a8-9085-a55a59c0af5b" :question/id #uuid "a225df54-1e5c-59a8-9085-a55a59c0af5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143004" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b0009b057eef7d5d4e380883f26442b2c4e5777e90c77a7fd7791506c87cbcd4.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCDE$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{BE}?$\n\nI. $\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}$\n\nII. $\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{DE}$\n\nIII. $\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE}$" :question/worked-solution "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE} & =(\\overset{\\to }{CB}+\\overset{\\to }{BA})+\\overset{\\to }{AE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{CE} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{BE}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BC}-\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD})+\\overset{\\to }{DE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD}+\\overset{\\to }{DE} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BE}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AE} & =\\overset{\\to }{BE}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e045fd4c-49c5-5aab-8afb-9ed4d45a7fd7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e045fd4c-49c5-5aab-8afb-9ed4d45a7fd7" :answer-field/id #uuid "e045fd4c-49c5-5aab-8afb-9ed4d45a7fd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be872f56-07d9-5cdd-93a0-3ef13124098b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a2189126-0c2c-57d3-b1ab-1faf368a33ed" "answer-b34ec392-fde6-5d28-8102-c76e7c6273f3" "answer-27f09596-2658-5073-a3eb-3e27627b85c0" "answer-be872f56-07d9-5cdd-93a0-3ef13124098b" "answer-c288c24a-395b-59d2-8695-f05a71c1d9fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a2189126-0c2c-57d3-b1ab-1faf368a33ed" :answer/id #uuid "a2189126-0c2c-57d3-b1ab-1faf368a33ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b34ec392-fde6-5d28-8102-c76e7c6273f3" :answer/id #uuid "b34ec392-fde6-5d28-8102-c76e7c6273f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27f09596-2658-5073-a3eb-3e27627b85c0" :answer/id #uuid "27f09596-2658-5073-a3eb-3e27627b85c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be872f56-07d9-5cdd-93a0-3ef13124098b" :answer/id #uuid "be872f56-07d9-5cdd-93a0-3ef13124098b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c288c24a-395b-59d2-8695-f05a71c1d9fc" :answer/id #uuid "c288c24a-395b-59d2-8695-f05a71c1d9fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096882 :activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186308314 :answer-field/choices 17592186307240] [:db/retract 17592186308096 :answer-field/choices 17592186306459] [:db/retract 17592186307573 :answer-field/choices 17592186306123] [:db/retract 17592186308271 :answer-field/choices 17592186307123] {:db/id 17592186166942 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ef6edd26-9db3-5670-acfc-376735700ecd" "question-78d32ec9-eea0-570e-81c9-743259524853"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef6edd26-9db3-5670-acfc-376735700ecd" :question/id #uuid "ef6edd26-9db3-5670-acfc-376735700ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-334268" :question/problem "![](images/97/97291bd2f6b0a958a6651a87076a8ea313bec31cd6004840e0d81247dc364bf8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i},$ where $a,b\\in R,$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow, notice that the given curve is $\\text{bounded}$ (and closed), representing $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{clockwise}.$\n\nBut we have that:\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a>0,$ and\n\n- ${e}^{at}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty $ when $a<0.$\n\nThus, we must have $a=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-43cb95e9-51cf-599a-bd5e-a3c29f1f9c2d" "answer-field-083b0ba0-7609-5ec6-a938-3390e2910826" "answer-field-b0ed1170-20ac-57d4-8bc2-7e1c095e6a0c" "answer-field-f17043e1-9f96-5f60-95a4-3f465e8429cb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43cb95e9-51cf-599a-bd5e-a3c29f1f9c2d" :answer-field/id #uuid "43cb95e9-51cf-599a-bd5e-a3c29f1f9c2d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-4f84dc65-c809-58fe-9248-5825204dea7b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4f84dc65-c809-58fe-9248-5825204dea7b" "answer-52d8604d-3b08-5dfc-b1df-7bfe0e66fe43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f84dc65-c809-58fe-9248-5825204dea7b" :answer/id #uuid "4f84dc65-c809-58fe-9248-5825204dea7b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "bounded" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52d8604d-3b08-5dfc-b1df-7bfe0e66fe43" :answer/id #uuid "52d8604d-3b08-5dfc-b1df-7bfe0e66fe43" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "unbounded" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-083b0ba0-7609-5ec6-a938-3390e2910826" :answer-field/id #uuid "083b0ba0-7609-5ec6-a938-3390e2910826" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-1bd91dd1-c90f-56c1-8a22-a4fb47fa5f76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2128144-a4af-5256-b36a-5181f7ed5ef4" "answer-1bd91dd1-c90f-56c1-8a22-a4fb47fa5f76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2128144-a4af-5256-b36a-5181f7ed5ef4" :answer/id #uuid "e2128144-a4af-5256-b36a-5181f7ed5ef4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a spiral" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1bd91dd1-c90f-56c1-8a22-a4fb47fa5f76" :answer/id #uuid "1bd91dd1-c90f-56c1-8a22-a4fb47fa5f76" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ellipse" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0ed1170-20ac-57d4-8bc2-7e1c095e6a0c" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ed1170-20ac-57d4-8bc2-7e1c095e6a0c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-35c949f2-67bb-5cc5-ab89-4f36613e0369" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35c949f2-67bb-5cc5-ab89-4f36613e0369" "answer-9b6e12f4-9d18-5592-b8ff-e4e7d21e2354"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35c949f2-67bb-5cc5-ab89-4f36613e0369" :answer/id #uuid "35c949f2-67bb-5cc5-ab89-4f36613e0369" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "clockwise" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b6e12f4-9d18-5592-b8ff-e4e7d21e2354" :answer/id #uuid "9b6e12f4-9d18-5592-b8ff-e4e7d21e2354" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "counterclockwise" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f17043e1-9f96-5f60-95a4-3f465e8429cb" :answer-field/id #uuid "f17043e1-9f96-5f60-95a4-3f465e8429cb" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-ce65b786-439c-5486-ad9e-d2dd2ab1cd2b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce65b786-439c-5486-ad9e-d2dd2ab1cd2b" "answer-c5af3527-a165-5d7d-83fe-e90f66fd95dc" "answer-debc4a56-1da5-5922-a8fd-52a2d630e6b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce65b786-439c-5486-ad9e-d2dd2ab1cd2b" :answer/id #uuid "ce65b786-439c-5486-ad9e-d2dd2ab1cd2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c5af3527-a165-5d7d-83fe-e90f66fd95dc" :answer/id #uuid "c5af3527-a165-5d7d-83fe-e90f66fd95dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a\\ne 0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-debc4a56-1da5-5922-a8fd-52a2d630e6b4" :answer/id #uuid "debc4a56-1da5-5922-a8fd-52a2d630e6b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "b=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-78d32ec9-eea0-570e-81c9-743259524853" :question/id #uuid "78d32ec9-eea0-570e-81c9-743259524853" :question/math-academy-id "q-334286" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e116b0a2ba3678cde3b321b23500f532037b0cafd3842be008ee8abc3c0ee28.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-5}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\nHence, \n{{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $v=p+q\\text{i},$ where $p,q\\in {R}^{2},$ be an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =a+b\\text{i}.$\n\nThen, a complex solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t)=v{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nUsing Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{complex}}(t) & =v{e}^{(a+b\\text{i})t} \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt))(p+q\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+\\text{i}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\text{x}(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)+{c}_{2}{e}^{at}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q),\n$$\n\nwhere the parts $(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)p-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)q)$ and $(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(bt)p+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(bt)q)$ are bounded.\n\nNow notice that the given curve is $\\text{unbounded},$ representing $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$ Thus, we must have $a\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, notice that the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}.$\n\nHence, to insure that ${e}^{at}\\to 0,$ we require $a<0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a05d8e3-5b15-538e-8600-72f7c77a4c2b" "answer-field-af031759-1c5f-5644-acc3-f8e47567aef5" "answer-field-ac708e0d-1927-567f-9585-04ae386c932f" "answer-field-f9ccf2e4-ade4-527a-9ccf-b085485fa9d7" "answer-field-9421f7fa-1c8e-53be-a123-4c39ce614322" "answer-field-e9cb65c4-4123-5279-9ed5-4cc0602834eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a05d8e3-5b15-538e-8600-72f7c77a4c2b" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a05d8e3-5b15-538e-8600-72f7c77a4c2b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-a0a30d5b-c52b-5d64-9e7a-685ee21f652e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c49d575e-35e3-5a93-a205-d45ca07df6e0" "answer-a0a30d5b-c52b-5d64-9e7a-685ee21f652e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c49d575e-35e3-5a93-a205-d45ca07df6e0" :answer/id #uuid "c49d575e-35e3-5a93-a205-d45ca07df6e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value 