/choices ["answer-ddc10df4-39dd-583d-baa4-d277ab1be472" "answer-439226bb-a6a9-5bb8-a8e7-a50c3987bc49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddc10df4-39dd-583d-baa4-d277ab1be472" :answer/id #uuid "ddc10df4-39dd-583d-baa4-d277ab1be472" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a<0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-439226bb-a6a9-5bb8-a8e7-a50c3987bc49" :answer/id #uuid "439226bb-a6a9-5bb8-a8e7-a50c3987bc49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "a>0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167242 :question/problem "![](images/79/79017dcca6805220acfca563829e1feb28e00520ca095cdb4f19f0f16223aff8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two complex eigenvalues $\\lambda =a\\pm b\\text{i}$ is shown above. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe given curve is \n{{field-1}},\nrepresenting\n{{field-2}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-3}}.\nThus, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-5}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\nHence, \n{{field-6}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308314 :answer-field/correct 17592186307512 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308096 :answer-field/correct 17592186305449 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186307573 :answer-field/correct 17592186306942 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186308271 :answer-field/correct 17592186306551 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f52aa899-b1e0-52c3-865d-5560a2dc7dfa" :activity/id #uuid "f52aa899-b1e0-52c3-865d-5560a2dc7dfa" :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Complex Eigenvalues" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049209 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-9fb9d22f-4511-506a-bc68-866d4beb9b4d" "step-3d934a50-1cd4-5216-bb35-6060f0b5e4e2" "step-a015cce1-74cb-5e4f-9268-b88d722a4a5f"] :activity/first-step "step-9fb9d22f-4511-506a-bc68-866d4beb9b4d" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9fb9d22f-4511-506a-bc68-866d4beb9b4d" :step/id #uuid "9fb9d22f-4511-506a-bc68-866d4beb9b4d" :step/content "question-ef6edd26-9db3-5670-acfc-376735700ecd" :step/next "step-3d934a50-1cd4-5216-bb35-6060f0b5e4e2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3d934a50-1cd4-5216-bb35-6060f0b5e4e2" :step/id #uuid "3d934a50-1cd4-5216-bb35-6060f0b5e4e2" :step/content "question-78d32ec9-eea0-570e-81c9-743259524853" :step/next "step-a015cce1-74cb-5e4f-9268-b88d722a4a5f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a015cce1-74cb-5e4f-9268-b88d722a4a5f" :step/id #uuid "a015cce1-74cb-5e4f-9268-b88d722a4a5f" :step/content 17592186167242 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186199018 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,y$, and $z$ be the box's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n2xz+2yz+xy=48.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=2xz+2yz+xy.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=48\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2z+y,2z+x,2x+2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}yz=\\lambda (2z+y) \\\\ xz=\\lambda (2z+x) \\\\ xy=\\lambda (2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}xyz=\\lambda x(2z+y) \\\\ xyz=\\lambda y(2z+x) \\\\ xyz=\\lambda z(2x+2y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this would give $xyz=0,$ which contradicts the constraints. Therefore, from the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(2z+y) & =y(2z+x) \\\\ 2xz+xy & =2yz+xy \\\\ xz & =yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z\\ne 0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2xz+2yz+xy & =48 \\\\ 4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ 12{z}^{2} & =48 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(4,4,2),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(4,4,2)=4\\cdot 4\\cdot 2=32\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2,5.5) & =2\\cdot 2\\cdot 5.5 \\\\  & =22 \\\\  & <f(4,4,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186399343 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be three positive numbers. We wish to maximize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xyz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=108.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=108\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}yz=2\\lambda x \\\\ xz=2\\lambda y \\\\ xy=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}xyz=2\\lambda {x}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {y}^{2} \\\\ xyz=2\\lambda {z}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(6,6,6),$ and the maximum value is \n\n$$\nf(6,6,6)=6\\cdot 6\\cdot 6=216.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the maximum (and not the minimum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(10,2,2) & =10\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =40 \\\\  & <f(6,6,6).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-4ec68829-0394-5492-8dd6-3692fcfc1d04" :activity/id #uuid "4ec68829-0394-5492-8dd6-3692fcfc1d04" :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050688 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-b28a72ae-5376-5f04-8f27-20d4b6edd06e" "step-1b2d2a8b-c653-55db-b415-039fee6fd0b6" "step-c76a4af5-1fa8-565f-8538-01592aa5b8dd" "step-214df3d6-efb3-56b8-a79a-9d19a508ecbe" "step-8ff88094-3c99-5b7f-a06a-3099afb04e32"] :activity/first-step "step-b28a72ae-5376-5f04-8f27-20d4b6edd06e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b28a72ae-5376-5f04-8f27-20d4b6edd06e" :step/id #uuid "b28a72ae-5376-5f04-8f27-20d4b6edd06e" :step/content 17592186199016 :step/next "step-1b2d2a8b-c653-55db-b415-039fee6fd0b6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-1b2d2a8b-c653-55db-b415-039fee6fd0b6" :step/id #uuid "1b2d2a8b-c653-55db-b415-039fee6fd0b6" :step/content 17592186199018 :step/next "step-c76a4af5-1fa8-565f-8538-01592aa5b8dd" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c76a4af5-1fa8-565f-8538-01592aa5b8dd" :step/id #uuid "c76a4af5-1fa8-565f-8538-01592aa5b8dd" :step/content 17592186399343 :step/next "step-214df3d6-efb3-56b8-a79a-9d19a508ecbe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-214df3d6-efb3-56b8-a79a-9d19a508ecbe" :step/id #uuid "214df3d6-efb3-56b8-a79a-9d19a508ecbe" :step/content 17592186199022 :step/next "step-8ff88094-3c99-5b7f-a06a-3099afb04e32" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8ff88094-3c99-5b7f-a06a-3099afb04e32" :step/id #uuid "8ff88094-3c99-5b7f-a06a-3099afb04e32" :step/content 17592186446817 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054807 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5d427fb2-847d-5c45-9124-9759bd9b4b84"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-5d427fb2-847d-5c45-9124-9759bd9b4b84" :question/id #uuid "5d427fb2-847d-5c45-9124-9759bd9b4b84" :question/math-academy-id "q-167067" :question/problem "What are the $xy$-coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n$A\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$- and $y$-axes.\n\n* The point is $2$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, its $x$-coordinate is $-2.$\n\n* The point is $3$ units *above* the origin, in the *positive* direction of the $y$-axis. So, its $y$-coordinate is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of our point are $(-2,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e0c4408-e28e-501a-87bf-74e351180982" "answer-field-875d3e82-b9de-5b7b-af8a-882e7608648a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e0c4408-e28e-501a-87bf-74e351180982" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e0c4408-e28e-501a-87bf-74e351180982" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-234a4add-4112-5e4c-9cd7-e931f52bb5e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-234a4add-4112-5e4c-9cd7-e931f52bb5e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-234a4add-4112-5e4c-9cd7-e931f52bb5e2" :answer/id #uuid "234a4add-4112-5e4c-9cd7-e931f52bb5e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-875d3e82-b9de-5b7b-af8a-882e7608648a" :answer-field/id #uuid "875d3e82-b9de-5b7b-af8a-882e7608648a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-36e48d3d-4733-542f-98fa-3ea3a4663971" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36e48d3d-4733-542f-98fa-3ea3a4663971"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36e48d3d-4733-542f-98fa-3ea3a4663971" :answer/id #uuid "36e48d3d-4733-542f-98fa-3ea3a4663971" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054669 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6b73135a-679b-5521-bab1-ffd76e8e9cf8"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-6b73135a-679b-5521-bab1-ffd76e8e9cf8" :question/id #uuid "6b73135a-679b-5521-bab1-ffd76e8e9cf8" :question/math-academy-id "q-167068" :question/problem "Write the equations of the vertical and horizontal lines that pass through the point $B.$\n\nThe **equation** of the vertical line is \n{{field-1}}\n\nThe **equation** of the horizontal line is \n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of $B$ are $(4,1).$\n\n* Since the vertical line should pass through $(4,1)$, the $x$-value for every point on the line is $4.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\n* Since the horizontal line should pass through $(4,1)$, the $y$-value for every point on the line is $1.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\ny=1.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2a7dc394-988b-531c-a6fb-3a938bffd1dc" "answer-field-8f600507-f040-5dba-b29a-f3b5303afbf5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2a7dc394-988b-531c-a6fb-3a938bffd1dc" :answer-field/id #uuid "2a7dc394-988b-531c-a6fb-3a938bffd1dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c453d67a-491f-52bb-bf5b-d73d3745c964" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c453d67a-491f-52bb-bf5b-d73d3745c964"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c453d67a-491f-52bb-bf5b-d73d3745c964" :answer/id #uuid "c453d67a-491f-52bb-bf5b-d73d3745c964" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f600507-f040-5dba-b29a-f3b5303afbf5" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f600507-f040-5dba-b29a-f3b5303afbf5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d1f16580-85bc-52cc-b5fd-1be71020ec6c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1f16580-85bc-52cc-b5fd-1be71020ec6c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1f16580-85bc-52cc-b5fd-1be71020ec6c" :answer/id #uuid "d1f16580-85bc-52cc-b5fd-1be71020ec6c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "y=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068720 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-53008a1b-048b-5530-aee9-c37309ee0c8c" "question-10bae273-6993-5b75-9a9e-139dcefc8633"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-53008a1b-048b-5530-aee9-c37309ee0c8c" :question/id #uuid "53008a1b-048b-5530-aee9-c37309ee0c8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-167069" :question/problem "What is the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$\n\n$|\\overset{―}{AB}|\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "From the picture, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,1),$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{―}{AB}| & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-(-2))^{2}+(1-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-356d702a-8ff9-5134-b7b6-f459b7f2fb8a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-356d702a-8ff9-5134-b7b6-f459b7f2fb8a" :answer-field/id #uuid "356d702a-8ff9-5134-b7b6-f459b7f2fb8a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6df9f240-6228-5924-a16f-fd52d2430387" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6df9f240-6228-5924-a16f-fd52d2430387"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6df9f240-6228-5924-a16f-fd52d2430387" :answer/id #uuid "6df9f240-6228-5924-a16f-fd52d2430387" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068894 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-db023775-f57a-5eec-8870-ad9e9767c00a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-db023775-f57a-5eec-8870-ad9e9767c00a" :question/id #uuid "db023775-f57a-5eec-8870-ad9e9767c00a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167070" :question/problem "What is the slope $m$ of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}?$\n\n$m\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "As we found previously, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,1),$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2740cd2d-d984-544c-8909-67c29a2952fd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2740cd2d-d984-544c-8909-67c29a2952fd" :answer-field/id #uuid "2740cd2d-d984-544c-8909-67c29a2952fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2be143ce-02c5-529c-91e7-30b4b109ab46" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2be143ce-02c5-529c-91e7-30b4b109ab46"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2be143ce-02c5-529c-91e7-30b4b109ab46" :answer/id #uuid "2be143ce-02c5-529c-91e7-30b4b109ab46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068925 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4a3e6aca-bc4b-546e-bf1b-908fe05212b5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4a3e6aca-bc4b-546e-bf1b-908fe05212b5" :question/id #uuid "4a3e6aca-bc4b-546e-bf1b-908fe05212b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167071" :question/problem "Find the $y$-intercept of $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ and express the equation of this line in slope-intercept form.\n\nThe $y$-intercept of the line is $b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n\nThe equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nAs we found previously, the slope is $m=-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+b \\\\ 1 & =-(\\frac{1}{3})(4)+b \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{4}{3}+b \\\\ 1+\\frac{4}{3} & =b \\\\ b & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-249d3757-eb22-5461-8905-b54111d72bd7" "answer-field-134b35bc-2fdc-55d1-875e-ea8fc777f10d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-249d3757-eb22-5461-8905-b54111d72bd7" :answer-field/id #uuid "249d3757-eb22-5461-8905-b54111d72bd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f31d8396-8c45-5a1c-8211-0e6b56b99240" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f31d8396-8c45-5a1c-8211-0e6b56b99240"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f31d8396-8c45-5a1c-8211-0e6b56b99240" :answer/id #uuid "f31d8396-8c45-5a1c-8211-0e6b56b99240" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-134b35bc-2fdc-55d1-875e-ea8fc777f10d" :answer-field/id #uuid "134b35bc-2fdc-55d1-875e-ea8fc777f10d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-8d240067-2b7d-5da0-8ced-35c460f862bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d240067-2b7d-5da0-8ced-35c460f862bc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d240067-2b7d-5da0-8ced-35c460f862bc" :answer/id #uuid "8d240067-2b7d-5da0-8ced-35c460f862bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186068926 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0bc94237-c7e5-59e2-b81f-4ca609b2837a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0bc94237-c7e5-59e2-b81f-4ca609b2837a" :question/id #uuid "0bc94237-c7e5-59e2-b81f-4ca609b2837a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167073" :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has equation\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & =-x+7 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=7.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(7,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fee87274-1758-561e-9348-38338dad65bb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fee87274-1758-561e-9348-38338dad65bb" :answer-field/id #uuid "fee87274-1758-561e-9348-38338dad65bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-268490bc-9e7e-5df1-9744-32be636876f5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-268490bc-9e7e-5df1-9744-32be636876f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-268490bc-9e7e-5df1-9744-32be636876f5" :answer/id #uuid "268490bc-9e7e-5df1-9744-32be636876f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087710 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3fd08c56-40e8-5db2-852c-0b5043fc5d70"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3fd08c56-40e8-5db2-852c-0b5043fc5d70" :question/id #uuid "3fd08c56-40e8-5db2-852c-0b5043fc5d70" :question/math-academy-id "q-167074" :question/problem "Write the equation of $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ in **standard form**.\n\nThe equation is \n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$y=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Our goal is to write the equation in the form \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+y & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x+3y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\nx+3y=7.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-39b1df79-bf8b-562d-b2ed-3bc481d2effb" "answer-field-b51244ef-8a40-5480-b54f-4378e874a5c5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-39b1df79-bf8b-562d-b2ed-3bc481d2effb" :answer-field/id #uuid "39b1df79-bf8b-562d-b2ed-3bc481d2effb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dd04c69d-53b7-5e6a-9054-97ee1c1c253f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd04c69d-53b7-5e6a-9054-97ee1c1c253f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd04c69d-53b7-5e6a-9054-97ee1c1c253f" :answer/id #uuid "dd04c69d-53b7-5e6a-9054-97ee1c1c253f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b51244ef-8a40-5480-b54f-4378e874a5c5" :answer-field/id #uuid "b51244ef-8a40-5480-b54f-4378e874a5c5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5ef26725-eef3-57f7-9013-c61e4007068e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ef26725-eef3-57f7-9013-c61e4007068e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ef26725-eef3-57f7-9013-c61e4007068e" :answer/id #uuid "5ef26725-eef3-57f7-9013-c61e4007068e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175910 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9a61b0f7-d3d4-5914-af7a-56cdc12fc887"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9a61b0f7-d3d4-5914-af7a-56cdc12fc887" :question/id #uuid "9a61b0f7-d3d4-5914-af7a-56cdc12fc887" :question/math-academy-id "q-167075" :question/problem "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,-2)$ and is parallel to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ that passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-2).$ \n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-3) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-44132ea1-b7c9-5842-96df-a89dcc91fc33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-44132ea1-b7c9-5842-96df-a89dcc91fc33" :answer-field/id #uuid "44132ea1-b7c9-5842-96df-a89dcc91fc33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ca9f6489-52e9-5751-9fa8-55e469b75710" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ca9f6489-52e9-5751-9fa8-55e469b75710"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca9f6489-52e9-5751-9fa8-55e469b75710" :answer/id #uuid "ca9f6489-52e9-5751-9fa8-55e469b75710" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}x-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c73684cd-cbd4-53ce-a02e-b8f120478413"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c73684cd-cbd4-53ce-a02e-b8f120478413" :question/id #uuid "c73684cd-cbd4-53ce-a02e-b8f120478413" :question/math-academy-id "q-167076" :question/problem "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,8)$ and is perpendicular to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other. Therefore, since $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{3},$ our perpendicular line must have a slope of \n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, our perpendicular line has the slope ${m}_{1}=3$ and passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,8).$ \n\nFinally, using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}_{1}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ y-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4d80dd15-54e7-5576-9c18-bbe7d5170a37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4d80dd15-54e7-5576-9c18-bbe7d5170a37" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d80dd15-54e7-5576-9c18-bbe7d5170a37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-363dfa66-499c-5d2c-a96a-6760e5b32e6f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-363dfa66-499c-5d2c-a96a-6760e5b32e6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-363dfa66-499c-5d2c-a96a-6760e5b32e6f" :answer/id #uuid "363dfa66-499c-5d2c-a96a-6760e5b32e6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x+2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-f93bccbd-7e87-5446-93d1-f17e4bdb0319" :multistep/id #uuid "f93bccbd-7e87-5446-93d1-f17e4bdb0319" :multistep/context "![](images/d4/d4bff62d0370b252f4bbbbace8503b225d71b05388f67038dff60d10a33a00ac.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above." :multistep/steps ["step-96b65a37-e583-5995-a1bf-702398f75b01" "step-71980436-9f6b-5480-98ee-52818effe5ca" "step-52217a6b-1ed6-54f2-b306-9197c33edb1f" "step-74402793-00a4-5df0-a606-8d38ba00eb3d" "step-eab2a3f1-57e3-5759-a9d3-a8cd3ff78ee3" "step-1d578ebe-cf01-5882-a3ba-912891fd208c" "step-a851ed19-5a59-5d18-994d-da92c50c5ecb" "step-418a9026-c9a0-531e-97f8-562e071db821" "step-9eabdcab-40d8-5341-9749-8c6cf187dc81"] :multistep/first-step "step-96b65a37-e583-5995-a1bf-702398f75b01" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-96b65a37-e583-5995-a1bf-702398f75b01" :step/id #uuid "96b65a37-e583-5995-a1bf-702398f75b01" :step/content "question-5d427fb2-847d-5c45-9124-9759bd9b4b84" :step/next "step-71980436-9f6b-5480-98ee-52818effe5ca" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-71980436-9f6b-5480-98ee-52818effe5ca" :step/id #uuid "71980436-9f6b-5480-98ee-52818effe5ca" :step/content "question-6b73135a-679b-5521-bab1-ffd76e8e9cf8" :step/next "step-52217a6b-1ed6-54f2-b306-9197c33edb1f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-52217a6b-1ed6-54f2-b306-9197c33edb1f" :step/id #uuid "52217a6b-1ed6-54f2-b306-9197c33edb1f" :step/content "question-53008a1b-048b-5530-aee9-c37309ee0c8c" :step/next "step-74402793-00a4-5df0-a606-8d38ba00eb3d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-74402793-00a4-5df0-a606-8d38ba00eb3d" :step/id #uuid "74402793-00a4-5df0-a606-8d38ba00eb3d" :step/content "question-db023775-f57a-5eec-8870-ad9e9767c00a" :step/next "step-eab2a3f1-57e3-5759-a9d3-a8cd3ff78ee3" :db/ensure [:step/valid